\textbf{On large datasets, boosting remains strongest.} On held-out datasets with more than 1{,}000 samples, SurvivalBoost is clearly the best method in IBS (Figure~\ref{fig:cdplotibslarge}, 86.9\% winrate). SurvICL ranks second in average IBS and first in C-index 50th quantile, ahead of TabSurv-WSA, RSF, and all other TFMs, although it wins fewer pairwise comparisons than on small datasets (Figure~\ref{fig:winplotibslarge}).

\textbf{Performance-runtime trade-off.}
SurvICL lies on the Pareto frontier across C-index and IBS for all dataset-size regimes (Appendix~\ref{sec:other_results}), and on the frontier 
for MAE in the small regime and all held-out datasets. SurvICL combines strong predictive performance with competitive runtime. 

\textbf{Calibration and time accuracy.} SurvICL is among the best-calibrated methods: its average KM-calibration rank ($6.0$) trails only the KM estimator ($4.3$), the Cox model ($5.0$), DeepSurv ($5.6$) and sksurv GBS-Cox ($5.9$) (Figure~\ref{fig:cdplotkmcal}). Both KM and the Breslow baseline hazard of the Cox model enforce calibration by construction. In MAE, SurvICL is close to Survival-IC and slightly behind SurvivalPFN, without statistically significant difference (Figure~\ref{fig:cdplotmae}).

\textbf{What makes SurvICL work.}
Figure~\ref{fig:main_results}c ablates design choices on development datasets with a shallower model trained for 10K steps, cf.\ Appendix~\ref{sec:ablations} for more details.
The prior has a substantial impact on performance: by keeping the exact same architecture and only changing the generation process, our \emph{proportional-odds prior} outperforms Cox ($77.6$) and mixed priors ($84.0$). Compared to the prior of the other TFMs, our prior yields better results than SurvPFN ($79.0$), SurvivalPFN ($82.7$), and SIC ($83.7$). This demonstrates that our prior is highly effective, while adding a censoring-time prediction head actually reduces performance ($81.0$).
Administrative censoring and time discretization have limited effects at this scale; we retain them to represent study-end
censoring and finite temporal resolution.
The final model uses $999$ quantiles for finer resolution, despite a small advantage for $99$ in the ablation
($88.5$ versus $87.2$). 

