\begin{abstract}
We introduce a class of marginal Structural Nested Mean Models (mSNMMs) that enable modeling time-varying effect modification by a subset of covariates required as adjustment variables. We focus on identifiability and estimation under conditional parallel trends assumptions, so mSNMMs are applicable in settings where time-varying Difference-in-Differences (DiD) methods are typically applied. Modeling effect modification by lower dimensional covariates reduces the risk of model misspecification and can improve interpretability relative to previously developed `full SNMMs'. mSNMMs enjoy two key advantages over alternative time-varying DiD methods. First, the parallel trends assumptions that they require are more plausible in the presence of time-varying confounding. Second, they are able to model effects of \textit{sustained} interventions even when treatment switches on and off in the data. We illustrate the methodology by applying mSNMMs to estimate how the effects of Medicaid expansion (a time-varying treatment) on local uninsurance rates vary with unemployment rate prior to expansion (a time-varying covariate), adjusting for additional time-varying demographic and economic variables.

\end{abstract}


\section{Introduction}
When the goal of a causal analysis is to learn about effect modification, there are multiple roles covariates might play. They might be effect modifiers of interest, adjustment variables to reduce bias and/or improve precision, or a combination. In this paper, we consider the scenario in which an investigator must adjust for a large set of covariates but, for reasons of interpretability or practicality, is only interested in effect modification by a subset (or other function) of those covariates. We focus in particular on settings in which the investigator is willing to make a conditional (given the set of adjustment variables) parallel trends assumption of the sort that underlies difference-in-differences (DiD) analyses. For example, suppose interest centers on how the effect of a minimum wage hike on county level unemployment rate varies with poverty rate prior to the hike. Parallel trends may only hold conditional on various demographic and economic county level characteristics in addition to poverty rate, but a researcher might nonetheless like to avoid modeling effect heterogeneity as a function of all of these additional covariates. 
Structural nested mean models (SNMMs) are models of (possibly time-varying) effect heterogeneity as a function of covariates \citep{jamie1994,jamie1997,jamie2004, snmm_review2014}. SNMMs were originally shown to be identified under a sequential exchangeability or no unobserved confounding assumption. Recently, \citet{us2022} showed that SNMMs are also identified under a conditional parallel trends assumption. However, \citet{us2022} only considered the case where the SNMM models effect modification by the full set of covariates that must be conditioned on to satisfy the parallel trends assumption. If there are many covariates, severe misspecification of this model may become likely. Further, even if correctly specified, a high dimensional SNMM may be difficult to interpret. 

Thus, marginal SNMMs (mSNMMs) that only model effect modification by a low dimensional function (usually a subset) of the covariates are desirable both to reduce bias from model misspecification and for their comparative interpretability. These models were introduced in chapter 6 of \citet{laan2003unified} in the separate context of generalizing  Robins' structural nested failure time model, which is a model for the treatment effect on the entire \textit{distribution} of an outcome, rather than just the mean. Under sequential conditional exchangeability, \citet{jamie2004} describes inverse probability of treatment weighting estimators for mSNMMs (see their Section 7.3).  We show that mSNMMs are identified under conditional parallel trends assumptions given the full set of covariates and provide doubly robust, semiparametric efficient estimators. In the point exposure setting, estimators closely related to ours were proposed in the PhD thesis of \citet{lei2011improved} under a conditional exchangeability assumption. Our class of estimators includes the inverse probability of treatment weighting estimator of \citet{abadie2005} as a special case. In the longitudinal setting, our identification and semiparametric estimation results are new and do not follow from the aforementioned references.

In concurrent work developed independently, \citet{lan2025meta} obtain results that partially overlap with our own. In the point exposure setting, they develop nonparametric estimators of the conditional treatment effect in the treated (marginal over additional adjustment covariates) under parallel trends, based on the same doubly robust conditional moment restriction as we use. However, in the longitudinal setting, their results deviate from ours. They align instead with other recent DiD methods for estimating time-varying effects \citep{roth2023,chaisemartin_review} in two important ways: they make similar parallel trends assumptions, and they consider the same limited range of `staggered adoption' treatment effects. We will argue that mSNMMs have advantages on both of these fronts.

First, a source of consternation in the time-varying DiD literature has been estimation of effects of sustained interventions when, in the observed data, treatment might switch on and off; see Section 3.4 of \citet{roth2023} and \citet{CandDH2024difference}. It is possible to identify these effects via either full SNMMs \citep{us2022} or mSNMMs under particular parallel trends assumptions appropriate for dynamic treatment regimes (see \citet{renson2023identifying} for an alternative approach). Due to model misspecification concerns regarding full SNMMs, investigators might prefer mSNMMs for this task. %If effect heterogeneity as a function of covariates is not of interest, one can even fit an `empty set' mSNMM (i.e. where the set of effect modifiers of interest is the empty set). 
Alternative DiD approaches settle for estimating the effect of first initiating treatment, marginalizing over future treatment patterns under the observational regime. 

Secondly, in contrast with the parallel trends assumptions underlying alternative time-varying DiD approaches, our parallel trends assumption \citep{us2022} might plausibly hold even in the presence of observed time-varying confounders. With the exception of \citet{renson2023identifying}, alternative methods can only plausibly adjust for time-invariant baseline covariates. The comparison between our parallel trends assumption and the `never treated' parallel trends assumption of \citet{callaway2021difference} is discussed at length in Section 8.2 of \citet{us2022} and in Remark \ref{remark_pt} below. Briefly, the issue is that their parallel trends assumptions are made with respect to a control group defined in terms of \textit{observed} future treatment status, e.g. the `never treated' \citet{callaway2021difference}, which would be influenced by future values of the confounders. Our parallel trends assumption is that at each time $t$, future expected \textit{counterfactual} untreated outcome trends are equal in units treated or untreated at $t$ (conditional on covariate and treatment history up to $t$). Because our parallel trends assumption contrasts comparable groups, it might hold even if time-varying covariates influence treatment and trends. %We discuss this point further in Remark \ref{remark_pt_comparison}, and we provide an illustrative simulation in Section \ref{section_pt_sim}. (See also \citet{us2022}, where the assumption was introduced.)


Outside of \citet{us2022} and \citet{lan2025meta}, existing time-varying DiD methods do not enable modeling of effect modification by time-varying covariates, and are therefore not directly comparable to mSNMMs when effect heterogeneity is of interest. However, we note that if an investigator is not interested in effect heterogeneity but prefers our parallel trends assumption to alternatives or wishes to estimate effects of sustained interventions, they can employ mSNMMs in which the set of effect modifiers of interest is specified to be the empty set. In staggered adoption settings, these can be used to target the same estimands as other approaches, i.e. what \citet{callaway2021difference} call `$ATT(g,t)$'.

The organization of the paper is as follows. In Section 2, we discuss mSNMMs for the point exposure setting and provide semiparametric efficient estimators. In Section 3, we provide identification results and estimators for time-varying mSNMMs. In Section 4, we discuss mSNMMs for dynamic treatment regimes in which treatment assignment might depend on evolving treatment and covariate history. We emphasize the special case of the `empty set' mSNMM for estimating effects of sustained treatment when treatment switches on and off in the observed data. In Section 5, we present simulation results. In Section 6, we apply mSNMMs to estimate heterogeneous effects of Medicaid expansion on uninsurance rates as a function of unemployment rate. In Section 7, we conclude.

\section{The Point Exposure Setting}
\subsection{Identification}

For didactic reasons, in this section we discuss identification and estimation in the point-exposure case. Suppose we observe a cohort of $N$ units indexed by $i \in \{1,\ldots,N\}$ and collect data on covariates $L_0$, pre-treatment outcome measurement $Y_0$, treatment $A$, and post-treatment outcome $Y_1$. We assume the temporal ordering $O=(L_0,Y_0,A,Y_1)$. Let $\tilde{L}_0$ denote some function (e.g. a subset) of $L_0$.  We will let $\mathbb{P}_N$ denote the empirical measure and $\mathbb{P}$ denote the true data generating law. The expectation $\mathbb{E}_P[\cdot]$ is evaluated at a law $P$; if not stated otherwise, the expectation $\mathbb{E}[\cdot]$ is evaluated at the true law $\mathbb{P}$. Finally, for some function $g(O)$, we let $\norm{g}$ denote $\{\int g(O)^2 d\mathbb{P}(O)\}^{1/2}$; we use $\norm{\cdot}_2$ to denote the Euclidean norm. 

In what follows, we are interested in inferring the conditional effect of treatment on the treated given $\tilde{L}_0$, i.e.
\begin{equation}\label{pe_msnmm}
\gamma(\tilde{l}_0)\equiv \mathbb{E}\{Y_1(1)-Y_1(0)|\tilde{L}_0=\tilde{l}_0,A=1\},
\end{equation}
where we adopt the usual counterfactual notation $Y_1(a)$ for the value of $Y_1$ had, possibly contrary to fact, treatment $A$ been set to value $a$. We will make the parallel trends assumption conditional on the full covariate $L_0$
\citep{abadie2005}.
\begin{assumption}\label{pe_pt}{(Conditional parallel trends)}
    \begin{align*}
\mathbb{E}\{Y_1(0)-Y_0|L_0,A=1\}=\mathbb{E}\{Y_1(0)-Y_0|L_0,A=0\}
\end{align*}
with probability one. 
\end{assumption}
We further make the following two assumptions that are standard in causal inference.
\begin{assumption}\label{consistency_pe}{(Consistency)}
\begin{align*}
   Y_1(a)=Y_1 \text{ whenever }A=a.
\end{align*}
\end{assumption}
\begin{assumption}\label{positivity_pe}{(Positivity)}
\begin{align*}
f_{A|L_0}(0|l_0)>0 \text{ whenever } f_{L_0}(l_0)>0.
\end{align*}
\end{assumption}
The consistency assumption states that the counterfactual outcome corresponding to the factual treatment is equal to the observed outcome. %Finally, we make the positivity assumption that 

The following theorem also follows from \citet{lan2025meta}.
\begin{theorem}\label{pe_id}
    Under Assumptions \ref{pe_pt}-\ref{positivity_pe}, $\gamma(\tilde{l}_0)$ is identified as\\ $\mathbb{E}\{Y_1-Y_0-\mathbb{E}(Y_1-Y_0|L_0,A=0)|\tilde{L}_0=\tilde{l}_0,A=1\}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
    \begin{align*}
    {\gamma}(\tilde{l}_0)&= \mathbb{E}\{Y_1(1)-Y_1(0)|\tilde{L}_0=\tilde{l}_0,A=1\}\\
    &=\mathbb{E}\{Y_1(1)-Y_0|\tilde{L}_0=\tilde{l}_0,A=1\} - \mathbb{E}\{Y_1(0)-Y_0|\tilde{L}_0=\tilde{l}_0,A=1\}\\
    &=\mathbb{E}(Y_1-Y_0|\tilde{L}_0=\tilde{l}_0,A=1) - \mathbb{E}[\mathbb{E}\{Y_1(0)-Y_0|L_0,A=1\}|\tilde{L}_0 =\tilde{l}_0,A=1]\\
    &=\mathbb{E}(Y_1-Y_0|\tilde{L}_0 =\tilde{l}_0,A=1) - \mathbb{E}[\mathbb{E}\{Y_1(0)-Y_0|L_0,A=0\}|\tilde{L}_0=\tilde{l}_0,A=1]\\
    &=\mathbb{E}(Y_1-Y_0|\tilde{L}_0 =\tilde{l}_0,A=1)-\mathbb{E}\{\mathbb{E}(Y_1-Y_0|L_0,A=0)|\tilde{L}_0=\tilde{l}_0,A=1\}.
\end{align*}
The first equality is the definition of $\gamma(\tilde{l}_0)$. The second simply adds and subtracts $\mathbb{E}(Y_0|\tilde{L}_0=\tilde{l}_0,A=1)$. The third follows from consistency and nested expectation. The fourth invokes parallel trends (\ref{pe_pt}). The final equality follows from consistency and has eliminated all counterfactuals, establishing identification.
\end{proof}


