\subsection{Semiparametric estimation}
We now turn our attention to estimation of a parametric single time point mSNMM (or just mSMM, standing for `marginal structural mean model', as there is no `nesting' in the point exposure setting). A parametric mSMM assumes that for each possible value $\tilde{l}_0$ of $\tilde{L}_0$, there exists a known function $\gamma(\tilde{l}_0;\cdot):\Psi \mapsto \mathbb{R}$, $0\in \Psi \subset \mathbb{R}^p$, satisfying $\gamma(\tilde{l}_0;0)=0$, such that 
\begin{equation}\label{smm}
\gamma(\tilde{l}_0)=\gamma(\tilde{l}_0;\psi^*)
\end{equation}
where $\psi^*\in \Psi$ is unknown. We call (\ref{smm}) a \textit{marginal} SMM because it models effect heterogeneity given $\tilde{L}_0$ marginal over other information contained in $L_0$. The identification assumptions of Theorem \ref{pe_id} can be shown not to restrict the distribution of the observed data $O$. Furthermore, under these assumptions, $\gamma(\tilde{l}_0)=\mathbb{E}\{Y_1-Y_0-\mathbb{E}(Y_1-Y_0|L_0,A=0)|\tilde{L}_0=\tilde{l}_0,A=1\}$. Thus, under (\ref{smm}), our statistical model $\mathcal{M}$ comprises the collection of distributions $P$ for which the conditional expectation function $\mathbb{E}_{P}\{Y_1-Y_0-\mathbb{E}_{P}(Y_1-Y_0|L_0,A=0)|\tilde{L}_0,A=1\}$ is equal to $\gamma(\tilde{L}_0;\psi)$ for some $\psi \in \Psi$; we assume $\gamma(\cdot;\cdot)$ is smooth in $\psi$. 

In this section, we will characterize the set of influence functions of all regular and asymptotically linear (RAL) estimators of $\psi^*$ under model $\mathcal{M}$.  This will then lead to the construction of doubly-robust machine learning (DR-ML) estimators of $\psi^*$. Let $\pi^*(L_0)\equiv \mathbb{E}(A|L_0)$ and $\mu^*(L_0)\equiv\mathbb{E}(Y_1-Y_0|L_0,A=0)$. 
\begin{theorem}\label{theorem:pe_IFs}
(i) Under the model $\mathcal{M}$, the set of influence functions for all RAL estimators of $\psi^*$ is given by
\begin{equation*}
    \left\{D(\psi^*,q)^{-1}\phi(O;\psi^*,q,\pi^*,\mu^*): q\textrm{ unrestricted} \right\}\cap \mathcal{L}_2
\end{equation*}
    where for any $\psi\in\Psi$ and any arbitrary functions $\pi(L_0)$ and $\mu(L_0)$ (such that $\pi(L_0)\neq1$ with probability one),
\begin{align*}
 D_P(\psi,q)\equiv& \frac{\partial }{\partial \psi}\mathbb{E}_P\left\{q(\tilde{L}_0)A\gamma(\tilde{L}_0;\psi)\right\}|_{\psi=\psi},\\
\phi(O;\psi,q,\pi,\mu)\equiv& q(\tilde{L}_0)\left[\frac{A-\pi(L_0)}{1-\pi(L_0)}\{Y_1-Y_0-\mu(L_0)\}-A\gamma(\tilde{L}_0;\psi)\right].
\end{align*}
Here, $q(\tilde{L}_0)$ is an arbitrary $p$-dimensional function of $\tilde{L}_0$ such that $ D(\psi^*,q)^{-1}$ exists and $\mathcal{L}_2\equiv \mathcal{L}_2(\mathbb{P})$ is the Hilbert space of real-valued mean-zero functions of $O$ with finite variance under $\mathbb{P}$. \\
(ii) Let $q_{eff}(\tilde{L}_0)$ denote the optimal index function, in the sense that under model $\mathcal{M}$, no RAL estimator of $\psi^*$ can have smaller asymptotic variance (in a positive definite sense) than one that uses $q_{eff}(\tilde{L}_0)$. Then
\[q_{eff}(\tilde{L}_0)=J(\tilde{L}_0;\psi^*)^T \textrm{var}\left[\frac{A-\pi^*(L_0)}{1-\pi^*(L_0)}\{Y_1-Y_0-\mu^*(L_0)\}-A\gamma(\tilde{L}_0;\psi^*)|\tilde{L}_0\right]^{-1}\]
where, $J(\tilde{L}_0;\psi^*)=\mathbb{E}(A|\tilde{L}_0)\frac{\partial}{\partial\psi}\gamma(\tilde{L}_0;\psi)|_{\psi=\psi^*}$. \\%Therefore the efficient influence function can be obtained as 
(iii) The estimating function $\phi(O;\psi^*,q,\pi^\dagger,\mu^\dagger)$ is doubly robust. That is, let $\pi^\dagger(L_0)$ and $\mu^\dagger(L_0)$ be arbitrary functions of $L_0$. We have, for any $p$-dimensional function $q(\tilde{L}_0)$,
\begin{equation}\label{pe_est_eq}
     \mathbb{E}\left\{\phi(O;\psi^*,q,\pi^\dagger,\mu^\dagger)\right\}=0
\end{equation}
 if either $\mu^\dagger=\mu^*$ or $\pi^\dagger=\pi^*$.
\end{theorem}
A proof of this result and all others can be found in the Appendix. Under the assumption of conditional exchangeability,  closely related estimators were derived in \citet{lei2011improved}.

We will now use Theorem \ref{theorem:pe_IFs} to motivate specific estimators for $\psi^*$. To construct estimators in practice, we need to estimate the unknown nuisance functions $\mu$ and $\pi$. We consider doubly robust machine learning (DR-ML) estimators combined with cross-fitting \citep{schick1986asymptotically,van2000asymptotic,chernozhukov2018double},  in which the nuisance functions are estimated by nonparametric estimators/machine learning algorithms. Algorithm \ref{algorithm:cfdrml} describes the implementation of the cross-fit estimator $\hat{\psi}^{cf}\equiv \hat{\psi}^{cf}(q,\hat{\pi},\hat{\mu})$ where $q(L_0)$ is again a user chosen vector function sharing the dimension of $\psi^*$. Here and elsewhere, the number of folds $S$ is assumed to be a fixed positive integer greater than or equal to 2. 

\begin{algorithm}
\caption{Implementation of a cross-fit DR-ML estimator: point-exposure setting}\label{algorithm:cfdrml}
 Randomly split the $N$ units into $S$ disjoint folds of size $n_s\approx N/S$.\\
For each $s=1,...,S$, construct estimators $\hat{\mu}^{(-s)}$ of $\mu^*$ and $\hat{\pi}^{(-s)}$ of $\pi^*$, using all the units except those in fold $s$.\\
For each $s=1,...,S$, compute $\hat{\psi}^{(s)}$ from the $s$-th fold as the (assumed unique) solution to the estimating equations $\mathbb{P}_{n_s}\{\phi(O;\psi,q,\hat{\pi}^{(-s)},\hat{\mu}^{(-s)})\}=0$ where $\mathbb{P}_{n_s}$ refers here to the empirical measure in the $s$-th fold.\\
Take the weighted average of the $S$ estimators:  $\hat{\psi}^{cf}=\sum^{S}_{s=1}\left(\frac{n_s}{N}\right)\hat{\psi}^{(s)}$.
\end{algorithm}

The following theorem establishes the asymptotic properties of $\hat{\psi}^{CF}$. 
\begin{theorem}\label{estimation_theorem}
Suppose, in addition to Assumption \ref{positivity_pe}, that the following conditions hold:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item The map $\psi \mapsto \mathbb{P}\{\phi(o;\psi,q,\pi,\mu)\}$ is differentiable at $\psi^*$ uniformly in $(\pi,\mu)$. 
    \item The matrix $D(\psi^*,q)$ is nonsingular.
    \item $\{1-\hat{\pi}^{(-s)}(L_0)\}\geq c>0$ and $\{1-\pi^*(L_0)\}\geq c>0$  with probability one for some constant $c$ and $s=1,...,S$.
    \item For a chosen $p$-dimensional function $q(\tilde{L}_0)$, the maximum absolute value of any element in $q(\tilde{L}_0)$ is upper bounded by a universal constant $C_1$ with probability one.
    \item $\mathbb{E}[\{Y_1-Y_0-\mu^*(L_0)-A\gamma(\tilde{L}_0;\psi^*)\}^2|L_0,A]$ is upper bounded by $C_2<\infty$ with probability one.
    \item $\hat{\psi}^{(s)}-\psi^*=o_{\mathbb{P}}(1)$, $\norm{\hat{\pi}^{(-s)} - \pi^*}=o_\mathbb{P}(1)$ and $\norm{\hat{\mu}^{(-s)}-\mu^*}=o_\mathbb{P}(1)$ for $s=1,...,S$.
    \item $\norm{\hat{\pi}^{(-s)} - \pi^*}\norm{\hat{\mu}^{(-s)}-\mu^*}=o_\mathbb{P}(N^{-1/2})$ for $s=1,...,S$.
    \item For any fixed ($\pi,\mu$), the class $\left\{\phi(o;\psi,q,\pi,\mu):\psi\in \Psi\right\}$ is Donsker in $\psi$. 
\end{enumerate}
Then $\hat{\psi}^{cf}$ satisfies the expansion
\begin{equation*}
\sqrt{N}(\hat{\psi}^{cf}-\psi^{*})=D(\psi^*,q)^{-1} \frac{1}{\sqrt{N}}\sum_{i=1}^N \phi(O;\psi^*,q,\pi^*,\mu^*) +o_\mathbb{P}(1)
\end{equation*}
In particular, $\sqrt{N}(\hat{\psi}^{cf}-\psi^{*})$ converges to a mean zero normally distributed variable with variance equal to $\textrm{var}\{D(\psi^*,q)^{-1}\phi(O;\psi^*,q,\pi^*,\mu^*)\}$, where  $\textrm{var}\{D(\psi^*,q)^{-1}\phi(O;\psi^*,q,\pi^*,\mu^*)\}$ is minimized (in a positive definite sense) at $q$ equal to the function $q_{eff}$ defined in Theorem \ref{theorem:pe_IFs}(ii). %$=J(\tilde{L}_0)^Tvar(\epsilon|
\end{theorem}
This theorem is a special case of the more general result for time-varying exposures, given in the following section. An asymptotically efficient estimator of $\psi^*$ can then be constructed via nonparametric estimation of $q_{eff}(\tilde{L}_0)$. A condition for asymptotic linearity for $\hat{\psi}^{cf}$ is therefore that the product of the rates of convergence of the nuisance estimators for $\mu^*(L_0)$ and $\pi^*(L_0)$ is $o_\mathbb{P}(n^{-1/2})$. This property has been referred to as `rate double robustness' \citep{smucler2019unifying}. The remaining conditions are standard regularity conditions for $Z$-estimators; see e.g. Chapter 5 of \citet{van2000asymptotic}. Here and elsewhere, we propose to estimate the variance of $\hat{\psi}^{cf}$ using a sandwich estimator: in this case, this is
\[\hat{D}(\hat{\psi}^{cf},q)^{-1}\left[\frac{1}{S}\sum^S_{s=1}\mathbb{P}_{n_s}\{\phi(O;\hat{\psi}^{cf},q,\hat{\pi}^{(-s)},\hat{\mu}^{(-s)})\phi(O;\hat{\psi}^{cf},q,\hat{\pi}^{(-s)},\hat{\mu}^{(-s)})^T\}\right]\{\hat{D}(\hat{\psi}^{cf},q)^{-1}\}^T/N\]
where 
\[\hat{D}(\hat{\psi}^{cf},q)=\frac{1}{S}\sum^{S}_{s=1} \mathbb{P}_{n_s}\left\{q(\tilde{L}_0)A\frac{\partial }{\partial \psi}\gamma(\tilde{L}_0;\psi)|_{\psi=\hat{\psi}^{cf}}\right\};\]
see Theorem 3.2 of \citep{chernozhukov2018double} for a justification.

Analysts can also choose to estimate $\psi^*$ by using parametric working models $\pi(L_0;\alpha)$ for $\pi^*(L_0)$ and $\mu(L_0;\beta)$ for $\mu^*(L_0)$, where for each possible value $\tilde{l}_0$ of $\tilde{L}_0$, 
$\alpha \mapsto \pi(l_0;\alpha)$ and $\beta  \mapsto \mu(l_0;\beta)$ are known functions that are smooth in finite-dimensional parameters $\alpha$ and $\beta$ respectively. In this case, cross-fitting is unnecessary. Estimators of $\psi^*$ constructed in this way will be consistent and asymptotically normal if either the propensity score or the outcome regression model is correctly specified. We also note that such an estimator reduces to the doubly robust estimator of the average treatment effect in the treated (ATT) proposed by \citet{sant2020doubly} in the special case that $\tilde{L}_0$ is the empty set. 


\begin{remark}\label{remark_abadie}
Suppose that the mSMM is incorrectly specified; then for every fixed $q$, our estimator will converge to a functional that is the solution of the  population estimating equation:
\[0=\mathbb{E}[q(\tilde{L}_0)\{Y_1-Y_0-\mu^*(L_0)-A\gamma(\tilde{L}_0;\psi^*)\} ].\]
In particular, if we choose $q(\tilde{L}_0)=\frac{\partial}{\partial\psi^T}\gamma(\tilde{L}_0;\psi)|_{\psi=\psi^*}$, then the solution coincides with the least squares projections of the conditional average treatment effect in the treated onto the low dimensional working model $\gamma(\tilde{L}_0;\psi^*)$. \citet{abadie2005} proposes an inverse probability weighted-estimator of this least squares projection parameter, using a nonparametric logistic series estimator of the propensity score. By taking a doubly robust approach, our estimators more easily facilitate valid inference using data-adaptive nuisance estimation (outside of series methods). Furthermore, although \citet{abadie2005} did not consider the mSMM parameter as the functional of inference, if one assumes that the mSMM is correctly specified, then our Theorem \ref{theorem:pe_IFs} shows that their estimator is inefficient. 
\end{remark}

