constitutes the vector of baseline covariates other than $Y_0$. Throughout, for an arbitrary vector $X=(X_0,\ldots,X_K)$ and $0\leq m\leq K$, we let $\bar{X}_m\equiv(X_0,\ldots,X_m)
$ and $\underline{X}%
_m\equiv(X_m,\ldots,X_K)$. Whenever $X_{-1}$ appears, we define it to take the null value with probability 1. Define $\bar{L}_m$ to be a function of $(\bar{Z}_m,\bar{Y}_{m})$, i.e. a possibly high dimensional summary of the covariate and outcome history through time $m$. We can regard $\bar L_m$ as analyst chosen. All results are stated in terms of an arbitrary $\bar L_m$, but it should be understood that assumptions might hold for some choices and not others depending on the data generating process. In particular, Remark 6 and Section 8.2 of \citet{us2022} discuss how it is in general undesirable to include outcome level histories $\bar Y_m$ directly in $\bar L_m$ (especially the immediately prior outcome $Y_m$) because it leads to assumption violations. However, increment histories $\bar\Delta_m$ where $\Delta_j\equiv Y_j-Y_{j-1}$ do not cause similar problems. Let $\tilde{\bar{L}}_m$ denote some function (e.g. a subvector) of $\bar{L}_m$ such that $\tilde{\bar{L}}_m$ is increasing in $m$, with `increasing' meaning that $\tilde{\bar{L}}_m$ is a function of $\tilde{\bar{L}}_{m+1}$ for all $m$. For example, for $\bar{L}_m=(\bar{L}_{m1},\ldots,\bar{L}_{mp}$), $\tilde{\bar{L}}_m$ might be $(\bar{L}_{m2},\bar{L}_{m5})$. We adopt the counterfactual framework for time-varying treatments
\citep{robins1986new} which posits that corresponding to each time-varying
treatment regime $\bar{a}_m$, each unit has a counterfactual or potential
outcome $Y_{m+1}(\bar{a}_{m})$ that would have been observed had that
unit received treatment regime $\bar{a}_m$. 

Now consider the so-called `blip' functions
\begin{equation}\label{blip_models}
    \gamma_{mk}(\tilde{\bar{l}}_m,\bar{a}_{m-1})\equiv \mathbb{E}\{Y_k(\bar{a}_{m-1},1,\underline{0})-Y_k(\bar{a}_{m-1},\underline{0})|\tilde{\bar{L}}_m=\tilde{\bar{l}}_m,\bar{A}_{m-1}=\bar{a}_{m-1},A_m=1\}.
\end{equation}
 with $\gamma_{mk}(\tilde{\bar{l}}_m,\bar{a}_{m-1})$ defined to be $0$ whenever $Pr(A_m=1|\tilde{\bar{L}}_m=\tilde{\bar{l}}_m,\bar{A}_{m-1}=\bar{a}_{m-1})=0$. We call (\ref{blip_models}) blip functions because they represent the conditional effect (in the treated at time $m$) of one final blip of treatment at time $m$ followed by no further treatment compared to no treatment at time $m$ or thereafter given (selected) covariate and treatment history. $\gamma_{mk}(\tilde{\bar{l}}_m,\bar{a}_{m-1})$ thus characterizes time-varying effect heterogeneity of blips of treatment. For example, (\ref{blip_models}) might represent how the lasting effects of a single year of small class size in year $m$ ($A_m=1)$ on subsequent test scores ($Y_k$, $k>m)$ depend on students' history of socioeconomic status and class size ($\tilde{\bar{L}}_m,\bar{A}_{m-1}$). Recalling that we defined $X_{-1}$ to be null, note that when $K=1$, $\gamma_{01}(\tilde{l}_0)$ corresponds to the $\gamma(\tilde{l}_0)$ from earlier sections. 

\subsection{Nonparametric Identification}
The following assumption formalizes the notion of parallel trends conditional on the full history of adjustment variables $\bar{L}_m$ \citep{us2022}:
\begin{assumption}\label{parallel_trends}{(Sequential conditional parallel trends)}
\begin{align*}
    &\mathbb{E}\{Y_k(\bar{a}_{m-1},\underline{0})-Y_{k-1}(\bar{a}_{m-1},\underline{0})|\bar{L}_m,\bar{A}_{m-1}=\bar{a}_{m-1},A_m=1\}\\&=\mathbb{E}\{Y_k(\bar{a}_{m-1},\underline{0})-Y_{k-1}(\bar{a}_{m-1},\underline{0})|\bar{L}_m,\bar{A}_{m-1}=\bar{a}_{m-1},A_m=0\}.
\end{align*}
with probability one.
\end{assumption}
In words, this
assumption states that, conditional on history of adjustment variables through
time $m$ and treatment history through time $m-1$, the expected
counterfactual outcome trends under no treatment from time $m$ onwards
do not depend on the treatment actually received at time $m$. 

\begin{remark}\label{remark_pt}
    Here, we provide a condensed summary of Section 8.2 of \citet{us2022} discussing Assumption \ref{parallel_trends} and comparing it to the `never-treated' parallel trends assumption of \citet{callaway2021difference} in the staggered adoption setting. See the original source for a fuller discussion. In our notation, `never-treated' parallel trends states 
    \begin{align}
\begin{split}\label{pt_cands}
     &\mathbb{E}\{Y_k(\bar{0})-Y_{k-1}(\bar{0})|\bar{L}_m,\bar{A}_{m-1}=\bar{0}_{m-1},A_m=1\}\\&=\mathbb{E}\{Y_k(\bar{0})-Y_{k-1}(\bar{0})|\bar{L}_m,\bar{A}_K=\bar{0}\}.
     \end{split}
\end{align}
That is, given covariate history through time $m$, the expected counterfactual untreated future outcome trends in units that started treatment at time $m$ are equal to the expected outcome trends in units that in actuality never initiate treatment for the duration of follow up. When $\bar L$ contains a common cause $\bar Z$ of treatment and trends. The problem is that the distribution of $\underline{Z}_{m+1}(\bar{0})$ in the never treated units ($\bar A_K=0$) defining the conditioning event on the right hand side of (\ref{pt_cands}) would differ from the distribution of $\underline{Z}_{m+1}(\bar{0})$ in the units defining the conditioning event of the left hand side of (\ref{pt_cands}). This is because future values of the covariate $\underline{Z}_{m+1}(\bar{0})$ are associated with future treatment $\underline{A}_{m+1}$ conditioned on only in the never treated control group. As $\bar{Z}$ is also associated with the trends, the trends will therefore also differ across the two groups, violating the assumption (\ref{pt_cands}).

A sketch of a formal argument comparing (\ref{parallel_trends}) and (\ref{pt_cands}) is as follows. First, whenever Assumption \ref{parallel_trends} holds, (\ref{pt_cands}) will fail. This is because the left hand sides of the two assumptions are identical, but their right hand sides will differ due to conditioning on future treatments in (\ref{pt_cands}). Furthermore, if Assumption \ref{parallel_trends} fails, then (\ref{pt_cands}) will only hold if two loosely related quantities (namely, the magnitude of the violation of Assumption \ref{parallel_trends} and the magnitude of an association between future treatments and counterfactual outcome trends) are exactly equal. Thus, in the presence of a time-varying confounder $\bar{Z}$, (\ref{pt_cands}) can only hold by remarkable coincidence. When all confounders are pre-baseline, however, the two assumptions are actually essentially equivalent. The above discussion also applies to other parallel trends assumption variants, such as the `not yet treated' by the time of the outcome assumption \citep{callaway2021difference}, that also condition on future treatment values.

\citet{us2022} provide a Single World Intervention Graph (SWIG) \citep{richardson2013single} illustrating conditions under which Assumption \ref{parallel_trends} would hold in the presence of a time-varying confounder $\bar{Z}$, which we reproduce in Figure \ref{fig:did_swig}. In the SWIG, $\bar L_m= (\bar Z_m,\bar\Delta_m)$ and the future counterfactual untreated trends are sequentially exchangeable with treatment, which can be read off the graph by the rules of d-separation. The graph allows for an unmeasured common cause $U_{A}$ of treatment history $\bar A_K$ and the baseline outcome $Y_0$ as well as an unmeasured common cause $U_{\bar L}$ of baseline outcome $Y_0$ and $\bar L_K$. No unmeasured common causes of $\bar A_K$ and $\bar L_K$ are permitted, which implies that $U_A$ and $U_{\bar L}$ are marginally independent. Furthermore, $Y_0$ does not cause $\bar L_K$ or $\bar A_K$. Note that $Y_0$ could not be included in the conditioning set $\bar L$, as doing so would open the collider path $A_1\leftarrow U_{A}\rightarrow Y_0 \leftarrow U_{L}\rightarrow\Delta_2(0,0)$, thereby breaking identification. For comparison, we also provide a SWIG in Figure \ref{fig:did_swig_alt} lacking time-varying confounding under which both \eqref{pt_cands} and (\ref{parallel_trends}) would hold. 
\end{remark}

