We further make sequential versions of the consistency assumption that counterfactual outcomes corresponding to observed treatments are equal to observed outcomes, and the positivity assumption. 
\begin{assumption}\label{consistency}{(Sequential consistency)}
\begin{align*}
Y_{m+1}(\bar{a}_{m}) = Y_{m+1} \text{ when }\bar{A}_m=\bar{a}_m.
\end{align*}
\end{assumption}
\begin{assumption}\label{positivity}{(Sequential positivity)}
\begin{align*}
    f_{A_m|\bar{L}_m,\bar{A}_{m-1}}(0|\bar{l}_m,\bar{a}_{m-1})>0 \text{ whenever } f_{\bar{L}_m,\bar{A}_{m-1}}(\bar{l}_m,\bar{a}_{m-1})>0.
\end{align*}
\end{assumption}
The identification derivation also makes use of the result that for increasing $\tilde{\bar{L}}_m$, without imposing any assumptions the consistency Assumption \ref{consistency}, blip functions satisfy the following important property \citep{jamie1994}:
\begin{equation}\label{blip_1994}
\mathbb{E}\left\{Y_k-\sum_{j=m}^{k-1}A_j\gamma_{jk}(\tilde{\bar{L}}_j,\bar{A}_{j-1})|\tilde{\bar{L}}_m,\bar{A}_m\right\}=\mathbb{E}\{Y_k(\bar{A}_{m-1},\underline{0})|\tilde{\bar{L}}_m,\bar{A}_m\}.
\end{equation}
For didactic purposes, in what follows we will derive nonparametric identification when $K=2$. In Theorem \ref{theorem_id_generalK} in the Appendix, we extend the result to general $K$ via induction.%In Appendix A, we extend the result to the general case via induction. 

When $K=2$, we have data $(L_0,Y_0,A_0,L_1,Y_1,A_1,Y_2)$ where $L_1$ may include $Y_0$. We can enumerate the blip functions (\ref{blip_models}) as follows:
\begin{align}
\gamma_{12}(\tilde{\bar{l}}_1,a_0)\equiv& \mathbb{E}\{Y_2(a_0,1)-Y_2(a_0,0)|\tilde{\bar{L}}_1=\tilde{\bar{l}}_1,A_0=a_0,A_1=1\}
\label{blips_2tp1}\\
\gamma_{01}(\tilde{l}_0)\equiv& \mathbb{E}\{Y_1(1)-Y_1(0)|\tilde{L}_0=\tilde{l}_0,A_0=1\}\label{blips_2tp2}\\
\gamma_{02}(\tilde{l}_0)\equiv& \mathbb{E}\{Y_2(1,0)-Y_2(0,0)|\tilde{L}_0=\tilde{l}_0,A_0=1\}.\label{blips_2tp3}
\end{align}
We can also enumerate the parallel trends assumptions: 
\begin{align}
\begin{split}\label{pt_2tp}
    \mathbb{E}\{Y_2(a_0,0)-Y_{1}|\bar{L}_1,A_0=a_0,A_1=1\}=&\mathbb{E}\{Y_2(a_0,0)-Y_{1}|\bar{L}_1,A_0=a_0,A_1=0\}\\
    \mathbb{E}\{Y_2(0,0)-Y_{1}(0)|L_0,A_0=1\}=&\mathbb{E}\{Y_2(0,0)-Y_{1}(0)|L_0,A_0=0\}\\
    \mathbb{E}\{Y_1(0)-Y_0|L_0,A_0=1\}=&\mathbb{E}\{Y_1(0)-Y_0|L_0,A_0=0\}.
    \end{split}
\end{align}
which all hold with probability one. Finally, the key instance of the property (\ref{blip_1994}) is that
\begin{align}
\mathbb{E}\{Y_2-A_1\gamma_{12}(\tilde{\bar{L}}_1,A_0)|\tilde{\bar{L}}_1,\bar{A}_1\}=\mathbb{E}\{Y_2(A_0,0)|\tilde{\bar{L}}_1,\bar{A}_1\}.
\end{align}
\begin{theorem}\label{id_2tp} \textbf{Two Time-Point Identification}. Under Assumptions \ref{parallel_trends}-\ref{positivity}, the blip functions (\ref{blips_2tp1}-\ref{blips_2tp3}) are identified as:
\begin{align*}
\gamma_{01}(\tilde{l}_0)=&\mathbb{E}(Y_1-Y_0|\tilde{L}_0 =\tilde{l}_0,A_0=1) - \mathbb{E}\{\mathbb{E}(Y_1-Y_0|L_0,A_0=0)|\tilde{L}_0=\tilde{l}_0,A_0=1\};\\
\gamma_{12}(\tilde{\bar{l}}_1,a_0)=&\mathbb{E}(Y_2-Y_1|\tilde{\bar{L}}_1=\tilde{\bar{l}}_1,A_0=a_0,A_1=1)\\& - 
    \mathbb{E}\{\mathbb{E}(Y_2-Y_1|\bar{L}_1 =\bar{l}_1,A_0=a_0,A_1=0)|\tilde{\bar{L}}_1=\tilde{\bar{l}}_1,A_0=a_0,A_1=1\};\\\gamma_{02}(\tilde{l}_{0})=&\mathbb{E}[Y_2-A_1\gamma_{12}(\tilde{\bar{L}}_{1},A_0)-\{Y_1-A_0\gamma_{01}(\tilde{l}_0)\}|\tilde{L}_0=\tilde{l}_0,A_0=1] \\
    &-\mathbb{E}[\mathbb{E}\{\mathbb{E}(Y_2-Y_1|\bar{L}_1,A_0=0,A_1=0)|L_0,A_0=0\}|\tilde{L}_0=\tilde{l}_0,A_0=1]
\end{align*}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
See Appendix A.
\end{proof}

In Appendix A, we also give a proof by induction of identification of blip functions (\ref{blip_models}) for general number of time points.  Importantly, (\ref{blip_1994}) implies that when the blip functions (\ref{blip_models}) are identified, so too is  $\mathbb{E}\{\bar{Y}_K(\bar{0})\}$, the expected outcome trajectory under no treatment. Specifically, $\mathbb{E}\{Y_k(\underbar{0})\}=\mathbb{E}\{Y_k-\sum_{j=0}^{k-1}A_j\gamma_{jk}(\tilde{\bar{L}}_j,\bar{A}_{j-1})\}$ for $k$ in $(1,\ldots,K)$. Thus, identification of the blip functions (\ref{blip_models}) enables identification of the total population level impact of observed interventions compared to if no interventions had occurred, i.e. $\mathbb{E}\{\bar{Y}_K-\bar{Y}_K(\bar{0})\}$. 

\subsection{Staggered Adoption: an Important Special Case}\label{section_staggered}
Most time-varying DiD methods have been developed for staggered adoption settings where all units start off untreated and then begin treatment at different times. In many applications, initial treatment is an absorbing state; once direct treatment is initiated, it is maintained throughout follow up. An example of an absorbing binary treatment is Medicaid expansion, which we analyze in our real data application in Section \ref{application}. DiD methods for staggered adoption then generally target the estimand 
\begin{align}\label{equation:staggered_1}
\mathbb{E}\{Y_k(\bar{a}_{m-1}=\bar{0},\underline{1})-Y_k(\bar{0})|\bar{A}_{m}=(\bar{0}_{m-1},1)\};
\end{align}
i.e. the effect of initiating and continuing treatment versus remaining off treatment \citep{callaway2021difference}. However, in certain cases, units may switch on and off of treatment over the course of follow up. In that case, existing approaches then target the effect of initiation versus remaining off treatment, marginalizing over future treatment patterns under usual care:
\begin{align}\label{equation:staggered_2}
\mathbb{E}\{Y_k(\bar{a}_{m-1}=\bar{0},a_m=1)-Y_k(\bar{0})|\bar{A}_{m}=(\bar{0}_{m-1},1)\}.    
\end{align}

The methodology described in this paper can be straightforwardly adapted for either context. If one codes exposure such that it takes the value 1 only at the time of first treatment and 0 at all other times, then, in the special case that $\tilde{\bar{L}}_m$ is the empty set, the blip functions (\ref{blip_models}) represent the effects of treatment initiation, but not necessarily continuation, in the treated. Specifically, under this `first treatment' coding, $\gamma_{mk}(\bar{a}_{m-1}=\bar{0})$ equals \eqref{equation:staggered_1} or \eqref{equation:staggered_2} (depending on whether treatment is absorbing). Of course, we do not restrict $\tilde{\bar{L}}_m$ to be the empty set and thus enable investigation of staggered adoption effects conditional on evolving covariate history. In our Medicaid application in Section \ref{application}, by estimating (\ref{blip_models}) with `first treatment' coding, we gain insight into how the effects of Medicaid expansion on uninsurance rates depend on unemployment rates prior to expansion. 

When treatment is non-absorbing, there may be still interested in inferring the effect of the sustained strategy as in \eqref{equation:staggered_1}; we discuss how this can be done using mSNMMs in Section \ref{section_general}

