\section{\uppercase{Introduction}}
\label{sec:introduction}

Neural networks (NNs) offer adaptability for controlling complex systems where nominal modeling proves intractable \cite{narendra1990identification}. Experiments with legged robots, including humanoids \cite{lippi2018prediction,wensing2024optimization,zhang2024humanoid,li2024okami} and lower-limb exoskeletons \cite{10556541,10759774,lippi2021icinco,mamou2014neural} demonstrate this capability. However, physical closed-loop deployment degrades the offline-validated performance of these ML systems. In both disturbance compensation \cite{lippi2018prediction} and gait segmentation \cite{lippi2021icinco}, this degradation required continuous online learning to recover functionality. 

These experimental results imply that deploying the NN alters the system dynamics it originally mapped: such distributional shift degrades network performance. This work formalizes this intuition to propose integration practices for NNs in control loops.

