\section{\uppercase{Illustrative Example}}
\label{sec:illustrative_example}

To validate the Action Error and the necessity of bounded continuous learning, we simulate a 1-DOF nonlinear Duffing oscillator. This system serves as a trackable model of wearable robotics (e.g., an exoskeleton joint exhibiting nonlinear ``hardening'' stiffness \cite{Schmalz2021.03.16.435713,paul2020dynamic}). It isolates the proposed taxonomy for geometric analysis without requiring novel controller designs.

The MATLAB source code for this simulation environment and all parallel ablation studies is publicly available at \url{https://github.com/VittorioFreiburg/Action-Error/}.

The discrete-time dynamics are governed by:
\begin{equation}
x_{k+1} = A x_k + B x_k^3 + u_k + w_k,
\end{equation}
where $A = 1.05$ represents the open-loop unstable linear dynamics, $B = 0.15$ introduces the unmodeled cubic stiffness, $u_k$ is the control input, and $w_k \sim \mathcal{N}(0, 0.025)$ represents stochastic environmental noise.

\textbf{Implementation Details.} The simulation and associated neural network architectures were implemented natively within the MATLAB environment, utilizing the Deep Learning Toolbox. The NN utilizes a single hidden layer (8 neurons, \text{tanh} activation), trained offline via Levenberg-Marquardt utilizing a full training batch size ($\mathcal{B} = \text{Full}$). Online adaptation operates via SGD evaluating a single sample per step ($\mathcal{B}_k = 1$) with gradient clipping bounded at $\|\nabla_\theta L\|_F \leq 0.5$. By restricting the maximum magnitude of the gradient step, this clipping acts as a safety filter to enforce the time-scale separation bound ($\lambda_{\max}(\Gamma) < 2/L_\theta$) established in Theorem 2, preventing the network from reacting aggressively to transient noise. In the context of the generalized control architecture presented in Figure \ref{fig:general_block_diagram}, the ``Plant'' block encapsulates this 1-DOF Duffing oscillator model. The physical state variable $x_k$, outputted by the plant, represents the angular displacement of the exoskeleton joint in radians, evaluated at discrete time steps $k$. To simulate mechanical joint limits and prevent numerical divergence, the plant's state update is subjected to a symmetric physical saturation limit of $\pm 5.0$ radians. While the generalized formulation in Section \ref{sec:formulation} treats the network as the unified controller, this hardware-inspired implementation retains the Proportional-Derivative (PD) baseline controller during deployment. The neural network acts as a parallel adaptive feedforward compensator, such that the total applied control effort is $u_k = u_k^{base}(x_k, x_{ref}) - g(x_k, x_{ref}, \theta)$.

The system undergoes a three-phase deployment (visually mapped in the experimental strategy of Figure \ref{fig:experiment_phases}):
\begin{enumerate}[label=\arabic*), leftmargin=*]
    \item \textbf{Calibration (Phase 1):} \looseness=-1 A baseline Proportional-Derivative (PD) controller ($K_p = 0.60, K_d = 0.25$) stabilizes the system to track a low-amplitude calibration sine wave ($x_{ref} = 0.3\sin(\pi k / 15)$) over a constrained dataset of $N = 150$ discrete samples. The plant is subjected to continuous Gaussian process noise $w_k \sim \mathcal{N}(0, 0.025^2)$. Because the state magnitude remains small, the cubic term $B x_k^3$ is negligible, leaving the nonlinearity hidden from offline identification.
\item \textbf{Deployment (Phase 2):} An offline neural network is trained on the Phase 1 residuals. The system is then tasked with a large-amplitude reference trajectory pushing the state into the unmapped nonlinear regime.
\item \textbf{Online Adaptation (Phase 3):} The network's weights are ``unfrozen'' and continuously updated online via standard Backpropagation. To re-establish Lyapunov stability, the gradients are bounded via clipping to enforce time-scale separation.
\end{enumerate}

To isolate the necessity of time-scale separation and gradient bounding, we perform parallel ablation studies on the Phase 3 online adaptation mechanism. To validate the proposed taxonomy, we calculate the three errors:

\begin{enumerate}[1),wide, labelwidth=!,labelindent=0pt]
    \item \textbf{Identification Error:} \looseness=-1 Evaluated offline, this represents the divergence between the network's prediction and the unmodeled dynamics over the calibration dataset $\mathcal{D}_{off}$. We quantify the estimation bias using an \textit{Estimated Bias Proxy} inspired by Instrumental Variable techniques, computing the ratio of the network output's Pearson correlation with the noisy baseline control to its correlation with the clean reference signal: $\text{Proxy} = |\rho(g_{\theta}, u^{base})| / (|\rho(g_{\theta}, x_{ref})| + \epsilon)$. In our evaluation, this yielded $0.041$. The variance explained by this noise correlation provides an expected lower-bound on the physical bias (the ``Ghost Variance''), calculated as $\text{Var}_{ghost} = \rho(g_{\theta}, u^{base})^2 \cdot \text{Var}(g_{\theta})$. This represents the footprint of the unmeasured noise that the neural network has internalized, confirming it mapped closed-loop artifacts rather than the deterministic plant dynamics.
   \item \textbf{Action Error (Generalization Gap).} Evaluated upon deployment, this is the difference between the network's training loss on $\mathcal{D}_{off}$ and its deployment loss on the out-of-distribution manifold $\mathcal{D}_{deploy}$. Bridging this metric with Theorem 1, we calculate the accumulation of the state sensitivity matrix ($S_k$). The Jacobians comprising $S_k$ are evaluated numerically at each time step via central finite differences ($\epsilon = 10^{-5}$) across the closed-loop plant and network parameters. While the physical trajectory in this representative execution settled into a sub-optimal offset, the underlying maximum sensitivity norm diverged ($\|S_k\|_F \approx 4.16 \times 10^{14}$). This divergence confirms unmitigated control authority. This numerical accumulation does not contradict the bounded LTI assumptions of Theorem 1, but illustrates the cascading global consequence of the Action Error. Theorem 1 guarantees the initial, local trajectory divergence. By shifting the state out-of-distribution, this bounded error acts as a catalyst, forcing the system into unmapped regimes where system-specific nonlinearities, such as the uncompensated cubic stiffness in this oscillator, take over. In these global regimes, local LTI approximations dissolve, allowing the tracking error to compound or diverge depending on the underlying physical topology of the plant.
    \item \textbf{Tracking Error:} Evaluated continuously during operation, this is the MSE between the physical closed-loop state $x_k$ and the reference limit cycle $x_{ref}$. Because this metric aggregates the entire deployment window, it captures the initial transient adaptation phase. Consequently, for the online architecture, this aggregate MSE serves as a conservative upper bound; over prolonged cyclic operation, the instantaneous tracking error asymptotically decays toward the system's stochastic noise floor.
\end{enumerate}

These metrics across the standard architectures and ablation models are summarized in Table \ref{tab:error_summary}.
Depending on stochastic noise realization, the unmitigated Action Error can cause the Offline NN to diverge (yielding undefined errors; e.g., maximum state sensitivity norm increasing to $\|S_k\| \approx 4.16 \times 10^{14}$). To allow meaningful comparison of bounded Tracking Errors, we executed a 100-iteration Monte Carlo simulation. Unmitigated Action Error caused the offline architecture to diverge in 64\% of trials. Table \ref{tab:error_summary} reports error metrics across the 36 stable executions (36\%).

As illustrated in Figure \ref{fig:ablation_results} for the Duffing oscillator and quantified in Table \ref{tab:error_summary}, the static offline network minimizes the initial Identification Error but fails to track the reference due to an Action Error. Conversely, the optimal online adaptation strategy bounds the trajectory shift and reduces the continuous Tracking Error. The ablation studies emphasize the necessity of Theorem 2: a sluggish learning rate fails to track the moving target, whereas an unstable learning rate breaches time-scale separation, inducing mechanical chatter. Divergent trials were excluded from these aggregations because the out-of-distribution trajectory divergence yields undefined or computationally infinite error metrics. This 64\% failure rate validates the Action Error: unmitigated static deployment is sub-optimal and hazardous to system stability.

