\subsection{Comparative Benchmarking Against Classical and Structural Baselines}
\label{sec:comparative_benchmarking}

To contextualize the necessity of bounded continuous learning, we benchmark our proposed architecture against two established control paradigms: classical Model Reference Adaptive Control (MRAC) \cite{ioannou1996robust} and the structural Phase 1 safeguard of Dual Input-Output Parameterization (Dual IOP) \cite{chen2023dualiop}.

\textbf{Classical Baseline (MRAC):}
MRAC avoids the Action Error by utilizing a linear-in-the-parameters (LIP) formulation governed by Lyapunov update laws, ensuring global stability. However, when deployed on the Duffing oscillator, this structural rigidity becomes its primary limitation. The LIP formulation fails to capture the spatial complexity of the unmodeled cubic stiffness ($B x_k^3$). While the MRAC remains bounded and avoids trajectory divergence, it exhibits a persistent steady-state tracking offset with an amplitude distortion of 2.49\% and a Mean Squared Error (MSE) of 0.0062 (Table \ref{tab:reviewer_baselines}). This highlights the trade-off: classical methods guarantee geometric safety at the cost of representational capacity, whereas our time-scale separated NN recovers representational capacity while imposing safety via gradient bounding.

\textbf{Structural Baseline (Dual IOP):}
To isolate the Action Error from the initial closed-loop identification bias ($E_{ID}$), we implemented a Dual IOP framework during the Phase 1 calibration. By restricting the network's hypothesis space to models that commute with the stabilizing baseline controller, Dual IOP eliminated the ``Ghost Variance'' caused by the stochastic noise. However, upon freezing this identified model for Phase 2 static deployment, the system still exhibited trajectory divergence, yielding an amplitude distortion of 48.04\% and an MSE of 0.2122.

This empirical result (Figure \ref{fig:comparative_benchmarks}) validates the thesis of this paper: even if an offline model is identified without bias, the act of static deployment itself shifts the state distribution, rendering the optimal parameters a moving target. Structural identification safeguards like Dual IOP and ICI \cite{ici2025} are effective for calibration, but they do not eliminate the Action Error. Time-scale separated online adaptation remains a necessity for safe, continuous operation.

