\section{\uppercase{Discussion and Conclusions}}
\label{sec:discussion}
This paper formalized the Action Error (\S~\ref{sec:action_error}): the performance decay caused when an offline-trained NN alters the state distribution upon closed-loop deployment. This framework integrates the Closed-Loop Identification Error with the continuous Tracking Error required during online adaptation.

\textbf{The Ubiquity of Closed-Loop Dynamics.} \looseness=-1
The Action Error emerges from closed-loop control. While initially observed in unstable systems (e.g., legged robots), it persists when loops are closed via human-exoskeleton interaction \cite{lippi2021icinco}. Deployed machine learning models alter their operational environments. In generative AI, recursive training on synthetic data causes ``Model Collapse'' \cite{shumailov2024ai} or ``Model Autophagy Disorder'' (MAD) \cite{alemohammad2024self}, demonstrating how iterative feedback acts as a lossy compression mechanism that monotonically degrades data entropy \cite{stranak2026entropic}. Mitigating this, although delayed by accumulating human data \cite{gerstgrasser2024accumulating}, requires structural guardrails to break the closed-loop feedback cycle. This cycle of data degradation mirrors philosophical critiques regarding the epistemology of artificial intelligence. Literature introduces the concept of \emph{generative algorithmic epistemic injustice}, which investigates how generative AI can undermine the integrity of collective knowledge and the processes we rely on to acquire, assess, and trust information \cite{kay2024epistemic}. Grounded in social and political philosophy, this phenomenon identifies how AI systems can produce or amplify misinformation, perpetuate representational harm, and create epistemic inequities \cite{kay2024epistemic}. Whether in the physical state space of a robotic joint or the latent space of a large language model, the mechanism remains identical: without interventions to preserve diversity or enforce exploratory persistent excitation, the agent's control authority will degrade the environment it seeks to model.

\textbf{Breaking the Loop.}
Neurology offers inspiration for breaking these loops: sensory inputs are subject to natural detection thresholds of variable entity \cite{kobel2021vestibular}. In human postural control, non-linear thresholds and intermittent control paradigms for the inverted pendulum operate on integrated vestibular and proprioceptive signals \cite{hettich2013human,hettich2015human,tigrini2022long}, modalities mimicked in robotics by IMUs and joint encoders. Replicating these thresholds via deadbands can block sensory noise from propagating through the continuous control loop \cite{mergner2010neurological,10.3389/fnhum.2021.615200}, breaking the loop around defined safe zones to suppress control authority without endangering stability \cite{icinco20}. Beyond rigid thresholds, cognitive neuroscience provides a framework for loop-breaking via Predictive Processing and Active Inference \cite{friston2017active,clark2013whatever}. In these frameworks, embodied agents minimize variational free energy (``surprise'') by altering the physical world to conform to internal hierarchical predictions \cite{lanillos2021active}. To avoid becoming trapped in feedback loops, biological systems dynamically modulate the ``precision'' (reliability) of sensory prediction errors \cite{pezzulo2015active}. In robotic control, engineering this adaptive precision-weighting acts as a neuromorphic deadband, allowing the controller to suppress transient sensorimotor errors \cite{lanillos2021active,oliver2022empirical}. This theoretical relationship is bidirectional: while active inference uses precision-weighting as a functional threshold, physical deadband models, such as the human-derived Disturbance Estimation and Compensation (DEC) framework, produce sensory reweighting as an emergent macroscopic behavior without requiring explicit gain scheduling \cite{lippi2017human}. This biologically plausible symmetry empowers the agent to balance stabilization with epistemic exploration, ensuring it does not overreact to its action-induced sensory noise \cite{lanillos2021active,oliver2022empirical,friston2017active}.

\textbf{Embodiment and the Limits of Offline Learning.}
The mathematical inevitability of the Action Error exposes a fundamental limit of offline learning: it treats the neural network as a disembodied artifact operating within an epistemic vacuum. Stabilizing a complex dynamic system mandates structural and physical embodiment, requiring continuous, situated interaction with the physical environment \cite{mergner2024embodiment}. Statically deploying a frozen model commits a fallacy of functional anthropomorphization \cite{mergner2024embodiment}; it expects robust adaptability while denying the system the continuous physical experience, or historical embodiment, necessary to co-evolve with a shifting state space \cite{mergner2024embodiment,mainzer2009embodied}. However, continuous adaptation risks recursive feedback degradation if the agent indiscriminately tracks its own self-produced errors. Safe historical co-evolution requires the agent to distinguish external physical disturbances from the trajectory shifts induced by its own parametric sub-optimality, a control-theoretic requirement that mirrors the biological somatosensory sense of self-agency (SSA) \cite{mergner2024embodiment}. By enforcing time-scale separation, the architecture achieves this balance: it maintains the continuous structural coupling required for historical embodiment \cite{mergner2024embodiment,mainzer2009embodied}, while mathematically bounding the network's authority to prevent it from recursively chasing its own sensory noise.

\textbf{Classical Adaptive Control and Robust MPC.} \looseness=-1
Architectures like Model Reference Adaptive Control (MRAC) \cite{ioannou1996robust,naranjo2025adaptive} and $\mathcal{L}_1$ Adaptive Control \cite{hovakimyan2010l1} mitigate parameter drift using robust bounds and low-pass filtering. They rely on linear-in-the-parameters (LIP) structures or continuous online tuning, which contrasts with static, non-convex offline models. While Robust Model Predictive Control (MPC) \cite{kouvaritakis2016robust} circumvents offline bias through online optimization, supervised neural approximations of these MPC laws \cite{fontes2009new} face spatial curvature artifacts that necessitate our proposed Hessian regularization and experience replay guardrails.

\textbf{Reinforcement Learning.}
These findings contextualize Reinforcement Learning (RL) \cite{sutton2018reinforcement}. While online RL circumvents identification bias through active interaction, offline RL remains vulnerable to the Action Error via out-of-distribution extrapolation \cite{fujimoto2019off,levine2020offline}. Continuous RL tracks moving targets but relies on exploratory noise, which is dangerous for safety-critical hardware like exoskeletons \cite{garcia2015comprehensive}, reintroducing the tension between Lyapunov stability and persistent excitation \cite{berkenkamp2017safe}.

\textbf{Structural Parameterizations, Certificates, and Advanced Safety Wrappers.}
Advancements in closed-loop system identification, such as Dual IOP \cite{chen2023dualiop}, Neural Identification \cite{boroujeni2025neural}, and the nonlinear generalization of Independent Component Identification (ICI) \cite{ici2025}, mitigate the initial Identification Bias by guaranteeing stabilized model extraction from closed-loop data. Mitigating the subsequent Action Error requires run-time safety. Certificate learning frameworks provide learned Lyapunov or Control Barrier Functions (CBFs) \cite{dawson2022safe}, which, like inherent Lyapunov structures \cite{min2023datadrivencontrolinherentlyapunov}, act as fast-loop spatial safety filters, while Safe Bayesian Optimization (BO) \cite{safeBO2024} provides probabilistic constraints during closed-loop tuning. Because these structural methods prioritize guaranteed bounds over spatial representation recovery, our proposed time-scale separated adaptation ($\lambda_{\max}(\Gamma) < 2/L_

