\begin{abstract}
We study a model of heterogeneous multiplicative growth with mean-field redistribution and independent local stochastic resetting.
The model describes populations or wealth distributed over sites with quenched growth-rate heterogeneity, while resets represent random local extinction or bankruptcy events.
At large redistribution or resetting rate, we find a delocalized phase and we compute the growth rate as well as the stationary distribution of the population of a given site.
We show that reset-free growth episodes generate power-law stationary tails with site-dependent exponents.
{For weak resetting and redistribution, favorable sites can undergo long reset-free episodes and localize. This produces a partially localized phase, in which a finite fraction of the total population is carried by a small set of sites whose identity changes on an $O(1)$ time scale within a subextensive pool of $N^{u^\star}$ favorable candidates, with $0<u^\star<1$.}
We derive the corresponding growth rate from a saddle point analysis, and show that the averaged stationary fractions display multifractal behavior.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\textit{Introduction.}
Populations, wealths, firm and city sizes often grow multiplicatively, with changes proportional to their current size. At the same time, migration, mutation, trade, or redistribution transfers population or wealth between different sites or agents. This combination appears in models of wealth and income dynamics, city and firm growth, ecology, evolution, and population dynamics \cite{BouchaudMezard2000,gabaix1999zipf,gabaix2009power,Kardar2020}. Multiplicative growth amplifies heterogeneity, while redistribution tends to homogenize the system. Their competition can lead either to a delocalized state, where many sites carry comparable fractions, or to localization/condensation, where a finite fraction is carried by a few favorable sites. In a mean-field version with quenched heterogeneous growth rates driven by \emph{continuous} multiplicative noise, this mechanism produces a genuinely localized phase in which a \emph{fixed} set of $O(1)$ favorable sites carries a finite fraction of the total population indefinitely \cite{BernardMFRandomG,bernard2026fluctuations,BernardBouchaudLeDoussal2026InPreparation}. As we show below, the nature of the temporal fluctuations qualitatively changes this picture.

Sudden local catastrophes (epidemics, earthquakes, bankruptcies, etc.) provide another form of temporal fluctuations.
They can be modeled by stochastic resetting: in its original formulation, a diffusing particle is returned to the origin at Poisson-distributed times, leading to a nonequilibrium stationary state \cite{EvansMajumdar2011}.
Resetting has since been extended to many stochastic processes and interacting systems, see \cite{EvansMajumdarSchehr2020,NagarGupta2023} for reviews.
In many-particle systems, resets may act globally, on the whole system at once, for instance in spin systems or Brownian particles \cite{MagoniMajumdarSchehr2020,biroli2025resetting,BasuKunduPal2019,KarthikaNagar2020}.
It can also act locally, on each particle independently, e.g. in exclusion processes, zero-range processes, and aggregation models \cite{Grange2020,MironReuveni2021,PelizzolaPrettiZamparo2021,Grange2021Aggregation,Grange2024}.

In the population-growth models we consider here, local resetting corresponds to an on-site catastrophic event: the population on one site is brought back to a small value, while the rest of the system continues to grow and exchange population.
A related additive-growth model with redistribution was studied in Ref.~\cite{Santra2022}, although the additive nature of the growth does not allow for localized phases.

\textit{Model.}
We consider $N$ sites with populations $x_i(t)\ge0$, quenched growth rates $m_i$, and mean-field redistribution at rate $\varphi$.
Between resetting events, the evolution of site $i$ is deterministic and given by\footnote{One can add a multiplicative noise term to this equation, as in Refs.~\cite{BouchaudMezard2000, BernardBouchaudLeDoussal2026InPreparation}, with a richer phenomenology; see Appendix S3-A.}
\begin{equation}
\frac{\ddd x_i}{\ddd t}
=
(m_i-\varphi)x_i+\varphi \overline{x},
\qquad
\overline{x}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{j=1}^N x_j .
\label{eq:model_deterministic_part}
\end{equation}
In addition, site $i$ is reset independently at Poissonian rate $r_i$ to a time-independent value $x_0$,\footnote{One may instead reset to a small fraction of the average population, $x_0 \to \epsilon \overline{x}$.
This slightly modifies the phase diagram, and in particular allows for $\gamma<0$, see Sec.~\ref{sec:frac} of the Supplemental Material.
}
\begin{equation}
x_i\to x_0 .
\label{eq:reset_rule}
\end{equation}
Typical trajectories are shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:resetting_trajectories}. To quantify how the total population grows, we introduce the growth rate of the system as\footnote{This may differ from the annealed growth rate defined through $\langle x(t) \rangle \sim e^{\gamma_a t}$, where $\langle \dots \rangle$ denotes the average over resetting events; see Sec.~\ref{sec:supp_annealed_gamma} of the Supplemental Material.}
\begin{equation}
\gamma=\lim_{t\to\infty}\frac{1}{t}\ln \overline{x}(t).
\label{eq:gamma_def}
\end{equation}
To measure how the population is spread among the $N$ sites, we introduce the fractions
\begin{equation}
p_i(t)=\frac{x_i(t)}{\sum_j x_j(t)},
\qquad
\sum_i p_i(t)=1.
\label{eq:p_def}
\end{equation}

