Our main interest is the system once the distribution of the $p_i$'s has reached its steady state for large but finite $N \gg 1$.

\textit{Derivation under a self-averaging assumption.}
Although $\overline{x}(t)$ fluctuates with the resetting noise, in a first stage we neglect these fluctuations and write, at large time, $\overline{x}(t)\simeq x_m\,\eee^{\gamma t}$, with $x_m$ a constant and $\gamma$ to be determined below. In that approximation the sites become effectively independent.
The fractions can then be written as $p_i(t)\simeq x_i/(N x_m\,\eee^{\gamma t})$, and from \eqref{eq:model_deterministic_part} one obtains, between resets,
\begin{equation}
\frac{\ddd p_i}{\ddd t}=a_i p_i+\frac{\varphi}{N},
\qquad
a_i=m_i-\varphi-\gamma,
\label{eq:p_linear}
\end{equation}
while at a resetting event $p_i(t)$ is set to
\begin{equation}
p_0=p_0(t,N)=\frac{x_0\,\eee^{-\gamma t}}{N x_m}.
\label{eq:p0_reset}
\end{equation}
Thus $p_0=O(N^{-1})$ when $\gamma=0$, while $p_0\to0$ exponentially in a growing phase $\gamma>0$. The deterministic solution of \eqref{eq:p_linear} at a time $\tau$ since the last reset is
\begin{equation}
p_i(\tau)=p_i^\star+(p_0-p_i^\star)\,\eee^{a_i \tau},
\qquad
p_i^\star=-\frac{\varphi}{N \,a_i}.
\label{eq:p_solution}
\end{equation}
Let $\tau(p)$ be the time needed to reach the value $p$ after the last reset. Since resetting is Poissonian, the stationary distribution of $p_i$ follows from the renewal formula \cite{EvansMajumdarSchehr2020}
\begin{equation}
P_i(p)=r_i\left|\frac{\ddd \tau}{\ddd p}\right|\eee^{-r_i\tau(p)}.
\label{eq:renewal}
\end{equation}
Inverting Eq.~\eqref{eq:p_solution} gives $\tau(p)=\frac{1}{a_i}
\ln\!\left[
\frac{N p+\varphi/a_i}{N p_0+\varphi/a_i}
\right]$, so that
\begin{align} \label{eq:distpisa}
P_i(p)
&=
|\mu_i|
\left|p_0+\frac{\varphi}{N a_i}\right|^{\mu_i}
\left|p+\frac{\varphi}{N a_i}\right|^{-1-\mu_i},
\nonumber\\
\mu_i
&=
\frac{r_i}{a_i}
=
\frac{r_i}{m_i-\varphi-\gamma},
\end{align}
with support\footnote{With $p_0=0$ for $\gamma>0$. Note that for $x_0>0$ one must have $\gamma \geq 0$.}
\begin{equation}
p
\in
\begin{cases}
\left[p_0,+\infty\right[, \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad a_i>0,\\[1mm]
\left[\min(p_0,p_i^\star),\max(p_0,p_i^\star)\right],  \quad a_i<0.
\end{cases}
\label{eq:Pi_growth}
\end{equation}
The stationary distribution displays two qualitatively distinct behaviors. If $a_i=m_i-\varphi-\gamma<0$ (and thus $\mu_i<0$), the intrinsic growth rate $m_i-\varphi$ is smaller than the global rate $\gamma$: after a reset $p_i(t)$ relaxes to $p_i^\star=\frac{\varphi}{N(\gamma+\varphi-m_i)}$, and the distribution has bounded support (diverging for $-1<\mu_i<0$, vanishing for $\mu_i<-1$ at the edge).
If $a_i>0$, the site grows faster than the total population between resets; $p_i(t)$ increases exponentially during reset-free intervals whose durations are exponentially distributed, producing the power-law tail in Eq.~\eqref{eq:distpisa} with exponent $\mu_i>0$.
For $p_i=O(1)$, the constraint $\sum_i p_i=1$ imposes an $O(1)$ cutoff whose description goes beyond the self-averaging approximation (see below), so that
\begin{equation}
P_i(p) \sim N^{-\mu_i} p^{-1-\mu_i}, \qquad N^{-1} \ll p \ll 1.
\label{eq:tail_mu}
\end{equation}
The stationary distribution depends on $\gamma$, which is fixed by the normalization $\sum_{i=1}^N \avg{p_i}=1$. If $\mu_i<0$, the distribution has compact support and $\avg{p_i}$ is set by the typical scale $p_i=O(N^{-1})$.
If $\mu_i>1$, a power-law tail is present but its average remains controlled by the same typical scale, $\langle p_i \rangle = O(1/N)$. In both cases, Eq.~\eqref{eq:distpisa} gives
\begin{equation}
\avg{p_i}
=
\frac{\varphi/N+r_i p_0}{\gamma+\varphi+r_i-m_i}.
\label{eq:mean_pi}
\end{equation}
By contrast, for $0<\mu_i<1$, the average is dominated by the $O(1)$ cutoff of the power-law tail and $\langle p_i \rangle \sim N^{-\mu_i}$.\footnote{From \eqref{eq:distpisa} one sees that $\mu_i$ cannot approach $0$.}
The normalization may thus be decomposed as\footnote{We neglect here sites with $\mu_i\simeq 1$, for which both the typical and cutoff contributions may matter.}
\begin{equation}
1=
\sum_{\mu_i>1\ {\rm or}\ \mu_i<0}\avg{p_i}
+
\sum_{0<\mu_i<1}\avg{p_i}.
\label{eq:normalization_decomp}
\end{equation}

We denote the first term $S_{\rm deloc} \equiv \sum_{\mu_i>1\ {\rm or}\ \mu_i<0}\avg{p_i}$ the delocalized contribution, and identify the \emph{delocalized phase} as the region where it dominates, $S_{\rm deloc} \simeq 1$.
This corresponds either to (i) all sites belonging to the first sum in Eq.~\eqref{eq:normalization_decomp}, or (ii) some sites belonging to the second sum, which nonetheless remains negligible as $N\to\infty$.
The normalization $S_{\rm deloc} \simeq 1$ then reads
\begin{equation} \label{selfconsistentdeloc}
1 \simeq  \frac{1}{N}\sum_{\mu_i>1\ {\rm or}\ \mu_i<0} \frac{\varphi+r_i N p_0}{\gamma+\varphi+r_i-m_i}.
\end{equation}
There are two cases: either (i) $\gamma=0$, and \eqref{selfconsistentdeloc} yields an equation for $p_0$ from which $x_m$ is determined; or (ii) $\gamma>0$, determined as the largest root of \eqref{selfconsistentdeloc} with $p_0=0$, which can be written
\begin{equation} \label{eq:eqgammadelocmui}
1 \simeq \frac{1}{N} \sum_{\mu_i>1\ {\rm or}\ \mu_i<0} \frac{\varphi}{r_i(1-\frac{1}{\mu_i})}.
\end{equation}

When this sum includes all sites, \eqref{selfconsistentdeloc} (with $p_0=0$) becomes identical to the equation obtained in \cite{BernardMFRandomG} for the growth rate $\gamma^0$ without resetting ($r_i=0$), up to the shift $m_i\to m_i-r_i$.
In the homogeneous case $m_i=m$, $r_i=r$, Eq.~\eqref{selfconsistentdeloc} gives $\gamma=m-r$, valid as long as $m>r$. If $m<r$, there is no growing solution and, for $x_0>0$, one has $\gamma=0$ with a stationary distribution given by \eqref{eq:distpisa} with $\mu_i=\mu=r/(m-\varphi)$ and $a_i=m-\varphi$. In the heterogeneous case with $r_i=r$ and no $\mu_i\in[0,1]$, the growth rate is $\gamma=\max(0, \gamma^0-r)$, with $\gamma^0$ determined in \cite{BernardMFRandomG}.\footnote{The condition that no $\mu_i\in[0,1]$ corresponds to the delocalized phase $\gamma^0=\gamma^0_{\rm deloc}$ in \cite{BernardMFRandomG}.}

In the general heterogeneous case, the phases and $\gamma$ depend on the distributions of $m_i$ and $r_i$. While populations or wealths can be very large, growth rates are typically finite; we focus on a generalized-Gaussian class. To keep the largest growth rates $O(1)$ as $N\to\infty$, the variance must scale with $N$:
\begin{equation}
\rho_N(m)=\frac{1}{Z_{b,N}}
\exp\!\left[-\left(\frac{(m-\mtyp)^2}{2\Sigma_N^2}\right)^b\right],
\qquad b>0,
\label{eq:generalized-gaussian}
\end{equation}
with $Z_{b,N}$ a normalization and
\begin{equation}
\Sigma_N=\frac{\Sigma_0}{\sqrt{2(\ln N)^{1/b}}},
\label{eq:SigmaN}
\end{equation}
so that $\max_i m_i\to \mtyp+\Sigma_0$ as $N\to\infty$.
The case $b=1$ is Gaussian, while $b=1/2$ corresponds to exponential tails. Most sites have $m_i\simeq \mtyp$, whereas sites near the upper edge $\mtyp+\Sigma_0$ are rare.

Alternatively, one may consider distributions with compact support, e.g.
\begin{equation} \label{eq:eqmcompact}
\rho(m)=\frac{\psi}{ m_>} \left(1 - \frac{m}{m_>}\right)^{\psi -1}
\qquad\text{for } 0<m<m_>,
\end{equation}
which behave differently near the upper edge and hence have different localization properties.
We analyze the generalized-Gaussian class in the main text and discuss the compact-support case in Sec.~\ref{sec:supp_compact_support} of the Supplemental Material.
For simplicity, we take a constant resetting rate $r_i=r$; the results below generalize to heterogeneous rates.

\textit{Delocalized phase.}
Since most $m_i$ are concentrated around $\mtyp$, Eq.~\eqref{selfconsistentdeloc} gives, for $\gamma>0$,
\begin{equation}
1 \simeq S_{\rm deloc} =
\frac{\varphi}{\gamma+\varphi+r-\mtyp}
+O(\Sigma_N^2).
\end{equation}
At leading order, $\gamma=\gamma_{\rm deloc}=\mtyp-r$, with corrections of order $\Sigma_N^2\sim(\ln N)^{-1/b}$. For these sites the tail exponent is $\mu_i=\frac{r}{r-(m_0-m_i)-\varphi}\simeq \frac{r}{r-\varphi}$, consistent with $\mu_i>1$ or $\mu_i<0$. This holds for $\mtyp>r$; for $\mtyp<r$, one has $\gamma_{\rm deloc}=0$ with a site-dependent stationary distribution \eqref{eq:distpisa}. In summary,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:predgaussdeloc}
\gamma_{\rm deloc}=\max(0,\mtyp-r).
\end{equation}

\textit{Partially localized phase.}
We now return to the second sum in Eq.~\eqref{eq:normalization_decomp}, $S_{\rm p.l.}\equiv \sum_{0<\mu_i<1}\avg{p_i}$.
When this contribution is $O(1)$, broad-tail sites contribute to the normalization and the fluctuations of $\bar x(t)$ are no longer negligible.
In a first stage we still neglect them, which yields a simple estimate of the tail exponent and the correct phase boundaries; their effect is discussed afterwards.
Within this approximation, once $p_i$ is large enough and $a_i>0$, the multiplicative term $a_i p_i$ dominates the exchange term, so the time to reach $p_i=p$ is
\begin{equation}
\tau(p)\simeq \frac{\ln(Np)}{m_i-\varphi-\gamma}.
\label{eq:tail_time_beyond_sa}
\end{equation}
Since a reset-free interval $\tau=\tau(p)$ occurs with probability $\sim e^{-r\tau}$, this gives, for $N^{-1}\ll p\ll1$,
\begin{equation}
P_i(p)\sim N^{-\mu_i}p^{-1-\mu_i},
\qquad
\mu_i\simeq \frac{r}{m_i-\varphi-\gamma}.
\label{eq:tail_mu_beyond_sa}
\end{equation}
In the main panel of Fig.~\ref{fig:tau_multifractal} we verify that $P_i(p)$ has the predicted power-law tail.
For a site with $0<\mu_i<1$, the average is dominated by the $O(1)$ cutoff,
\begin{equation}
\avg{p_i}\sim N^{-\mu_i}.
\label{eq:mean_pi_broad}
\end{equation}
To simplify, we take the $m_i$ deterministic, ordered $m_1>m_2>\dots$, and given by their quantiles $\int_{m_i}^{+\infty}\rho_N(u)\,du=i/N$. For $i\sim N^u$ with $0<u<1$,
\begin{equation}
m_i\simeq m(u)=\mtyp+\Sigma_0(1-u)^{1/(2b)}.
\label{eq:m_of_u}
\end{equation}
The sites with $0<\mu_i<1$ then contribute
\begin{align}
S_{\rm p.l.}
&\sim
\sum_{0<\mu_i<1}N^{-\mu_i}
\nonumber\\
&\simeq
\ln N\int_0^{u_c}
\ddd u\,\exp\!\left[\ln N\,(u-\mu(u))\right],
\label{eq:SN_saddle}
\end{align}
where
\begin{equation}
\mu(u)=\frac{r}{m(u)-\varphi-\gamma}
\label{eq:g_def}
\end{equation}
is increasing on $u\in[0,1]$, and $u_c$ is defined by $\mu(u_c)=1$. The factor $N^u$ counts the sites at rank $i\sim N^u$, while $N^{-\mu(u)}$ is the probability for such a site to be localized ($p_i=O(1)$). At large $N$, $S_{\rm p.l.}$ is controlled by
\begin{equation}
G(\gamma)=\max_{0\le u\le u_c} \big[u-\mu(u)\big].
\label{eq:G_gamma_def}
\end{equation}
If $G(\gamma)<0$ the broad-tail contribution vanishes; if $G(\gamma)>0$, $S_{\rm p.l.}$ grows with $N$, inconsistent with $\sum_i \langle p_i\rangle=1$.
The partially localized estimate is thus the value for which $G(\gamma_{\rm pl})=0$. Denoting $u^\star$ the maximizer, this requires
\begin{equation}
u^\star-\mu(u^\star)=0,
\qquad
1-\mu'(u^\star)=0
\label{eq:saddle_conditions}
\end{equation}
simultaneously,\footnote{$u^\star=u_c$ is never reached, as it would imply $u^\star=\mu(u_c)=1$, hence $\gamma_{\rm pl}=\mtyp-\varphi-r<\gamma_{\rm deloc}=\mtyp-r$.} giving
\begin{equation}
\gamma_{\rm pl}=m(u^\star)-\varphi-\frac{r}{u^\star}, \qquad r=-m'(u^\star)\,(u^\star)^2.
\label{eq:gamma_pl_general}
\end{equation}
For Eq.~\eqref{eq:m_of_u}, the second equation reads\footnote{For $b<1/2$ the r.h.s. reaches a maximum at $u^\star=4b/(1+2b)$, so the phase can exist only for $r<r_c$.}
\begin{equation}
r=
\frac{\Sigma_0}{2b}(u^\star)^2
(1-u^\star)^{\frac{1}{2b}-1}.
\label{eq:r_u_star}
\end{equation}
The true growth rate is the maximum of the two estimates,
\begin{equation}
\gamma=\max(\gamma_{\rm deloc},\gamma_{\rm pl}).
\label{eq:gamma_max_selection}
\end{equation}
Indeed, $1=S_{\rm deloc}(\gamma)+S_{\rm p.l.}(\gamma)$ with both terms decreasing in $\gamma$, so the solution is unique. If $\gamma_{\rm deloc}>\gamma_{\rm pl}$, then $G(\gamma_{\rm deloc})<0$, $S_{\rm p.l.}\to0$, and the delocalized estimate is consistent; if $\gamma_{\rm deloc}<\gamma_{\rm pl}$, the p.l. contribution would exceed $O(1)$ and the only solution is $\gamma=\gamma_{\rm pl}$. The transition line is $\gamma_{\rm pl}=\gamma_{\rm deloc}$.

The behavior of $S_{\rm p.l.}$ thus defines two phases. When $S_{\rm p.l.}\to0$, the broad-tail sites are irrelevant at large $N$ and $\gamma$ is set by sites with $\langle p_i \rangle=O(1/N)$, Eq.~\eqref{selfconsistentdeloc}; the probability that any site carries $p_i=O(1)$ vanishes, defining the \emph{delocalized phase}.
When $S_{\rm p.l.}=O(1)$, rare reset-free episodes let a site carry a finite fraction of the population. The saddle in \eqref{eq:SN_saddle} selects sites with $m_i\simeq m(u^\star)$, for which $\mu(u^\star)=u^\star$: each carries $p_i\sim O(1)$ with probability $N^{-u^\star}$, but there are $N^{u^\star}$ candidates. The system is therefore localized at a given time, while the dominant site changes within a subextensive pool of $N^{u^\star}$ favorable sites on the $O(1)$ resetting timescale. We call this the \emph{partially localized phase}.

The above uses the self-averaging approximation. The fluctuations of $\ln\bar x(t)$, encoded in their asymptotic variance $\sigma_S^2$, can be included; the equations no longer close and $\sigma_S^2$ is evaluated numerically (see End Matter). This yields a corrected tail exponent \eqref{eq:mu_sigma} and hence a corrected $\gamma_{\rm pl}$, Eqs.~\eqref{eq:gamma_pl_sigma}, \eqref{eq:ustar_sigma}. Since $\sigma_S$ vanishes at the transition, the phase diagram in Fig.~\ref{fig:phase_and_growth} is unchanged. The correction slightly decreases $\mu_i$ and slightly increases $\gamma_{\rm pl}$, consistent with Fig.~\ref{fig:tau_multifractal} and the inset of Fig.~\ref{fig:phase_and_growth}. Finite-size effects are strong but their direction matches the analytical prediction, and are fitted in Fig.~\ref{fig:logfit}.

\textit{Multifractality.}
In the partially localized phase and for $q>1$, one has $\langle \sum_i p_i(t)^q \rangle=O(1)$: since for $0<\mu_i<1$ the upper cutoff gives $\avg{p_i^q}\sim \avg{p_i}\sim N^{-\mu_i}$, the averaged moment $\sum_i\avg{p_i^q}$ is controlled by the same saddle as the normalization and is $O(1)$.
By contrast, the moments of the averaged fractions $Y_q:=\sum_i\avg{p_i}^{q}$, which for $\mu_i<1$ measure how the probability to be localized is distributed among sites, display multifractal behavior. Using Eq.~\eqref{eq:mean_pi_broad}, the $0<\mu_i<1$ sites contribute
\begin{equation}
Y_q^{\ploc}
\sim
\sum_{0<\mu_i<1} N^{-q\mu_i}
\sim
\int \ddd u\,
\exp\!\left[\ln N\,h_q(u)\right],
\label{eq:Yq_saddle}
\end{equation}
with
\begin{equation}
h_q(u)=u- q\,\mu(u), \qquad \mu(u) \equiv\frac{r}{m(u)-\varphi-\gamma}.
\label{eq:hq_def}
\end{equation}
The $N$ delocalized sites, with $\langle p_i \rangle\sim 1/N$, contribute $Y_q^{\deloc}\sim N^{1-q}$, so that
\begin{equation}
Y_q\sim N^{-\theta(q)},
\qquad
\theta(q)=
-\max\!\left[
1-q,\,
\max_{0\le u\le u_c}h_q(u)
\right].
\label{eq:tau_q_saddle}
\end{equation}
The inset of Fig.~\ref{fig:tau_multifractal} shows the measured $\theta(q)$ for several reset rates, together with the saddle predictions. In the delocalized phase, higher moments can still be dominated by the saddle if some sites have $\mu_i<1$ (red and yellow curves).

