For comparison, a fully delocalized phase gives $\theta(q)=q-1$, while complete localization gives $\theta(q)=0$ for $q>1$.
Finally, $\theta(q)$ is related to the fractal-dimension spectrum $d(\alpha)$ by the Legendre transform $\theta(q)=\min_{\alpha}\left[q\alpha-d(\alpha)\right]$, where the number of sites with $\avg{p_i}\sim N^{-\alpha}$ scales as $N^{d(\alpha)}$. In our parametrization, $d(\mu(u))=u$, together with the delocalized point $d(1)=1$.

\textit{Discussion.}
The nature of the temporal noise, and not merely the average dynamics, controls how the population organizes. With continuous multiplicative noise the same model sustains a genuinely localized phase in which a \emph{fixed} $O(1)$ set of favorable sites carries a finite fraction of the total forever \cite{BernardMFRandomG}. Local resetting removes this possibility: after a reset a site restarts from $p_i=O(1/N)$ and needs a reset-free interval of order $\ln N$ to climb back to an $O(1)$ share, while resets recur on an $O(1)$ timescale, so no fixed finite set can survive. What remains is a partially localized phase in which dominance is real but transient: a subextensive pool of $\sim N^{u^\star}$ near-optimal sites competes, and at any instant a small subset holds an $O(1)$ fraction while the leaders continually turn over. Reset-interrupted growth episodes indeed generate power-law tails with \emph{site-dependent} exponents $\mu_i=r/(m_i-\varphi-\gamma)$, and hence a multifractal spectrum $\theta(q)$.
Such a family of models therefore naturally generates weight multifractality,
similar to the fractal and multifractal eigenstates found in generalized
Rosenzweig--Porter random-matrix ensembles
\cite{Kravtsov2015,BogomolnySieber2018,VonSoostenWarzel2019,cugliandolo2024multifractal,KutlinKhaymovich2024}.

This decoupling of \emph{concentration} from \emph{persistence} is, we believe, the most interesting feature of the model, and a natural one in the context of firm or city growth, where resetting mimics bankruptcy or disruptive obsolescence. A very high level of concentration can coexist with permanent reshuffling of the leaders — the losers of today can be the winners of tomorrow — without any contradiction.
A related and more realistic variant would draw a
fresh growth rate $m_i$ at each resetting event. We expect this to leave the phase
diagram unchanged, amounting only to a relabelling of the changing leaders, while making the turnover of dominant
sites explicit.


A second useful output is a sharp criterion for aggregate growth. In the delocalized phase $\gamma=\max(0,\mtyp-r)$: the system grows only if the \emph{typical} unit outpaces the reset rate. In the partially localized phase, growth can be positive even when the typical unit does not grow, because it is carried by the rare, rotating stars; $\gamma_{\rm pl}$ is then set by the best available units rather than the bulk. The transition therefore separates growth as a bulk property from growth as an extreme-value phenomenon. Resetting acts singularly here: for small $r$, $\gamma_{\rm pl}$ approaches its deterministic value as $\gamma_{\rm pl}\simeq \mtyp+\Sigma_0-\varphi-\sqrt{2\Sigma_0 r/b}$ (Supplemental Material), so an arbitrarily small rate of catastrophes has an outsized, non-analytic effect on the growth carried by dominant units.

The case of partial resetting is also interesting, where $x_i \to f x_i + (1 - f) x_0$, generalising the $f=0$ case considered above and treated in SI3.A. This is a stylized model of inheritance tax (tax rate $1-f$), with Poisson death events. When $f > f_c$ is sufficiently large, the localized phase discussed in \cite{BernardMFRandomG} reappears, meaning that dominant dynasties can persist for ever in that case. Within the self-averaging approximation and Gaussian rates the localized phase exists for $f>f_c = \exp( -\sqrt{\Sigma_0/r})$.

Several further extensions are natural: slow evolution of the $m_i$'s on time scale $\sim \ln N$, correlated or anti-correlated $(m_i,r_i)$ (productive units failing less often or becoming more fragile), the compact-support case, which yields only a marginal partially localized phase ($\mu\to1$, $\gamma=m_>-\varphi-r$, Supplemental Material) and underlines the role of the upper edge of $\rho(m)$. Addition of independent white noises to the local growth rates, $m_i \to m_i + \xi_i(t)$, see Supplemental Material, allows to interpolate with the results of Ref.~\cite{BernardMFRandomG}. The most important extension, in our view, is to consider finite dimensional lattices and investigate whether our scenario generates genuine multifractal fields in that case.
Other interesting directions involve adding logistic term as in some ecology models where power law distribution of fractions can also be dynamically generated \cite{al2026spatiotemporal}.


