{\it Tail exponent}. We derive here the correction to the tail exponent taking into account the fluctuations of the total population. From Eq.\eqref{eq:model_deterministic_part} the total population satisfies,
\begin{equation}
\frac{\ddd\ln \bar x}{\ddd t}
=
\sum_j m_jp_j.
\label{eq:endmatter_logX_det}
\end{equation}
while the fractions obey the exact equation
\begin{equation}
\frac{\ddd p_i}{\ddd t}
=
\left(m_i-\varphi-\frac{\ddd\ln \bar x}{\ddd t}\right)p_i
+\frac{\varphi}{N}.
\label{eq:endmatter_exact_pi_log}
\end{equation}
Let $t_0$ be the last resetting time of site $i$. If site $i$ is not reset between $t_0$ and $t$, integration gives
\begin{align}
p_i(t)
&=
p_i(t_0^+)
\exp\!\left[
(m_i-\varphi)(t-t_0)
-\ln\frac{\bar x(t)}{\bar x(t_0^+)}
\right]
\nonumber\\
&\quad+
\frac{\varphi}{N}
\int_{t_0}^t\ddd s\,
\exp\!\left[
(m_i-\varphi)(t-s)
-\ln\frac{\bar x(t)}{\bar x(s)}
\right].
\label{eq:endmatter_pi_logX}
\end{align}
In the partially localized phase, $\gamma>0$ and the first term is negligible.

Moreover, we assume that the fluctuations of $\ddd\ln \bar x/\ddd t$ have an $O(1)$ correlation time set by the resetting time, and that the accumulated fluctuations are Gaussian and diffusive. This is checked numerically in Fig. \ref{fig:logfit} (Left). We therefore write
\begin{equation}
\ln\frac{\bar x(t)}{\bar x(t-s)}
\simeq
\gamma s+\sigma_S B_s,
\label{eq:logx_brownian}
\end{equation}
where $B_s$ is a standard Brownian motion.
Eq.~\eqref{eq:endmatter_pi_logX} becomes
\begin{equation}
p_i(t)
\simeq
\frac{\varphi}{N}
\int_0^\tau \ddd s\,
\exp\!\left[a_i s+\sigma_S B_s\right],
\qquad
\tau=t-t_0 .
\label{eq:endmatter_brownian_integral}
\end{equation}
with $a_i=m_i-\varphi-\gamma$ and $\tau$ is the time since the last reset of site $i$.
The upper tail is controlled by the exponential scale of the last reset-free episode. We therefore write, for the purpose of extracting the exponent,
\begin{equation}
p_i(t)\simeq C_i
\exp\!\left[a_i \tau+\sigma_S B_\tau\right],
\qquad
C_i=O(N^{-1}),
\end{equation}
Here $\tau$ is distributed exponentially with rate $r$, and independent of $B$. Let
\begin{equation}
w=\ln\frac{p_i}{C_i}=a_i \tau+\sigma_S B_\tau.
\end{equation}
Conditioned on $\tau=s$, $w$ is Gaussian with mean $a_i s$ and variance $\sigma_S^2 s$. Averaging over the exponential distribution of $\tau$ gives
the PDF of $w$, $P(w)$ as
\begin{align}
P(w)
&=
\int_0^\infty \ddd s\,
r\eee^{-r s}
\frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi\sigma_S^2 s}}
\exp\!\left[-\frac{(w-a_i s)^2}{2\sigma_S^2s}\right]
\nonumber\\
&=
\frac{r}{\Delta_i}
\exp\!\left[
\frac{a_i w-\Delta_i |w|}{\sigma_S^2}
\right],
\label{eq:endmatter_fx}
\end{align}
where $\Delta_i=\sqrt{a_i^2+2r\sigma_S^2}$. For $w>0$, $P(w) \sim \exp(-\mu_i w)$, with
\begin{equation}
r
=
a_i\mu_i+\frac{\sigma_S^2}{2}\mu_i^2 .
\label{eq:mu_quadratic_sigma}
\end{equation}
Equivalently,
\begin{equation}
\mu_i
=
\frac{\sqrt{a_i^2+2r\sigma_S^2}-a_i}{\sigma_S^2}
=
\frac{2r}{\sqrt{a_i^2+2r\sigma_S^2}+a_i}.
\label{eq:mu_sigma}
\end{equation}
One recovers Eq.\eqref{eq:distpisa}, $\mu_i=r/a_i$ when $\sigma_S^2\to0$. Since $p_i=C_i\eee^w$, the change of variables gives the upper tail
\begin{equation}
P_i(p)\sim N^{-\mu_i}p^{-1-\mu_i},
\qquad
N^{-1}\ll p\ll 1,
\label{eq:tail_mu_sigma}
\end{equation}
with an $O(1)$ cutoff imposed by normalization.

At fixed $\sigma_S^2$, the partially localized estimate follows from the same saddle calculation as in the main text.
\begin{equation}
S_{\rm p.l.}
\sim
\int \ddd u\, N^{u-\mu(u)} ,
\end{equation}
where $m(u)$ is the rank profile and $\mu(u)$ is given by Eq.~\eqref{eq:mu_sigma}, with $a_i$ replaced by
$m(u)-\varphi-\gamma$. At an interior saddle $u^\star$,$
\mu(u^\star)=u^\star$ and $\mu'(u^\star)=1$ which yields
\begin{equation}
\gamma_{\rm pl}(\sigma_S^2)
=
m(u^\star)-\varphi-\frac{r}{u^\star}
+\frac{\sigma_S^2}{2}u^\star,
\label{eq:gamma_pl_sigma}
\end{equation}
with
\begin{equation}
r
=
-(u^\star)^2m'(u^\star)
-\frac{\sigma_S^2}{2}(u^\star)^2.
\label{eq:ustar_sigma}
\end{equation}
For the generalized-Gaussian distribution $m(u)=\mtyp+\Sigma_0(1-u)^{1/(2b)}$, this becomes
\begin{equation}
r
=
\frac{\Sigma_0}{2b}(u^\star)^2
(1-u^\star)^{\frac{1}{2b}-1}
-\frac{\sigma_S^2}{2}(u^\star)^2.
\label{eq:ustar_sigma_generalized_gaussian}
\end{equation}
These equations reduce to Eqs.~\eqref{eq:gamma_pl_general} and \eqref{eq:r_u_star} for $\sigma_S^2=0$. They are corrected estimates at fixed $\sigma_S^2$, not a closed prediction, because $\sigma_S^2$ is itself determined numerically from the slope of mean square variation, as done in Fig. \ref{fig:logfit}.

{\it Exact expression for the growth rate $\gamma$}.
Eq. \eqref{eq:endmatter_logX_det} yields a useful expression for the  growth exponent. When site $i$ resets, $\bar x\to \bar x(1-p_i+x_0/(N \bar x))$ and therefore
\begin{equation}
\Delta\ln \bar x
=
\ln\left(1-p_i+\frac{x_0}{(N \bar x)}\right).
\end{equation}
Taking the average, in the growing regime where $x_0/(N \bar x)\to0$,
\begin{equation}
\gamma
=
\sum_j m_j\avg{p_j}
+
\sum_j r_j\avg{\ln(1-p_j)}.
\label{eq:endmatter_gamma_log_balance}
\end{equation}
In the delocalized phase, the relevant sites have either $\mu_i<0$ or $\mu_i>1$, so $p_i=O(N^{-1})$ typically. Expanding $\ln(1-p_i)\simeq-p_i$ in Eq.~\eqref{eq:endmatter_gamma_log_balance} gives
\begin{equation}
\gamma
\simeq
\sum_{\mu_i<0 \, \text{or} \, \mu_i>1} (m_i-r_i)\avg{p_i}.
\end{equation}
Together with the expression for $\avg{p_i}$ given in Eq.~\eqref{eq:mean_pi}, one can check that it is equivalent to Eq.~\eqref{selfconsistentdeloc}. In the partially localized phase, Eq.\eqref{eq:endmatter_gamma_log_balance} gives a direct relation between the growth rate $\gamma$ and the marginal distributions $P_i(p)$.



