\section{Annealed Growth Exponent}
\label{sec:supp_annealed_gamma}

The exponent $\gamma$ used in the main text is a typical growth exponent,
\begin{equation}
\gamma=\lim_{t\to\infty}\frac{1}{t}\ln\overline{x}(t).
\end{equation}
One can alternatively define an annealed exponent $\gamma_a$
{
\begin{equation}
\gamma_a =\lim_{t\to\infty}\frac{1}{t}\ln \avg{\overline{x}(t)},
\label{eq:supp_gamma_a_def}
\end{equation}
} where the average is over resetting events for a given set of $m_i$'s. The annealed exponent is sensitive to rare fluctuations and might differ from the typical exponent $\gamma$. {Self-averaging and Jensen’s inequality then imply} $\gamma_a \geq \gamma$.

We first recall the one-site calculation.
Consider
\begin{equation}
\dot x=m x,
\qquad
x\to x_0
\end{equation}
at Poissonian rate $r$.
If no reset has occurred before time $t$, the position is $x_0\eee^{mt}$ and this event has probability $\eee^{-rt}$.
Otherwise, the last reset occurred a time $\tau$ before observation, with probability $r\eee^{-r\tau}$.
The renewal formula { for the PDF of $x$, $P(x,t)$,} is therefore
\begin{equation}
P(x,t)
=
\eee^{-rt}\delta\!\left(x-x_0\eee^{mt}\right)
+r\int_0^t\ddd \tau\,\eee^{-r\tau}
\delta\!\left(x-x_0\eee^{m\tau}\right).
\label{eq:supp_single_site_renewal}
\end{equation}
for $x(0)=x_0$.
The { average} follows directly from Eq.~\eqref{eq:supp_single_site_renewal},
\begin{equation}
\avg{x(t)}
=
x_0\eee^{(m-r)t}
+r x_0\int_0^t\ddd \tau\,\eee^{(m-r)\tau}.
\label{eq:supp_single_site_mean}
\end{equation}
For $m<r$, the {average} reaches a stationary value
\begin{equation}
\avg{x}_\infty=x_0\frac{r}{r-m}.
\end{equation}
For $m>r$, it grows as $\avg{x(t)} {\simeq  \frac{m x_0}{m-r}} \eee^{(m-r)t}$, {so $\gamma_a=m-r$. For $m=r$ it also grows, but as
$\avg{x(t)}=x_0(1+ r t)$, so $\gamma_a=0$}.
\\

We now turn to a $N$-site problem, {with a general growth rate matrix $M$ and where sites reset independently.}
Between resets,
\begin{equation}
\dot x_i=\sum_j M_{ij}x_j,
\label{eq:supp_general_linear}
\end{equation}
and site $i$ is reset to $x_{0i}$ at rate $r_i$.
Let ${\cal R}_k$ be the operator which resets site $k$,
\begin{equation}
({\cal R}_k x)_i=x_i(1-\delta_{ik})+x_{0k}\delta_{ik}.
\label{eq:supp_reset_operator}
\end{equation}
{ The superposition of the independent reset clocks is a Poisson process of rate $\sum_j r_j$.} Conditioning on the last reset event {and the site $k$ where it occurs,} gives the renewal equation
\begin{equation}
P(\vec x,t)
=
\delta\!\left(\vec x-\eee^{Mt}\vec x(0)\right)
\eee^{-\sum_j r_j t}
 +
\sum_k r_k\int_0^t\ddd \tau\,\eee^{-\sum_j r_j\tau}
\int \ddd \vec y\,P(\vec y,t-\tau)
\delta\!\left(\vec x-\eee^{M\tau}{\cal R}_k\vec y\right).
\label{eq:supp_vector_renewal}
\end{equation}
{where we use the vector notation $\vec x=(x_1,\dots,x_N)$.}
Taking the first moment of Eq.~\eqref{eq:supp_vector_renewal} yields
\begin{equation}
\avg{\vec x}_t =
\eee^{-\sum_j r_j t}\eee^{Mt}\vec x(0)
\,+ \,
\sum_k r_k\int_0^t\ddd \tau\,\eee^{-\sum_j r_j\tau}
\eee^{M\tau}\avg{{\cal R}_k\vec x}_{t-\tau}.
\label{eq:supp_mean_renewal}
\end{equation}
{where $\avg{\vec x}_t=\avg{\vec x(t)}= \int d^Nx \vec x P(\vec x,t)$,
and
$\avg{{\cal R}_k\vec x}_{t-\tau}$ means the average of the reset-transformed state.}
Using
\begin{equation}
\sum_k r_k({\cal R}_k x)_i
=x_i\left(\sum_k r_k-r_i\right)+r_i x_{0i},
\label{eq:supp_reset_average}
\end{equation}
and the Laplace transform $\widehat{x}_i(s)=\int_0^{+\infty} dt e^{-s t} \avg{x_i(t)}$ one has
\begin{equation}
\sum_j\left[(s+r_i)\delta_{ij}-M_{ij}\right]\widehat{x}_j(s)
=x_i(0)+\frac{r_i x_{0i}}{s}.
\label{eq:supp_laplace_mean}
\end{equation}
Hence,
\begin{equation}
\widehat{x}_i(s)
=
\sum_j
\left[(sI+R-M)^{-1}\right]_{ij}
\left(x_j(0)+\frac{r_jx_{0j}}{s}\right).
\label{eq:supp_laplace_solution}
\end{equation}
where $R_{ij}=r_i \delta_{ij}$, which upon Laplace inversion
determines $\langle x(t) \rangle$. {Note that \eqref{eq:supp_laplace_mean} is equivalent to the equation
\begin{equation}
\frac{\ddd}{\ddd t}\avg{\vec x(t)}
=
(M-R)\avg{\vec x(t)}
+
R\vec x_0.
\label{eq:supp_mean_dynamics}
\end{equation}
}
Therefore, focusing on a symmetric matrix $M$, the annealed growth rate is fixed by the largest eigenvalue of $M-R$ (provided it is positive):
\begin{equation}
\gamma_a=\max\left(0,\lambda_{\max}\left[M-R \right]\right),
\label{eq:supp_gamma_a_matrix}
\end{equation}
for generic initial conditions. If all eigenvalues of $M-R$
are strictly negative, there is a steady state with $\gamma_a=0$ and $\langle \vec x(t) \rangle \to (R-M)^{-1} R \vec x_0$.
If the largest eigenvalue is zero, then $\gamma_a=0$ but the mean may grow polynomially because of the constant reset source.
The above results are true for arbitrary symmetric matrices $M$, hence for the model {defined on any graph, and in any space dimension.}

For the mean-field case,
\begin{equation}
M_{ij}=(m_i-\varphi)\delta_{ij}+\frac{\varphi}{N}.
\label{eq:supp_M_matrix}
\end{equation}
The eigenvalue problem for $M-R$ is the same as the heterogeneous mean-field problem without resetting, studied in Ref.~\cite{BernardMFRandomG}, after the shift $m_i\to m_i-r_i$.
When the largest eigenvalue of $M-R$ is strictly positive, the
annealed growth exponent $\gamma_a$ is the largest solution of
\begin{equation}
1=
\frac{\varphi}{N}\sum_i
\frac{1}{\gamma_a+\varphi+r_i-m_i} = \frac{1}{N}\sum_i
\frac{\varphi}{r_i( 1- \frac{1}{\mu_i^a})}  \quad, \quad \mu_i^a
=
\frac{r_i}{m_i-\varphi-\gamma_a}
\label{eq:supp_gamma_a_finiteN}
\end{equation}
If the largest eigenvalue of $M-R$ is zero or negative, then
$\gamma_a=0$. When it is strictly positive, the growth rate is thus given by $\gamma^0|_{M \to M-R}$ where $\gamma^0$ is the growth rate in the absence of resetting, i.e. for $r_i=0$, determined in \cite{BernardMFRandomG}. The latter is exact and valid in all the
phases of Ref. \cite{BernardMFRandomG}.
We have introduced the annealed analog of the exponents $\mu_i=\frac{r_i}{m_i-\varphi-\gamma}$ defined in the text, with $\mu_i^a \geq \mu_i$.
When all the $\mu_i>1$ {or $\mu_i<0$}, this equation is the same as Eq.\eqref{eq:mean_pi},\eqref{eq:normalization_decomp} and $\mu_i=\mu_i^a$, $\gamma=\gamma_a$. On the other hand, when the smallest positive $\mu_1 =\min_{\mu_i>0}{\mu_i}$ (which is associated to the largest $m_i-r_i$) becomes smaller than $1$,  $\mu_1^a$ is forced to saturate at $1^+$ as $1/(1-1/\mu_1^a)$ in \eqref{eq:supp_gamma_a_finiteN} starts diverging. In this case, $\mu_1^a>\mu_1$ and $\gamma_a>\gamma$.
Hence, when there exist sites with $0<\mu_i<1$, the annealed exponent is instead dominated by rare reset-free periods, and differs from the typical growth exponent. {We emphasize that} it can happen already in the delocalized phase. {Note that individual terms in the sum
\eqref{eq:supp_gamma_a_finiteN} can be interpreted as annealed fractions, i.e. defining
$p_i^a(t) = \langle x_i(t) \rangle/\sum_j \langle x_j(t) \rangle$, one has
for $\gamma_a>0$, $p_i^a = \lim_{t \to +\infty} p_i^a(t)= \frac{\varphi}{N r_i( 1- \frac{1}{\mu_i^a})}$. The case $\mu_i^a\to 1^+$ can thus be
associated to "annealed localization" i.e. the first moment being dominated by rare reset histories associated with the most favorable site.}

For homogeneous parameters $m_i=m$ and $r_i=r$, Eq.~\eqref{eq:supp_gamma_a_finiteN} gives
\begin{equation}
\gamma_a=\gamma=\max{(0,m-r)}.
\end{equation}
For $m_i$ chosen according to the generalized-Gaussian class and for uniform $r=r_i$, the large-$N$ solution of Eq.~\eqref{eq:supp_gamma_a_finiteN} is given by
\begin{equation}
\gamma_a= \max{(0,m_0-r,m_0+\Sigma_0-r-\varphi)}
\label{eq:supp_gamma_a_edge}
\end{equation}
We have used the result of Ref.~\cite{BernardMFRandomG} for $\gamma^0_{\rm loc}$. Since $\lim_{N \to +\infty} \max_i m_i=m_0+\Sigma_0$, the collective/delocalized and edge/localized branches approach $m_0-r$ and
$m_0+\Sigma_0-r-\varphi$, respectively, leading to \eqref{eq:supp_gamma_a_edge}.


\section{Compact-Support Class}
\label{sec:supp_compact_support}

\begin{figure}
\centering
