We now estimate the growth rate and the distribution of the $p_i$ when the
$m_i$ are drawn from a distribution with a finite upper edge $m_>$, near which
the density behaves as $\rho(m)\sim \left(m_>-m\right)^{\psi-1}$ with $\psi>0$. In particular, we will study
\begin{equation}
\rho(m)
=
\frac{\psi}{m_>}
\left(1-\frac{m}{m_>}\right)^{\psi-1},
\qquad
0<m<m_>.
\label{eq:supp_compact_density}
\end{equation}
and a uniform resetting rate $r_i=r$.

\subsection{Delocalized phase} \label{Db}

In the delocalized phase, we show below that no site has $
\mu_i=\frac{r}{m_i-\varphi-\gamma}$ in $[0,1]$. For $\gamma>0$, the normalization condition $\sum_{i=1}^N\langle p_i \rangle=1 $ leads to the exact equality
\begin{equation}
1=
\frac{\varphi}{N}\sum_i
\frac{1}{\gamma + r+\varphi-m_i}
\label{eq:supp_compact_deloc_finiteN}
\end{equation}
This equation is identical to the one obtained in Ref.~\cite{BernardMFRandomG} for the growth rate $\gamma^0$ in
the absence of resetting, i.e. for $r=0$, which implies that
in that case
\be  \label{eq:defgammagamma0}
\gamma = \gamma^0 - r.
\ee
which is valid for arbitrary $N$. Note that the condition that no $\mu_i$ is in $[0,1]$
implies that $\gamma > m_i - r - \varphi$ for all $i$, hence from
\eqref{eq:defgammagamma0}, that $\gamma^0 > m_i - \varphi$, for all i.

In the limit of large $N$, it was shown that $\gamma^0= \gamma^0(\varphi)$ is
determined as the solution of the equation
\begin{equation}
\frac{1}{\varphi}=G(\gamma^{0}+\varphi),
\qquad \gamma^{0}+\varphi>m_>, \qquad
G(z)=\int \ddd m\,\frac{\rho(m)}{z-m}.
\label{eq:supp_compact_stieltjes}
\end{equation}
Since we assume here that
$\mu_1 =\min_{\mu_i>0}{\mu_i}>1$, it implies that, in the
large $N$ limit,
$\gamma^0>m_> - \varphi$, i.e. the delocalized branch,
hence there exists a unique solution
to \eqref{eq:supp_compact_stieltjes}. For the model \eqref{eq:supp_compact_density}, one has the explicit expression $G(z)=
\frac{1}{z}
{}_2F_1\!\left(1,1;\psi+1;\frac{m_>}{z}\right)$, as shown in \cite{BernardMFRandomG}.

When the resetting rate is sufficiently large, so that $\gamma^{0}(\varphi)-r$ becomes negative, the system instead has a delocalized stationary state with $\gamma=0$.

The delocalized growth exponent is therefore
\begin{equation}
\gamma_{\rm deloc}=\max\left(\gamma^{0}(\varphi)-r,0\right).
\label{eq:supp_compact_gamma_deloc}
\end{equation}
Hence, within the delocalized phase, there is a transition
at $\varphi=\varphi_c^0$ between
a phase with growth and a phase without growth, where
$\varphi_c^0$ is solution of
\be
\gamma^{0}(\varphi_c^0)=r
\ee
where we recall that $\gamma^{0}(\varphi)$ is a decreasing
function. In
Fig. \ref{fig:phasediagcompact} we show the region
of the phase diagram where this transition occurs.

\subsection{Marginal partially localized branch} \label{Mplb}

We now study the existence and properties of a localized phase. We use again the quantiles of the distribution
For the  density \eqref{eq:supp_compact_density}, they satisfy
\begin{equation}
\frac{i}{N}
=
\left(1-\frac{m_i}{m_>}\right)^\psi,
\qquad
m_i=m_>\left[1-\left(\frac{i}{N}\right)^{1/\psi}\right].
\label{eq:supp_compact_quantiles}
\end{equation}

We recall the tail exponent $
\mu_i=\frac{r}{m_i-\varphi-\gamma}$,
and we now show that one cannot have $\mu_1<1$. We first assume that it is possible and then show a contradiction.
Introducing
\begin{equation}
\Delta=m_>-\varphi-r-\gamma
\end{equation}
one has, with $y=m_>-m$,
\begin{equation}
\mu(y)=\frac{r}{r+\Delta-y}.
\end{equation}
The sites with $0<\mu(y)<1$ have $0<y<\Delta$.
Since the number of sites with $y<m_i<y+dy$ is given by $N \rho(m_>-y) dy$, the partially localized contribution to the normalization, $S_{\rm p.l.} = \sum_{0<\mu_i<1} \langle p_i \rangle$, reads
\begin{equation}
S_{\rm p.l.}
\sim
N\int_0^\Delta \ddd y\,\rho(m_>-y)\,N^{-\mu(y)}.
\end{equation}
Using $\rho(m_>-y)\sim y^{\psi-1}$, this gives
\begin{equation}
S_{\rm p.l.}
\sim
\int_0^\Delta \ddd y\,y^{\psi-1}
\exp\!\left[
\ln N\,\frac{\Delta-y}{r+\Delta-y}
\right].
\label{eq:supp_compact_Spl_integral}
\end{equation}
For fixed $\Delta>0$, the exponent is positive at $y=0$, so $S_{\rm p.l.}$ diverges with $N$.
The normalization therefore forces $\Delta\to0$, i.e.
\begin{equation}
\mu_1\to1,
\qquad
\gamma\to m_>-\varphi-r.
\end{equation}

In other words, the extensive number of sites near the upper edges forces $\mu(m_>)$ to saturate at $1$ and the growth rate is $\gamma=\gamma_{\rm pl} = m_>-\varphi-r$.
We thus denote this phase as marginal partially localized.



