\section{Introduction}\label{sec:intro}

Large language models generalise in ways that were not anticipated in training.
In particular, novel behaviours can be elicited simply via prompting, including few-shot learning of supervised tasks~\citep{brown2020language} or adopting different roles depending on a set of instructions~\citep{shanahan2023role}.
This In-Context Learning (ICL) mechanism is not captured by the classical learning-theoretic paradigm, where a model is evaluated on fresh samples from same the distribution it was trained on.
Nevertheless, ICL is a critical source of both generality and risk~\citep{anwar2024foundational} in modern AI systems, calling for a theory of what trained models do beyond their training distribution.

An early theoretical attempt framed ICL as implicit Bayesian inference of the latent concept behind the prompt~\citep{xie2022bayesian}.
This framework has found extensive support:
trained transformers behave as Bayes-optimal estimators for many families of tasks~\citep{garg2022what}, and how such estimators are represented and acquired during training is understood in simple settings~\citep{nichani2024how,edelman2024the,lu2025asymptotic,zhang2025training,bondaschi2026from}.
However, other settings admit distinct classes of predictors, and models transition between them as the training conditions vary~\citep{akyurek2023what}.
\citet{raventos2023pretraining} identified task diversity as the key control parameter:
When trained at low diversity, the transformer can only memorise the training tasks, acquiring general ICL abilities only above a certain task-diversity threshold.

The role of task diversity has been explored in synthetic data, revealing that the competition between the two behaviours extends along training time~\citep{singh2023transient,reddy2024mechanistic,nguyen2025differential,park2025competition,gibson2026distinct}, culminating in a hierarchical Bayesian framework that fits the model behaviour to a weighted mixture of the memorising and generalising predictors~\citep{carroll2025dynamics, wurgaft2025incontext}.
This framework is phenomenological: still missing is a \emph{microscopic} theory that predicts the favoured solution from data properties alone, and how much data it takes for one solution to give way to the other.

\paragraph{Our Contribution}
Here we provide this theory in a synthetic dataset containing the minimal required ingredients: a task diversity parameter $D$, a vocabulary of size $K$, a document length $T$ and a corpus of $N$ training documents.
Task diversity enters as a mixture of $D$ latent topics from which documents are generated~(\autoref{fig:main}, left).
In this setting, we provide:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{A solvable model of strategy competition.} We characterise analytically two distinct Bayes-optimal predictors: a \emph{specialised} one, relying on knowledge of the $D$ topics in the data-generating process, and an \emph{in-context} one, equivalent to the add-one estimator from statistics under our data model (\autoref{sec:rtm}).
  \item \textbf{A first-principles learning-curve theory for specialisation.} We derive the specialisation learning curve in a Bayesian learning setting: the excess loss over the asymptotic floor is $DK/(2NT)$ at leading order---a function of the number of training tokens per parameter of the data-generating process (\autoref{sec:special-theory}).
  \item \textbf{A statistical mechanism for the in-context phase.} The in-context predictor is linear in the token counts, and a learner that estimates this map from data acquires it at a sample complexity set by its $K^2$ parameters and by the information that a context carries about the next token: both independent of $D$. Hence, comparison with the specialisation scale gives a task-diversity threshold above which (see~\autoref{fig:main}) \emph{the in-context solution emerges first, it remains stable in a window that widens with task diversity, then it gives way to the specialised solution} (\autoref{sec:icl-theory}).
  \item \textbf{Parameter-free agreement with trained transformers.} Across vocabularies, task diversities, sequence lengths and corpus sizes, transformer in-distribution losses collapse as functions of $NT/DK$, converging to the specialised solution at the predicted rate. Non-trivial ICL performance develops at the predicted, $D$-independent sample complexity, then vanishes again with specialisation (\autoref{sec:experiments}).
  \item \textbf{Separation from classical spectral methods.} Reconstructing the topics from low-order moments~\citep{hsu2012spectral,anandkumar2014tensor} requires resolving a Marchenko--Pastur bulk of eigenvalues, which takes $N\sim D^2K$ samples: a worse scaling with task diversity than the Bayes-optimal $NT\sim DK$ followed by trained transformers (\autoref{ssec:spectral}).
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Related work}\label{sec:related}

\paragraph{Mixtures of tasks as ICL testbeds.}
Finite task mixtures underlie most theoretical studies of ICL: linear regression~\citep{garg2022what,akyurek2023what,raventos2023pretraining,lu2025asymptotic,zhang2025training,bordelon2026theory,letey2026pretraintest}, classification~\citep{reddy2024mechanistic,nguyen2025differential}, and mixtures of Markov~\citep{edelman2024the,park2025competition,bondaschi2026from,gibson2026distinct} and hidden Markov~\citep{riechers2025next,ray2026nonergodic} models.
The ``Balls \& Urns'' setting of \citet{wurgaft2025incontext} is precisely the random topic model studied here, and their memorising and generalising predictors are our specialised and in-context ones.
Our work complements their phenomenological Bayesian framework: where they assume a scaling law and a low-Kolmogorov-complexity bias, we derive the sample complexities of the two predictors from first principles.
Thus, our approach does not require estimating Kolmogorov complexities and fitting parameters, but rather prescribes a simple picture where the functional simplicity of the ICL solution promotes its emergence as long as it leads to a noticeable loss gain with fewer data.

\paragraph{Representations, Belief-state geometry and task vectors.} Due to next-token pretraining, transformers linearly represent the belief state over latent structure~\citep{shai2024transformers}.
In particular, mixtures of independent components, or \emph{non-ergodic} sources, give rise to a telescopic geometry, which sparsifies along the context~\citep{ray2026nonergodic}.
The random topic model is the simplest non-ergodic source, and our results predict the sample dependence of the resulting belief geometry. 
Beyond controlled data structures, ICL has been linked to task vectors encoding the inferred task~\citep{hendel2023incontext}, whose geometry distinguishes dual modes of task inference corresponding to our two predictors~\citep{yan2026task}.
Overall, these approaches describe \emph{how} a trained network implements the chosen strategies; ours predicts \emph{when} the data favours each.

\paragraph{Learning theories of ICL.}
Most analytical studies of learning focus on the infinite-data and infinite-diversity limit.
\citet{lu2025asymptotic} is closest to us: they solve linear attention trained on Gaussian regression in a proportional asymptotic limit, obtaining sharp learning curves and a genuine transition with increasing task diversity.
However, since their linear model cannot fit the specialised predictors exactly, they did not observe the crossover from in-context to specialised predictor as the number of data increases.
Furthermore, our setup can be extended to mixtures of Markov chains~\citep{gibson2026distinct} and generic hidden Markov models~\citep{riechers2025next}.

\section{Data model and optimal predictors}\label{sec:rtm}

This section introduces our synthetic model of mixture data and the two reference predictors representing the \emph{specialised} and \emph{in-context solutions}.
The solutions are distinguished by their next-token-prediction loss profiles.
As shown in~\autoref{fig:id-vs-ood-profiles}, trained transformers behave like the specialised predictor at low data diversity, while converging to the in-context one at high diversity.
The rest of the paper explains why, and on which scale of the corpus size $N$ each solution is selected.

\paragraph{Random Topic Model}
A dataset is specified by a vocabulary size $K$, a number of topics $D$ and a document length $T$.
Each topic is a probability distribution over the vocabulary, $\mathbf{p}_z\in \Delta^{K-1}$ with entries $p_z(a)$ for $a\,{=}\,1,\dots,K$, drawn independently and uniformly from the simplex, $\mathbf{p}_z\sim\mathrm{Dir}_K(\mathbf{1})$ for $z\,{=}\,1,\dots,D$, and then held fixed.
We collect the topics as the columns of the topic matrix $P\,{=}\,[\mathbf{p}_1,\dots,\mathbf{p}_D]\in\mathbb{R}^{K\times D}$.
A document is generated by drawing a topic index $Z$ uniformly from $\lbrace 1,\dots,D\rbrace$, then $T$ tokens $X_1,\dots,X_T\in\lbrace 1,\dots,K\rbrace$ independently from $\mathbf{p}_Z$ (\autoref{fig:main}, left).
Given the topic, the likelihood of a context $X_{\leq t}\,{=}\,(X_1,\dots,X_t)$ depends only on its histogram, the count vector
\begin{align}
  C(X_{\leq t}) \in \mathbb{N}^{K},\qquad C_a(X_{\leq t})\,{:=}\,\#\lbrace s\,{\leq}\,t : X_s\,{=}\,a\rbrace.
\end{align}
The training corpus consists of $N$ documents, all generated with the same topic matrix $P$.

\paragraph{Data versus topic distribution.}
Two kinds of averages appear throughout.
$\mathbb{E}[\cdot]$ denotes the expectation over documents at fixed topic matrix $P$, i.e. over the topic index $Z$ and the tokens: this is the average that defines the loss of any predictor, including a trained transformer.
$\overline{\,\cdot\,}$ denotes the further average over the draw of the topics.
Unless stated otherwise, the curves in the figures are averaged over independent realisations of the topic matrix $P$.

\paragraph{Next-token prediction, In- and Out-Of-Distribution.}
We consider two test distributions.
In-distribution (ID) documents are generated as the training ones, from one of the $D$ topics of $P$.
Out-of-distribution (OOD) documents are generated from a fresh topic $\mathbf{p}_\star\sim\mathrm{Dir}_K(\mathbf{1})$, drawn from the same prior as the training topics but independently, and hence almost surely different from all of them.
A next-token predictor $\mathbf{f}$ maps each context $X_{\leq t}$ to a distribution $\mathbf{f}_t(X_{\leq t})\in\Delta^{K-1}$ over $X_{t\,{+}\,1}$. We measure ID performance by the next-token loss at context length $t$, or $t$-gram entropy
\begin{align}\label{eq:ntp-loss}
  \mathcal{L}_t[\mathbf{f}] := \mathbb{E}\left[ -\log f_t\!\left(X_{t+1}\mid X_{\leq t}\right) \right],
  \qquad t\,{=}\,0,\dots,T\,{-}\,1,
\end{align}
where the expectation is over fresh documents generated from the $D$ training topics.
The same expectation over a fresh topic $\mathbf{p}_\star$ defines the OOD loss $\mathcal{L}^\star_t[\mathbf{f}]$.
The usual autoregressive loss $\mathcal{L}_{\rm AR}$ is the average of $\mathcal{L}_t$ over positions.

\subsection{The specialised predictor}\label{ssec:specialised}

A learner that knows the topic matrix $P$ still does not observe the topic $Z$ of a given document, and must infer it from the context.
The Bayes-optimal next-token distribution is the posterior mixture of the topics,
\begin{align}\label{eq:specialised}
  \mathbf{q}_t(X_{\leq t}) := \sum_{z=1}^D \gamma_t(z)\,\mathbf{p}_z,\qquad \gamma_t(z) := \mathbb{P}\left( Z\,{=}\,z \mid X_{\leq t}\right)
  = \frac{\prod_{a=1}^K p_z(a)^{C_a}}{\sum_{y=1}^D \prod_{a=1}^K p_y(a)^{C_a}},
\end{align}
where the posterior over the topic $\gamma_t(z)$ depends on the context only through the counts.
$\mathbf{q}_t$ is the true conditional distribution of $X_{t+1}$ given the context, hence it minimises $\mathcal{L}_t$ over all predictors (\autoref{app:specialised}).
We call it \emph{specialised}, as it is built from the $D$ topics realised in training.

The cross-entropy loss splits into an entropy plus a Kullback--Leibler divergence,
\begin{align}\label{eq:specialised-loss}
  \mathcal{L}_t[\mathbf{q}] = \frac{1}{D}\sum_{z=1}^D H(\mathbf{p}_z) + \mathbb{E}\left[\KL\left(\mathbf{p}_Z \,\Vert\, \mathbf{q}_t\right)\right],
\end{align}
both averaged over the unknown topic.
The first term is the loss of an oracle that observes the topic, and no predictor can go below it: it is the \emph{entropy rate} of the data-generating process.
For sufficiently large $D$, the entropy rate converges to the average over draws of the topic matrix $\overline{H(\mathbf{p})}$, which is $\log K - 1 + \gamma_{\mathrm{E}} + O(1/K)$ for large $K$ (\autoref{app:topic-stats}), with $\gamma_{\mathrm{E}}\simeq 0.57721$ Euler's constant.
The second term is the excess loss due to the uncertainty on the topic and, by~\autoref{eq:specialised}, it is controlled by the posterior mass on the wrong topics $y\,{\neq}\,z$.
As shown in~\autoref{app:specialised}, the mass on a wrong topic $y$ decays exponentially in $t$, at a rate given by the divergence $\KL(\mathbf{p}_z\Vert\mathbf{p}_y)$.
For random topics this divergence is of order one (\autoref{app:topic-stats}), so that discriminating the true topic from the other $D\,{-}\,1$ requires a context of length $t\sim\log D$ (\autoref{app:specialised}).
This scale is visible in the left panel of~\autoref{fig:id-vs-ood-profiles}.

\subsection{The in-context predictor}\label{ssec:in-context}

Consider now an OOD document, generated from a new topic $\mathbf{p}_\star$ that does not belong to the topic matrix $P$.
A learner that knows the prior $\mathrm{Dir}_K(\mathbf{1})$ but not $\mathbf{p}_\star$ must infer from the context the topic itself, rather than its index within a known dictionary.
By Dirichlet categorical conjugacy, the posterior over $\mathbf{p}_\star$ given $X_{\leq t}$ is $\mathrm{Dir}_K(C_1{+}1,\dots,C_K{+}1)$, and the Bayes-optimal next-token distribution is its mean, the add-one smoothing of the histogram (\autoref{app:in-context})
\begin{align}\label{eq:in-context}
  r_t\!\left(a\mid X_{\leq t}\right) := \mathbb{P}\left( X_{t+1}\,{=}\,a \mid X_{\leq t}\right) = \frac{C_a(X_{\leq t}) + 1}{t + K}.
\end{align}
We call it \emph{in-context} as it does not depend on the topic matrix $P$, and thus applies unchanged to documents from seen or unseen topics.

The OOD loss of $\mathbf{r}_t$ decomposes as in~\autoref{eq:specialised-loss}, with the unseen topic in place of $\mathbf{p}_Z$,
\begin{align}\label{eq:in-context-loss}
  \mathcal{L}^\star_t[\mathbf{r}] = \overline{H(\mathbf{p})} + \mathbb{E}\left[\KL\left(\mathbf{p}_\star\,\Vert\,\mathbf{r}_t\right)\right]
  = \overline{H(\mathbf{p})} + \frac{K-1}{2t} + O\!\left(\frac{\log t}{t^2}\right),
\end{align}
where the expansion holds at fixed $K$ for $t\gg K$ (\autoref{app:in-context}).
Simply put, the histogram needs to include at least order $K$ tokens to depart from a trivial prediction $1/K$.
Note that, since $\mathbf{p}_\star$ is drawn from the same prior as the topics of $P$, the entropy floor is the same for the two predictors, while the approach is significantly different (see~\autoref{fig:id-vs-ood-profiles}, left).
Identifying one of $D$ known topics requires $\log D$ nats of evidence, which the context delivers at a constant rate per token, hence the exponential convergence of \autoref{ssec:specialised}.
Estimating a new topic is instead a $(K{-}1)$-dimensional problem, and the excess in~\autoref{eq:in-context-loss} is the classical half of the number of parameters per observation behaviour.
