\section{Learning-curve theory of specialisation}\label{sec:special-theory}

The training corpus contains only the $D$ topics in the matrix $P$.
Hence, a sufficiently expressive, well-optimised machine learning model \emph{must} converge to the specialised solution~\autoref{eq:specialised} in the infinite-data and training time limit.
But \emph{how many training documents does it take to specialise?}

\paragraph{Bayesian learning}
We answer this question with a Bayesian learner that uses the training corpus optimally:
It knows the model class, including $D$ and $K$, but not the realised topic matrix $P$, and observes a collection $\mathcal{C}_N$ of $N$ length-$T$ training documents, together with the test context.
The training corpus informs a posterior $\pi(dP|\mathcal{C}_N)$ over the topic matrix $P$, proportional, by Bayes' rule, to prior $\mu(dP)$ times the joint probability of the corpus under $P$, $\mathbb{P}\left\lbrace\mathcal{C}_N\right\rbrace$.
The Bayes-optimal learner is the posterior mean of the $P$-dependent specialised predictor from~\autoref{eq:specialised},
\begin{align}\label{eq:bayes-learner}
  \hat{\mathbf{q}}_t(X_{\leq t}) \propto \int \mathbf{q}_t(X_{\leq t}; P) \, \pi(dP|\mathcal{C}_N, X_{\leq t}),
\end{align}
where the posterior also includes the test context $X_{\leq t}$ (see~\autoref{app:bayes-learner} for details).
Note that, after averaging over the draw of the topics, no predictor built from the same data has a lower loss than $\hat{\mathbf{q}}$.
Its loss is the statistical limit of specialisation from $N$ training documents, hence a natural reference against which a transformer trained on the same corpus can be measured.

\paragraph{Learning curve and mutual information.}
The learning curve of specialisation is the excess loss of the Bayes-optimal learner over the specialised predictor, averaged over topic matrices,
\begin{align}\label{eq:learning-curve-def}
  \varepsilon_t(N) := \overline{\mathcal{L}_t[\hat{\mathbf{q}}] - \mathcal{L}_t[\mathbf{q}]}\,,
\end{align}
Averaging over the topics gives it an information-theoretic meaning: $\varepsilon_t(N)$ is the conditional mutual information between $P$ and the next token, given corpus and context (\autoref{app:bayes-learner}),
\begin{align}\label{eq:learning-curve-information}
  \varepsilon_t(N) = I(P;X_{t+1}\mid X_{\leq t}, \mathcal{C}_{N}) \Rightarrow \sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\varepsilon_t(N) = I(P;\mathcal{C}_{N+1}) - I(P;\mathcal{C}_N),
\end{align}
where $\mathcal{C}_{N+1}$ adds the test document to the corpus.
The excess loss of the learner on the test document equals the information that this document adds about the topic matrix.
Thus, the learning curve quantifies how fast the corpus reveals $P$.

\paragraph{The $DK/NT$ law.}
The topic matrix has $D(K{-}1)$ free parameters, and a corpus of $N$ documents reveals them at the rate of parametric statistics: under identifiability and regularity, $I(P;\mathcal{C}_N) = (D(K-1)/2)\log N + O(1)$~\citep{clarke1990information,clarke1994jeffreys}.
The increment gives the leading term of the learning curve at fixed $D$ and $K$ (\autoref{app:bayes-learner}),
\begin{align}\label{eq:dk-nt-law}
  \frac{1}{T}\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\varepsilon_t(N) = \frac{D(K-1)}{2NT} + o\!\left(N^{-1}\right).
\end{align}
Specialisation thus requires $NT\gg DK$, and the learning curve is a function of the number of training tokens per parameter, $x\,{:=}\,NT/(DK)$, decaying as $x^{-1}$.
As shown in~\autoref{sec:experiments}, trained transformers specialise on the same sample-complexity scale, and with the same decay.
Two remarks.
First, identifiability, i.e. that the law of a document determines $P$ up to a relabelling of the topics, requires a sufficiently long document and a \emph{generic} topic matrix.
For $D\leq K$, $T\,{\geq}\,3$ suffices: the columns of $P$ are linearly independent almost surely under the topics draw, and the third-order moment of the tokens has a unique decomposition with the topics as factors~\citep{kruskal1977threeway,anandkumar2014tensor}.
For larger $D$ the same holds for the lifted topics $\mathbf{p}_z^{\otimes m}$, which are generic in the same sense, i.e. linearly independent, as long as $D\leq\binom{K+m-1}{m}$, the dimension of the space of symmetric tensors, at the price of longer documents, $T\,{\geq}\,2m{+}1$ (\autoref{app:bayes-learner}).
For instance, pairs of tokens cover $K<D\leq K(K{+}1)/2$ with $T\,{\geq}\,5$.
Secondly, the learning curve is an average over topic matrices, whereas an experiment runs on individual realisations.
Comparing the two assumes that the excess loss of a given realisation is close to its average, i.e. typicality of the random topic matrix.
An empirical check of this typicality is the spread across independent realisations in \autoref{sec:experiments}.

\paragraph{The same law at fixed, large position.}
Equation~\ref{eq:dk-nt-law} concerns the loss averaged over positions; at a fixed position $t$ the same law follows from a simple picture.
Beyond the resolution scale $\log D$ of \autoref{ssec:specialised}, the learner can identify the topic of the test document, and likewise the topics of the training documents if $T\gg\log D$.
It is then left with estimating the token distribution of the test topic from the training documents of that topic, about $N/D$ of them, i.e. from $m\approx NT/D$ tokens: this is the problem solved by the in-context predictor of \autoref{ssec:in-context}, with $m$ in place of $t$, whose excess $(K-1)/(2m)$ becomes $D(K-1)/(2NT)$.
The $t$-gram loss at large $t$ therefore obeys the same law as the average, and \autoref{app:bayes-learner} makes this rigorous at fixed $P$, under conditions on $t$, $T$ and $N$; \autoref{fig:tgram-scaling} compares it with the $T$-gram learning curves of trained transformers.

\section{When does ICL emerge?}\label{sec:icl-theory}

\paragraph{Simplicity of the in-context predictor.}
If any learner eventually converges to the specialised predictor, \emph{when does ICL emerge?}
Both predictors of \autoref{sec:rtm} are functions of the counts, but of very different complexity.
The in-context predictor is linear up to normalisation,
\begin{align}\label{eq:add-one-linear}
  \mathbf{r}_t(X_{\leq t}) \propto \mathbf{C}(X_{\leq t}) + \mathbf{1},
\end{align}
whereas the specialised predictor is exponential in the counts (\autoref{eq:specialised}).
Then, simplicity bias suggests that the in-context predictor is easier to learn.
$\mathbf{r}_t$ represents a point in the family of $K^2$ linear maps from counts to next-token probability, whereas random topic models $P$ lie in $D(K\,{-}\,1)$ dimensions.
The estimation error follows from the same argument as \autoref{sec:special-theory}, in the form appropriate to a fitted parametric family rather than a Bayesian posterior (\autoref{app:icl}).
Simply put, a learner estimating the $K^2$ parameters of the linear map from a corpus of $NT$ tokens pays an excess $K^2/(2NT)$ over the optimal in-context solution, again proportional to the number of parameters per training token.

\paragraph{Estimation penalty versus loss gain.}
This excess must be compared with the gain of the add-one rule over the uniform predictor, equal to the mean mutual information between the next token and its context (\autoref{app:icl}),
\begin{align}\label{eq:icl-gain}
  G(K,T) \coloneq \log K-\frac1T\sum_{t<T}\mathcal{L}^\star_t[\mathbf{r}] = \frac{1}{T}\sum_{t=0}^{T-1} I\left(X_{t+1}; X_{\leq t}\right).
\end{align}
The new solution can only appear once the error due to finite data $K^2/(2NT)$ is smaller than the gain, i.e. for
\begin{align}\label{eq:n-icl}
  N \gtrsim N_{\mathrm{icl}} := \frac{K^2}{2T\,G(K,T)},
\end{align}
independently of the task diversity $D$.

\paragraph{Task-diversity threshold.}
By \autoref{eq:dk-nt-law}, the specialised solution emerges at $N_{\mathrm{spec}}\,{=}\,DK/T$.
Hence, if
\begin{align}\label{eq:diversity-threshold}
  D \gg \frac{K}{2\,G(K,T)},
\end{align}
then the in-context solution emerges before the specialised one, and persists in the window $(N_{\mathrm{icl}}, N_{\mathrm{spec}})$.
The resulting picture is that of \autoref{fig:main}, right.
In the $(N_{\mathrm{icl}}, N_{\mathrm{spec}})$ window the learner applies the same rule to seen and unseen topics alike, thus OOD and ID loss decay together towards the OOD optimum $\mathcal{L}^\star_t[\mathbf{r}]$.
At $N_{\mathrm{spec}}$, the learner resolves the dictionary: the ID loss descends to the entropy rate, and the OOD loss rises towards $\mathcal{L}^\star_t[\mathbf{q}]$---OOD loss of the specialised predictor.

\section{Transformer experiments}\label{sec:experiments}

This section collects empirical learning curves of one-block transformers trained with Adam on data generated from random topic models of varying $K$ and $D$.
Experiments with varying $T$ are reported in~\autoref{app:varying-T}.
Since we are testing the scaling of performance with the number of training tokens, we conducted extensive hyperparameter search, looking for the best configuration of model size, learning rate, batch size and training time at fixed depth and optimiser.
Further details of architectures and training algorithms are reported in~\autoref{app:experiments}.

\autoref{fig:learning-curve-scaling} shows that transformers learn random topic models at the same rate as the Bayesian learner of~\autoref{sec:special-theory}.
\begin{figure}[h!]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/learning-curve-scaling__T128__decay.pdf}
  \vspace{-10pt}
  \caption{
   \textbf{Transformers match the Bayes-optimal specialisation learning curve.}
   \textbf{Left:} Scaled ID autoregressive loss $(\mathcal{L}_{\rm AR}(N)-\mathcal{L}_{\rm AR}(\infty))/(\log{K}-\mathcal{L}_{\rm AR}(\infty))$ versus number of training sequences for one-block transformers trained on random topic model data with varying $(K,D)$, $T\,{=}\,128$.
   The scaled loss is $1$ at the uniform predictor $1/K$, $0$ at the specialised predictor.
   \textbf{Middle:} Rescaling the $x$-axis as the number of tokens per parameter of the data-generating process collapses all the curves onto a single functional form.
   \textbf{Right:} The decay towards $0$ (black dashed) shows remarkable agreement with the $x^{-1}$ rate of the Bayes-optimal learning curve.
  }
  \label{fig:learning-curve-scaling}
\end{figure}

Even above the task-diversity threshold~\autoref{eq:diversity-threshold}, specialisation kicks in as soon as the number of tokens $NT$ surpasses the number of parameters $DK$.
This is clearly shown by~\autoref{fig:ood-window}: the large-$N$ decay of the ID loss scales with $(NT/(DK))$, while the early-$N$ decay develops a $D$-independent behaviour.
Correspondingly, the OOD losses at early stopping (dashed lines) show an initial descent, followed by a steep ascent around $N\sim (DK)/T$.
This result shows that the transience of ICL is inevitable, unless the learning model is regularised.
