\subsection{Comparison with spectral baselines}\label{ssec:spectral}

Latent-variable models like the random topic model are classically learnt by spectral methods based on the moments of the tokens~\citep{hsu2012spectral,balle2014methods,anandkumar2014tensor}.
The first step is recovery of the $D$-dimensional subspace spanned by the topics from a low-order moment: the pair moment $M_2=\frac1D\sum_z\mathbf{p}_z\mathbf{p}_z^\top$, of rank $D$, when $D\leq K$, and the moment of $2m$ tokens when $D\leq\binom{K+m-1}{m}$, the number of independent products of $m$ topic entries.
The topics themselves then follow from the moment with one more token, which is the identifiability step of \autoref{sec:special-theory} (\autoref{app:spectral}).
The subspace is resolved once the empirical moment, estimated from $N$ independent $2m$-tuples, is close to the population moment.
The corresponding sample size is a lower bound for the method to work.

The error on the empirical moment is of order $(K^m N)^{-1/2}$ by the matrix Bernstein inequality (\autoref{app:spectral}, verified numerically in~\autoref{fig:M2-convergence}).
The scales of the population moments, instead, follow from random matrix theory, in the limit $D, K\to\infty$.
With $D/K\,{=}\,\alpha<1$ and $m\,{=}\,1$, the bulk of $M_2$'s spectrum converges to a Marchenko--Pastur distribution with eigenvalues of order $1/(DK)$ (\autoref{fig:M2-spectrum}).
Comparison with the noise gives $N \gtrsim D^2 K$.
With $D/(K(K+1)/2)\,{=}\,\beta<1$ and $m\,{=}\,2$, the relevant bulk of $M_4$ is of order $1/(DK^2)$ (\autoref{fig:M4-spectrum}).
Comparison with the noise gives $N \gtrsim D^2 K^2$.
This analysis is carried out in a different limit from the rest of the paper, and it counts independent tuples rather than documents of $T$ tokens, so we only compare the dependence on task diversity:
quadratic in $D$ for the spectral baseline, against the linear $NT\sim DK$ of the Bayes-optimal learner, which the trained transformers follow.
Furthermore, in the spectral baseline, the order of $K$ grows with the order of the required moment.

\section{Conclusions}

We have studied a solvable model of a mixture of $D$ topics, where two strategies compete: in-distribution specialisation to the finite mixture, or out-of-distribution generalisation to topics never seen in training.
Specialisation requires more training tokens than there are parameters in the data-generating process---a statistical limit that no learner can beat.
The in-context solution is available at a $D$-independent scale, hence it comes first whenever the number of topics is large enough.
Trained transformers follow both scales.
It follows that the transience of ICL~\citep{singh2023transient} is inevitable as the corpus grows, unless a constraint on parameters or training budget keeps the learner in the in-context window.

The specialised solution was called \emph{memorising} in previous studies of the same competition~\citep{raventos2023pretraining,nguyen2025differential,carroll2025dynamics,wurgaft2025incontext}.
Indeed, its placement after the in-context phase mirrors the training dynamics of diffusion models, where generalisation is acquired before the memorisation of the training set~\citep{favero2025bigger}.
We find the label misleading: the specialised predictor stores the $D$ topics of the population, not the $N$ documents of the corpus---memorisation happens in \emph{feature} space.
This is a form of in-distribution generalisation, distinct from the adaptation to new topics of the in-context predictor, and we name it after the specialisation of teacher-student networks, where the hidden units of the student align with those of the teacher~\citep{goldt2019dynamics, barbier2025optimal}.

Several directions are open.
The first is the training dynamics of transformers on the random topic model, which would connect our sample complexities with the phenomenological description of~\citet{wurgaft2025incontext}.
More importantly, the dynamics determine which representations are learned in each phase and the corresponding geometry of the residual stream~\citep{shai2024transformers,ray2026nonergodic}.
Clarifying the distinction between representations in the two phases could help understand the emergence and role of task vectors~\citep{hendel2023incontext}, including scenarios where multiple solutions coexist~\citep{yan2026task}.
The second is the competition between task diversity and other structures of the data.
The tools developed here can address this problem in general non-ergodic hidden Markov models~\citep{riechers2025next}, of which the random topic model is the simplest instance.
Such mixtures are natural models of large heterogeneous corpora.
Solvable models like the one proposed here could help understand how heterogeneity contributes to data statistics and scaling laws~\citep{cagnetta2026deriving}, and how improvements on the individual components of a mixture interact.


