\begin{abstract}
\noindent
Two documents can be close because they cite the same works or because they write
about the same things, and the two kinds of proximity often disagree. Science
mapping reads them in separate maps or merges them, as hybrid clustering does;
either way the disagreement is lost. We introduce multiplex coupling, a
framework that couples the same documents by their references and by their
terms, keeps the two layers separate and analyses their relation. Similarities are standardised against a null model, and the agreement
between the layers is measured on random pairs of documents. Consensus clustering
partitions each layer into roots, groups sharing an intellectual base, and
themes, groups sharing a topic. A test on the roots $\times$ themes table
identifies branching roots and convergent themes, and pairs of roots are followed
over time. Every structure is reported with its persistence across numbers of
neighbours, which on synthetic data identifies most false branching.
Applications to Web of Science, Scopus and OpenAlex collections show that the
two layers carry largely complementary information, and reveal substantive and
methodological bases that have grown into several topics. The framework is
implemented in \textit{bibliometrix}.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} bibliographic coupling, science mapping, multiplex networks,
research fronts, thematic analysis, bibliometrix

