\subsection{Few Steps Suffice for High Posterior Sample and Density Quality}
\label{sec:exp1}

\paragraph{Setup and baseline.}
The two moons task has a two-dimensional parameter and observation, a bimodal crescent posterior, and an exact likelihood and posterior sampler \citep{greenberg2019automatic, lueckmann2021benchmarking}.
All methods learn the \emph{posterior} direction and are sampled directly with 5{,}000 draws per dataset, which we score against 32{,}000 exact reference draws.
We compare a conditional mean flow at step counts $S \in \{1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32\}$ with three baselines: a conditional normalizing flow, a conditional consistency model as the established few-step method \citep{song2023consistency, schmitt2024consistency}, and a separately trained conditional flow matching model integrated with $S$ Euler steps or an adaptive Tsit5 solver \citep{tsitouras2011runge--kutta}.
We integrate the velocity head of the mean flow with the same Euler or adaptive scheme, which repurposes the mean flow as a flow matching model.
For both the consistency model and the mean flow, we tuned the sampling schedule for each step count on held-out observations, which are disjoint from the evaluation datasets.

\paragraph{Sample quality.}
The conditional mean flow reaches a similar C2ST at every step count (\autoref{fig:overview} and \autoref{fig:exp1-steps}\refsubfigure{A}), so a single step suffices for sampling and already outperforms the normalizing flow ($0.523$ against $0.543$).
In contrast, the consistency model needs at least 4 steps to approach this accuracy, and both flow matching variants need at least 32 Euler steps.
The adaptive solver further improves the accuracy, but requires more than 300 solver steps.
Hence, the mean flow with $S{=}1$ draws 5{,}000 samples about 23 times faster than flow matching with 32 Euler steps and 140 times faster than with the adaptive solver (\autoref{fig:runtimes}).
It is also about 6 times faster than the normalizing flow and only reaches its sampling time at $S{=}8$.

Since the velocity head of the mean flow and the separately trained model perform similarly under both solvers, mean flow training does not seem to degrade the learned velocity field.
The consistency model is also sensitive to its discretization exponent, whose candidates span a C2ST from $0.54$ to $0.98$ at $S{=}4$, whereas the EDM schedule of the mean flow changes the C2ST by at most $0.015$~(\autoref{tab:schedule}).

\paragraph{Density quality.}
In contrast to sampling, we find that additional mean flow steps improve the density, and the Hellinger distance to the exact posterior decreases with $S$ (\autoref{fig:exp1-steps}\refsubfigure{B} and \autoref{tab:exp1-density}).
With $S{=}1$, the mean flow places mass between the two modes (\autoref{fig:overview}\refsubfigure{C}), but it still outperforms the normalizing flow.
Euler integration of either velocity field remains less accurate than the mean flow with $S{\ge}2$ up to 32 steps.
The adaptive solver is most accurate, with the velocity head of the mean flow ahead of the separately trained flow matching model.

Moreover, the mean flow density is close to normalized, whereas the Euler densities overshoot unit mass by many orders of magnitude at $S{=}1$ and remain far from normalized at $S{=}32$ (\autoref{tab:exp1-density}).
We attribute this to the Euler scheme approximating both the trajectory and the divergence integral in \eqref{eq:cnf}, whereas the mean flow computes the exact Jacobian determinant of each step.
The normalizing flow is exactly normalized, but its density shows fold artifacts and places mass outside the prior support~(\autoref{fig:overview}\refsubfigure{A}).
Flow matching models with adaptive solvers provide the most accurate densities, but more than 300 steps per evaluation make them too expensive for repeated density evaluation.

\subsection{Single-Step Likelihoods Yield Error Comparable to 200-Step Flow Matching}
\label{sec:exp2}

\paragraph{Setup and baseline.}
The cognitive model describes two-alternative decisions as a Wiener first-passage process with drift $\nu$, boundary separation $a$ and non-decision time $t_0$ \citep{ratcliff2016diffusion}, and is a standard benchmark for learned single-trial likelihoods \citep{fengler2021likelihood}.
We train one conditional mean flow on the signed response time $y = (2c-1)\,t$, with choice $c \in \{0, 1\}$ and response time $t$, and compose the learned trial likelihood into joint posteriors over $N \in \{4, 16, 64, 256\}$ trials, which we sample with NUTS.
Since a response cannot be faster than $t_0$, we truncate the prior of $t_0$ at the fastest observed response time of each run.
We compare mean flow step counts $S \in \{1, 2, 4, 8\}$ with a flow matching model integrated with 200 Euler steps.

\paragraph{Results.}
With $S{=}1$, the NRMSE and posterior contraction of the mean flow are comparable to the flow matching baseline at every $N$ (\autoref{fig:exp4}, \autoref{tab:exp4} and \autoref{fig:exp4-posteriors}), while sampling is 182 to $216\times$ faster (\autoref{fig:runtimes}\refsubfigure{D}).
Additional steps increase the cost and the number of convergence failures without clearly reducing the parameter error (\autoref{fig:exp4-steps}).
From all MCMC runs, $8.5\%$ failed to converge, and all failed runs used $S \ge 4$ (\autoref{tab:exp4}).
We attribute this to the fact that the mean flow is trained for few-step sampling and evaluation only, and hence the intermediate maps might be poorly trained and additional steps, unlike in the discretization of an ODE, need not reduce the approximation error.

However, for every method the coverage of the 95\% central interval decreases and the worst posterior predictive discrepancy increases with $N$ (\autoref{fig:exp4}\refsubfigure{B}).
Composing many approximate likelihoods accumulates their errors while the posterior contracts, which makes even the 200-step baseline overconfident.
With $S{=}1$, the additional discretization error further lowers the coverage at $N{=}256$, whereas with $S{=}2$ coverage and predictive discrepancy are comparable to the baseline.

