        NRMSE & $\mathbf{0.187}$ (0.008) & 0.199 (0.009) \\
        RMSE $\tilde{y}_t$ & $\mathbf{0.055}$ (0.007) & 0.159 (0.036) \\
        RMSE $y_t$ & $\mathbf{0.060}$ (0.013) & 0.125 (0.031) \\
        ESS & $\mathbf{4{,}234}$ (247)	& 2{,}700 (629) \\
        Leapfrog steps & $\mathbf{121}$ (4) & 480 (57) \\
        Wall time (s) & 36{,}146 (6{,}157) & $\mathbf{1{,}624}$ (285) \\
        \bottomrule
    \end{tabular}
\end{table}


\paragraph{Setup and baseline.}
Since conditional mean flows do not constrain the network architecture, we can tailor the backbone to observations with missing entries.
We consider a stochastic renewal process, a standard forward model for estimating time-varying reproduction numbers $R_t$ \citep{fraser2007estimating, cori2013new, gostic2020practical}, in which the incidence is driven by a generation-interval force (Appendix~\ref{app:task-renewal} and \autoref{fig:exp3-scenario}).
The parameter is the 128-dimensional coefficient vector $\z$ with $\log R = L\z$, where $L$ is the Cholesky factor of a squared-exponential kernel \citep{rasmussen2005gaussian}, such that the prior is $\normal(\0, \I)$.
We withhold 30\% of the observed steps completely at random and redraw this pattern for each dataset.
Under this mechanism, inference only requires the marginal likelihood $p(\y_{\mathcal{O}} \given \z)$ of the observed entries $\mathcal{O}$ \citep{little2019statistical}.
We parameterize the mean flow with a masked transformer trained with missing coordinates and a causal mask over time.
In contrast, the normalizing flow has a fixed input width, so we train and evaluate it with a value of $-1$ in the missing entries (\autoref{fig:exp3-scenario}\refsubfigure{C}).
Both models are trained on a low budget of 8{,}192 simulations, and the mean flow is evaluated with $S{=}1$.

\paragraph{Posterior accuracy and sampling cost.}
Both methods converge on every dataset without divergent transitions.
The conditional mean flow with the masked transformer has a lower NRMSE to the generating coefficients than the normalizing flow, its posterior draws reproduce the observed $y_t$ more closely under the learned likelihood and the simulator (\autoref{tab:exp3}), and its ESS per network evaluation is six times higher.
However, each evaluation of the transformer is computationally more costly, such that the normalizing flow finishes sampling $22\times$ sooner, despite requiring more leapfrog steps inside NUTS.

\paragraph{Posterior predictive.}
Simulations from the learned likelihoods at posterior draws reveal the problem of sentinel values (\autoref{fig:exp3_prediction}).
The predictive bands of the mean flow contract around the observed data at every observed time point.
In contrast, the normalizing flow frequently generates the sentinel value $-1$ also at observed time points, which is reflected in a higher predictive RMSE~(\autoref{tab:exp3}).

In summary, the flexible architecture of mean flows allows a masked transformer to marginalize missing observations.
This yields a lower posterior error than a normalizing flow with sentinel values, but at a higher cost per density evaluation.


\subsection{More Posterior Draws Are Better Than More Flow Steps}
\label{sec:exp4}

\begin{figure*}[t]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/exp2_budget}
    \caption{\emph{Budget-matched posterior accuracy on g-and-k.}
    (\textbf{A})~C2ST against the exact posterior over mean flow steps $S$, at the shared budget of Appendix~\ref{app:samplers}.
    (\textbf{B})~Minimum bulk ESS per 1{,}000 network evaluations.
    (\textbf{C})~Draws kept per chain under the shared budget.
    Lines show the mean over 10 independent datasets, bands the standard deviation; the delayed acceptance MCMC line is indexed by its coarse step count, and horizontal lines mark the normalizing flow and the flow matching model with 200 Euler steps outside the budget.}
    \label{fig:exp2-budget}
\end{figure*}

\paragraph{Setup and baseline.}
The g-and-k distribution is a standard benchmark with an intractable likelihood, defined through its quantile function \citep{rayner2002numerical, drovandi2011likelihood-free}.
We use a bivariate version with a Gaussian copula and parameters $(g, k, \rho)$, and datasets of $N{=}50$ observations (Appendix~\ref{app:task-gnk}).
The conditional mean flow learns the likelihood of a single observation, and we sample the posterior with NUTS.
To assess whether a computational budget should be spent on more mean flow steps or on more posterior draws, we fix the product of requested draws per chain and network evaluations per density (\autoref{app:samplers}).
Under this budget, we compare mean flow step counts $S \in \{1, 2, 4, 8\}$ with a normalizing flow.
In addition, we evaluate delayed acceptance MCMC, which screens proposals with the mean flow at $S_c$ steps and accepts them against a 100-step Tsit5 evaluation of the velocity head of the same network (\autoref{sec:sampling} and \autoref{app:samplers}).
A separately trained flow matching model integrated with 200 Euler steps serves as a baseline outside the budget, with 28 times more network evaluations.

\paragraph{Steps against draws.}
All mean flow settings converge on every dataset and reach a similar accuracy, with the lowest mean C2ST at $S{=}4$, although these differences are smaller than the spread across datasets (\autoref{fig:exp2-budget}\refsubfigure{A}, \autoref{tab:exp2}).
Fewer steps permit longer chains, and the minimum bulk ESS per network evaluation drops by an order of magnitude from $S{=}1$ to $S{=}8$ (\autoref{fig:exp2-budget}\refsubfigure{B--C}).
Hence, beyond $S{=}4$, a more accurate likelihood does not compensate for fewer posterior draws, and in practice, we recommend $S{=}2$ for this benchmark.
Under the same budget, \textit{the normalizing flow has a higher C2ST than every mean flow setting} and requires more than four times the wall time of the mean flow ($S{=}2$; \autoref{fig:runtimes}\refsubfigure{B}).
Regular flow matching also has a higher C2ST despite requiring more evaluations, and the same mean flow is about $50\times$ faster.

\paragraph{Delayed acceptance MCMC.}
Screening proposals with a coarse mean flow and accepting them against the fine evaluation of the same network gives an accuracy comparable to NUTS (\autoref{tab:exp2}).
With the coarse acceptance rate adapted to $0.3$, the fine level accepts 80 to 85\% of the evaluated proposals, rising with $S_c$.
Delayed acceptance MCMC is about 26--37 times faster than the flow matching baseline, but slower than NUTS with the mean flow likelihoods and yields a lower ESS.

In summary, a few mean flow steps with more posterior draws give a lower posterior error than both the normalizing flow and the flow matching baseline, at lower wall time.
Delayed acceptance MCMC with the same network gives comparable accuracy without gradients.

\section{DISCUSSION}
\label{sec:discussion}

Neural density estimators for SBI have so far traded flexibility for fast density evaluation. Conditional mean flows relax this trade-off by allowing the step count to be chosen after training. In low-dimensional benchmarks, one or two steps matched the accuracy of flow matching while reducing MCMC time by up to two orders of magnitude. Few steps also yielded accurate posterior samples, and under a fixed MCMC budget, more draws were more valuable than more steps.

\textbf{Limitations.} The main limitation is the cost of density evaluation in high dimensions.
Each step requires its full Jacobian and a log-determinant, whose cost grows cubically with the dimension, and differentiating the log-determinant for NUTS involves second-order derivatives.
Together with the higher cost of each transformer evaluation, this made MCMC in the renewal application slower than with the normalizing flow despite using fewer network evaluations.
In addition, the cycle loss encourages but does not guarantee invertibility, so the learned densities may not exactly be normalized.
Finally, the coverage of every method, including flow matching with 200 steps, decreased at large trial counts, consistent with error accumulation.

\textbf{Outlook.} Beyond MCMC, fast mean flow densities can support self-consistency objectives \citep{schmitt2024leveraging} and, together with prior and likelihood evaluations, evidence estimation \citep{kucharsky2026improving}.
Since these methods also rely on repeated density evaluation, our work could make flexible flow-based estimators practical in any SBI setting that requires densities.

