\section{Proofs}
\label{app:proofs}

\subsection{Conditional Mean Flows}
\label{app:conditional}

Conditioning reduces the main population objective to an unconditional mean flow objective for each conditional target, as in conditional diffusion and flow matching \citep{vincent2011connection, batzolis2021conditional, arruda2025diffusion}.
We write $\Psi^{\x}_{s\to t}$ for the ODE flow of $\v^*$ and $\u^*$ for its average velocity, defined by $\Psi^{\x}_{s\to t}(\z)=\z+(t-s)\u^*(\z,s,t,\x)$, with diagonal value $\u^*(\z,s,s,\x)=\v^*(\z,s,\x)$.

\paragraph{Assumptions.}
\begin{enumerate}[label=(A\arabic*)]
    \item \emph{Noise.} $\epsilonb\sim\normal(\0,\I)$ is independent of $(\z_0,\x)$, and $\E\|\z_0\|^2<\infty$.
    \item \emph{Time pairs.} $(s,t)$ is independent of $(\z_0,\x,\epsilonb)$, and its distribution has an absolutely continuous component whose density is strictly positive on $(0,1)^2$.
    \item \emph{Regularity.} For $p(\x)$-almost every $\x$, the conditional target $p(\z_0\given\x)$ has a density, and $\v^*(\cdot,\cdot,\x)$ is continuously differentiable in state and time and globally Lipschitz in the state, uniformly over $[0,1]$.
    \item \emph{Realizability.} The admissible pairs $(\u_{\psib},\hat{\v}_{\psib})$ are jointly measurable in $\x$, continuously differentiable in state and time, and have finite population loss, and they contain the exact pair $(\u^*,\v^*)$ simultaneously for all conditions.
\end{enumerate}
(A3) gives a global continuously differentiable flow through both endpoints.
For targets with compact support or discontinuous densities, such as the uniform priors in two moons, $\v^*$ is in general not Lipschitz near $t{=}0$.
For such targets, the argument below applies on $[\delta, 1]$ for every $\delta > 0$ and, for schedules starting at $t_0=\delta$, yields the density of the slightly smoothed target $\z_\delta = (1-\delta)\z_0 + \delta\epsilonb$, which converges to the target as $\delta \to 0$.
In practice, we do not truncate the schedule and keep $t_0 = 0$, since $s = 0$ is almost surely never sampled, and the network's value at $s = 0$ is the continuous extension of the fitted values at small $s$.

{\renewcommand{\theproposition}{\ref{prop:conditional}}\addtocounter{proposition}{-1}
\begin{proposition}[Conditional mean flows]
Assume (A1)--(A4), and let $\loss_{\mathrm{MF}}$ be unweighted ($w{=}1$).
Then every population minimizer satisfies, for $p(\x)$-almost every $\x$,
\begin{equation*}
    \hat{\v}_{\psib}(\z,s,\x)=\v^*(\z,s,\x)
    \quad\text{and}\quad
    \u_{\psib}(\z,s,t,\x)=\u^*(\z,s,t,\x)
    \qquad\text{for all } \z\in\R^d,\ s,t\in[0,1].
\end{equation*}
Consequently, for every schedule $0=t_0<\dots<t_S=1$, the composed map $T$ equals $\Psi^{\x}_{0\to1}$, a diffeomorphism that transports $p(\z_0\given\x)$ to $\normal(\0,\I)$, and \eqref{eq:composed-density} equals $\log p(\z\given\x)$.
\end{proposition}}

\begin{proof}[Proof of Proposition~\ref{prop:conditional}]
Let $\mathbf Y=\epsilonb-\z_0$ and $\mathbf m=\v^*(\z_s,s,\x)$.
By (A1) and (A2), $\E[\mathbf Y\given\z_s,s,t,\x]=\mathbf m$, and (A1) ensures that all terms are finite.
Both predictions in the main loss are functions of these inputs, so the conditional-expectation decomposition gives
\begin{equation*}
    \loss_{\mathrm{MF}}
    =\big(1+\tfrac{1}{d}\big)\E\|\mathbf Y-\mathbf m\|^2
     +\E\|\mathbf V_{\psib}-\mathbf m\|^2
     +\tfrac{1}{d}\E\|\hat{\v}_{\psib}-\mathbf m\|^2.
\end{equation*}
By (A4) and the MeanFlow identity, the exact pair $(\u^*,\v^*)$ makes the last two terms zero.
Every minimizer therefore satisfies $\hat{\v}_{\psib}=\mathbf V_{\psib}=\v^*$ almost everywhere under the training distribution.
For almost every condition, the Gaussian interpolation has full support at interior times.
Together with the time-pair density and continuity, this extends the equalities to all states and times.

Fix such a condition and a trajectory $\z_\tau=\Psi^{\x}_{s\to\tau}(\z)$.
Since $\hat{\v}_{\psib}=\v^*$, the total derivative in $\mathbf V_{\psib}$ is taken along this trajectory, and for fixed $t$,
\begin{equation*}
    \frac{\mathrm d}{\mathrm d\tau}\flowmap(\z_\tau;\tau,t,\x)
    =\v^*(\z_\tau,\tau,\x)-\mathbf V_{\psib}(\z_\tau,\tau,t,\x)=\0.
\end{equation*}
Since $\flowmap(\z_t;t,t,\x)=\z_t$, we obtain $\flowmap=\Psi^{\x}$ and hence $\u_{\psib}=\u^*$, with the diagonal identity following by continuity.
Since $\z_1=\epsilonb$, the flow of $\v^*$ transports $p(\z_0\given\x)$ to $\normal(\0,\I)$ under (A3) \citep{lipman2023flow, liu2022flow}.
Flow composition gives $T=\Psi^{\x}_{0\to1}$ for every schedule, and change of variables gives \eqref{eq:composed-density}.
\end{proof}


The exact flow map satisfies $\Psi^{\x}_{t\to s}\circ\Psi^{\x}_{s\to t}=\mathrm{id}$ for every condition, including swapped ones, so the exact pair also attains the minimum $\loss_{\mathrm{cyc}}=0$.
Hence, adding $\lambda\,\loss_{\mathrm{cyc}}$ does not change the set of population minimizers.
For $p > 0$, the weight $w$ depends on the regression target $\mathbf Y$ through the residual, and the decomposition above no longer applies.
The adaptive weight thus acts as a robust loss that down-weights large residuals \citep{geng2025mean}, and in general changes the learned velocity, but empirically seems to stabilize training (see \autoref{tab:hyperparameters}).


\subsection{Marginalization by Masking}
\label{app:marginal}

We show that the masked objective of \autoref{sec:backbone} targets the marginal distribution of the retained target coordinates.
For a set $\mathcal{O} \subseteq \{1, \dots, d\}$, we write $\z_{\mathcal{O}}$ for the retained coordinates of $\z$, and $\u_{\psib,\mathcal{O}}$ and $\hat{\v}_{\psib,\mathcal{O}}$ for the outputs of the network on $\mathcal{O}$.

{\renewcommand{\theproposition}{\ref{prop:marginal}}\addtocounter{proposition}{-1}
\begin{proposition}[Marginalization by masking]
Let the mask $\mathcal O\subseteq\{1,\dots,d\}$ be nonempty with law $\pi$, independent of $(\z_0,\x,\epsilonb,s,t)$, let the network outputs on $\mathcal O$ depend on the target only through $\z_{\mathcal O}$, and let the unweighted loss be restricted to the coordinates in $\mathcal O$.
Assume (A1)--(A3) for each marginal target $\z_{0,\mathcal O}$ with $\pi(\mathcal O)>0$, and (A4) simultaneously for all such masks.
Then every population minimizer satisfies, for every $\mathcal O$ with $\pi(\mathcal O)>0$ and $p(\x)$-almost every $\x$, $\hat{\v}_{\psib,\mathcal O}=\v^*_{\mathcal O}$ and $\u_{\psib,\mathcal O}=\u^*_{\mathcal O}$, where $\u^*_{\mathcal O}$ is the average velocity of
\begin{equation*}
    \v^*_{\mathcal O}(\z_{\mathcal O},s,\x)
    =\E[\epsilonb_{\mathcal O}-\z_{0,\mathcal O}
          \given\z_{s,\mathcal O}=\z_{\mathcal O},\x].
\end{equation*}
Consequently, \eqref{eq:composed-density}, with the base density and Jacobians evaluated in $|\mathcal O|$ dimensions, equals $\log p(\z_{\mathcal O}\given\x)$ for every schedule.
\end{proposition}}

\begin{proof}[Proof of Proposition~\ref{prop:marginal}]
For a fixed mask, independence leaves the simulation law unchanged, and the coordinate-wise interpolation is
\begin{equation*}
    \z_{s,\mathcal O}=(1-s)\z_{0,\mathcal O}+s\epsilonb_{\mathcal O},
    \qquad\epsilonb_{\mathcal O}\sim\normal(\0,\I_{|\mathcal O|}).
\end{equation*}
The masked main loss is therefore a positively weighted sum of the conditional mean flow objectives for the marginal targets.
By the simultaneous realizability in (A4), the exact pairs attain every summand's lower bound, so every minimizer attains each one.
Proposition~\ref{prop:conditional} applied to $\z_{0,\mathcal O}$ gives the result.
\end{proof}

Additional attention restrictions are covered only when they preserve the ability to represent $\v^*_{\mathcal O}$ and its average velocity.
The independence of the synthetic training mask holds by construction and does not impose a missingness mechanism on the observed data.
The marginal density uses only the observed coordinates.
For $p > 0$, the residual and hence $w$ depend only on the retained coordinates, so the reduction to the marginal targets still holds, but each summand then has the weighted minimizer discussed in Appendix~\ref{app:conditional}.

