\begin{abstract}
Reliable uncertainty quantification is central to modern forecasting.
Conformal prediction offers model-agnostic intervals with coverage
guarantee under exchangeability, but existing software often targets
general prediction problems or individual time series procedures rather
than integrated workflows for multistep forecasting applications. We
introduce \textbf{conformalForecast}, an open-source R package for
constructing, updating, and evaluating conformal prediction intervals
for multistep time series forecasts. The package separates point
forecasting from calibration, accepts forecasts from diverse sources
through a common interface, and preserves horizon-specific forecast
errors and calibration states. It implements several conformal
calibration schemes spanning empirical-quantile, feedback-based, and
dependence-aware approaches, together with tools for assessing coverage,
interval width, and interval scores over time and across horizons.
Fitted objects can be extended as new observations arrive while
retaining previously issued intervals and avoiding recomputation at
earlier forecast origins. An application to short-term electricity price
forecasting across five Australian regions illustrates the workflow.
Aggregate coverage is generally close to the nominal level, while
rolling coverage and interval efficiency vary across methods and
horizons. In the reported experiment, incremental updating reproduces
full-reconstruction intervals under identical forecasting and
calibration settings while substantially reducing computation.
\textbf{conformalForecast} provides a reproducible and extensible
framework linking conformal methodology with sequential forecasting
practice.
\end{abstract}

