\section{Introduction}\label{sec-intro}

Forecasting requires not only accurate point predictions but also
reliable quantification of their uncertainty. In short-term wholesale
electricity markets, for example, a nominal 90\% price prediction
interval supports hedging and operational planning only if it is well
calibrated, with empirical coverage close to 90\%, and sharp enough to
distinguish different levels of price risk. Conformal prediction
\citep{papadopoulos2002, vovk2005} provides a general framework for
constructing such intervals. Under exchangeability, it offers
finite-sample, distribution-free marginal coverage guarantees
\citep{shafer2008, lei2018}, and it can be applied on top of any point
forecast, whether from a statistical/machine learning model, or a
judgmental process, without specifying a predictive distribution.

Time series pose a fundamental challenge for this framework. Serial
dependence, nonstationarity, and distributional shifts violate
exchangeability, so the classical guarantee need not hold. One line of
research extends conformal prediction beyond exchangeability under
additional conditions on the form of the shift or dependence
\citep{tibshirani2019, chernozhukov2018, xu2021, barber2023}. A
complementary line adapts calibration online as observations arrive,
without modeling how the distribution evolves. Adaptive conformal
inference adjusts the miscoverage level in response to past coverage
errors \citep{gibbs2021}, and later developments include expert
aggregation \citep{zaffran2022}, adaptive regret minimization
\citep{bhatnagar2023}, dynamically tuned step sizes \citep{gibbs2024},
PID-based threshold updates \citep{angelopoulos2023}, and multistep
forecasting with dependence across horizons \citep{wang2024}. These
methods turn calibration into a sequential task, and this sequential
structure makes their implementation a forecasting-specific software
problem.

Forecasting practice imposes requirements that extend beyond a generic
regression interface. Forecasts are issued repeatedly as the forecast
origin advances, often at several horizons, and their errors become
observable only as outcomes are realized. A conformal forecasting
workflow should therefore (i) accept point forecasts from any source,
(ii) preserve the joint origin--horizon structure of the forecast
errors, (iii) update calibration as new observations arrive without
repeating earlier computations, and (iv) evaluate interval performance
over time and across horizons.

Existing open-source software meets these requirements only in part.
General-purpose tools such as \textbf{pintervals}
\citep{randahl2026pintervals}, \textbf{marginaleffects}
\citep{arel-bundock2024}, and \textbf{probably} \citep{kuhn2025probably}
within \textbf{tidymodels} workflows \citep{kuhn2020tidymodels} in R;
\textbf{crepes} \citep{bostrom2024}, \textbf{puncc} \citep{mendil2023},
and \textbf{TorchCP} \citep{huang2025} in Python; and
\textbf{ConformalPrediction.jl} \citep{altmeyer2022} in Julia, target
regression and classification problems. Other R packages address
specific model classes or response structures, including
\textbf{conformalbayes} \citep{mccartan2025} for Bayesian models,
\textbf{conformalInference.fd} \citep{diquigiovanni2022fd} and
\textbf{conformalInference.multi} \citep{diquigiovanni2025multi} for
functional and multivariate responses, and \textbf{LCP} \citep{guan2023}
for localized conformal prediction. A small number of packages address
the time series framework. In R, \textbf{modeltime}
\citep{dancho2026modeltime} adds Gaussian-residual and split-conformal
intervals to \textbf{tidymodels} workflows, whereas
\textbf{caretForecast} \citep{akay2026} constructs horizon-specific
intervals by rolling-origin calibration. In Python, \textbf{sktime}
\citep{sktime2026} wraps arbitrary forecasters with empirical and
conformal intervals. \textbf{StatsForecast}
\citep{garza2022statsforecast} and \textbf{MLForecast}
\citep{morales2026mlforecast} calibrate multistep intervals for
statistical and machine-learning forecasters, respectively, using
rolling-origin residuals. \textbf{MAPIE} \citep{cordier2023} implements
EnbPI and adaptive conformal inference through a dedicated time series
regressor, while \textbf{puncc} and \textbf{crepes} also provide
sequential procedures. Few of these combine the four requirements within
a single model-agnostic workflow, so applying and comparing conformal
methods across forecasting models and horizons still requires custom
code.

This paper presents \textbf{conformalForecast}, an open-source R package
whose design follows from these four requirements. Point forecasts may
come from many sources, including operational systems outside R, so
calibration is separated from forecasting and accepts any function that
returns a \texttt{forecast} object. Forecasts issued at successive
origins are stored with their errors in a common origin--horizon
representation that respects the horizon-dependent delay with which
errors become observable; five calibration schemes, including the
multistep approach of \citet{wang2024}, operate on it through a single
interface. As new observations arrive, fitted objects are extended
rather than rebuilt: with the forecasting function and calibration
settings unchanged, updating reproduces the intervals of a full
reconstruction and leaves previously issued intervals untouched.
Finally, since calibration quality varies over time and with the
horizon, coverage, interval width, and interval scores are reported
overall, over rolling windows, and by horizon. The resulting objects
integrate with the \textbf{forecast} ecosystem
\citep{Hyndman2008-forecast, hyndman2026}.

We illustrate these design choices in an operational setting: the
calibration of short-term electricity price forecasts published by the
Australian Energy Market Operator for the five regions of the National
Electricity Market. The point forecasts are produced outside R and come
without prediction intervals; they are issued repeatedly at horizons
from 15 to 60 minutes, and their intervals are updated as new prices
become available. The package is available from CRAN, with the
development version at
\url{https://github.com/xqnwang/conformalForecast}; the analyses in this
paper use version 0.2.0.

The remainder of the paper is organized as follows.
Section~\ref{sec-method} reviews the online conformal prediction methods
implemented in the package. Section~\ref{sec-software} presents the
design and implementation of \textbf{conformalForecast}.
Section~\ref{sec-application} demonstrates the workflow in short-term
electricity price forecasting. Section~\ref{sec-conclusion} concludes.

\section{Time series conformal prediction}\label{sec-method}

\subsection{Sequential multistep forecasting
framework}\label{sec-framework}

Let \(\{y_t\}_{t=1}^T\) denote a univariate time series. At forecast
origin \(t\), a forecasting method produces point forecasts
\(\widehat{y}_{t+h\mid t}\) for horizons \(h=1,\ldots,H\), with error
\(e_{t+h\mid t}=y_{t+h}-\widehat{y}_{t+h\mid t}\) once the target is
observed. Repeating this procedure over successive origins
\citep{Hyndman2021-fpp3} gives the origin--horizon array in
Figure~\ref{fig-framework}. The conformal layer aligns forecasts with
their targets and does not require the internal specification of the
forecasting model.

\begin{figure}[h]

\centering{

\includegraphics[width=1\linewidth,height=\textheight,keepaspectratio]{figures/fig-framework.pdf}

}

\caption{\label{fig-framework}Sequential multistep forecasting and
conformal calibration.}

\end{figure}%

At origin \(t\), an \(h\)-step forecast issued at origin \(j\) can
contribute a calibration score only when \(j+h\leq t\). With initial
origin \(t_0\), the expanding history is \[
\mathcal{S}^{\mathrm{exp}}_{t,h}
=
\left(s_{j+h\mid j}\right)_{j=t_0}^{\,t-h},
\] and is empty when \(t-h<t_0\). Feedback arrives with an \(h\)-period
delay, so longer horizons have shorter histories under a common starting
origin. A rolling history retains the most recent \(m\) available
scores, whereas an expanding history retains all of them. Neither should
be confused with the data window used to estimate the point forecasting
model.

Symmetric calibration uses absolute error
\(s_{t+h\mid t}=|e_{t+h\mid t}|\). Asymmetric calibration instead uses
\(s^{\mathrm{l}}_{t+h\mid t}=-e_{t+h\mid t}\) and
\(s^{\mathrm{u}}_{t+h\mid t}=e_{t+h\mid t}\). For target miscoverage
\(\alpha\in(0,1)\), the relevant histories or recursively updated states
produce thresholds \(q^{\mathrm{l}}_{t,h}\) and \(q^{\mathrm{u}}_{t,h}\)
and the interval \[
\widehat{C}^{\alpha}_{t+h\mid t}
=
\left[
\widehat{y}_{t+h\mid t}-q^{\mathrm{l}}_{t,h},
\widehat{y}_{t+h\mid t}+q^{\mathrm{u}}_{t,h}
\right].
\] Symmetric calibration sets
\(q^{\mathrm{l}}_{t,h}=q^{\mathrm{u}}_{t,h}=q_{t,h}\); asymmetric
calibration allocates the total miscoverage across the two tails.

The methods below differ in how these horizon-specific score streams
determine the thresholds, but share this input--output structure with
the \textbf{conformalForecast} package in Section~\ref{sec-software}.

\subsection{Conformal calibration methods}\label{sec-cpmethods}

Coverage statements require a distinction between marginal validity and
long-run calibration. Classical conformal prediction gives finite-sample
marginal validity,
\(\Pr\{y_{t+h}\in\widehat C^\alpha_{t+h\mid t}\}\geq1-\alpha\), when the
calibration and test scores are exchangeable. For online methods, let
\(\operatorname{err}_{u\mid u-h}=\mathbf{1}\{y_u\notin\widehat C^\alpha_{u\mid u-h}\}\)
and let \(u_h\) be the first evaluated target time. Long-run validity
requires the time-average miscoverage to have limit superior at most
\(\alpha\), whereas exact long-run calibration requires \[
\lim_{T\to\infty}
\frac{1}{T-u_h+1}\sum_{u=u_h}^{T}\operatorname{err}_{u\mid u-h}
=\alpha.
\] Neither property implies conditional coverage at each origin,
coverage in every local window, or simultaneous coverage of the forecast
path.

Table \ref{tab:calibration-methods} summarizes the five calibration
schemes implemented in \textbf{conformalForecast}, which fall into three
families: empirical quantile calibration, feedback-based online
calibration, and dependence-aware multistep calibration. They differ in
the quantity that is adapted as new scores arrive and in the coverage
statement that is available, together with the conditions under which it
holds.

