\subsubsection{Empirical quantile
calibration}\label{empirical-quantile-calibration}

Split conformal prediction (SCP) calibrates each horizon with an
empirical score quantile \citep{papadopoulos2002, vovk2005, lei2018}.
For the origins \(\mathcal J_{t,h}\) retained at \((t,h)\), the
corrected construction is \[
q_{t,h}
=Q_{1-\alpha_*}\!\left(
\sum_{j\in\mathcal J_{t,h}}\widetilde w_{j,t,h}\,
\delta_{s_{j+h\mid j}}
+\widetilde w_{\infty,t,h}\,\delta_{+\infty}
\right),
\] where \(Q_\tau\) is the generalized inverse quantile, \(\delta_a\) is
a unit point mass, and \(\alpha_*=\alpha\) for absolute scores or
\(\alpha/2\) for each signed tail. SCP assigns mass
\(1/(|\mathcal J_{t,h}|+1)\) to every calibration score and to
\(+\infty\), which supplies the conformal rank correction. If the
forecaster is fitted on separate training data and the calibration and
test scores are exchangeable, this construction has finite-sample
marginal validity without requiring a correctly specified forecasting
model. Calibrating each signed tail at \(\alpha/2\) gives interval
coverage of at least \(1-\alpha\) by the union bound. Temporal
dependence, distributional change, and repeated refitting can violate
score exchangeability in rolling-origin applications. Interpolated
quantiles and effective-sample-size adjustments likewise do not
automatically inherit the exact guarantee.

Weighted split conformal prediction (WCP) uses the same construction
with prespecified, data-independent weights \citep{barber2023}. Each
calibration score receives \(w_j\in[0,1]\), the point mass at
\(+\infty\) receives weight one, and the weights are normalized. The
corrected quantile remains marginally valid when the calibration and
test scores are exchangeable. More generally, under the swap-based
framework of \citet{barber2023}, the coverage shortfall is bounded by a
weighted sum of total-variation distances between the joint sequence and
its calibration--test swaps. Recency weighting can reduce the influence
of less representative scores, but does not itself guarantee validity
under dependence or distributional change. In
\textbf{conformalForecast}, WCP is obtained from \texttt{scp()} with a
user-supplied weight function. The stated theoretical results require
the corresponding exchangeability or swap conditions, admissible fixed
weights, and the corrected generalized-inverse quantile; other
weighting, quantile, and effective-sample-size choices are
implementation options described in Section~\ref{sec-software-cp}.

\subsubsection{Feedback-based online
calibration}\label{feedback-based-online-calibration}

Adaptive conformal prediction (ACP) adjusts the miscoverage level using
observed score violations \citep{gibbs2021}. For horizon \(h\), let
\(v_{t,h}=\mathbf{1}\{s_{t\mid t-h}>q_{t-h,h}\}\) indicate whether the
newly observed score exceeds its issued threshold. Then \[
\alpha_{t,h}
=\alpha_{t-1,h}+\gamma\{\alpha_*-v_{t,h}\},
\] where \(\gamma>0\) controls responsiveness, and \(\alpha_*=\alpha\)
for symmetric calibration or \(\alpha/2\) for each tail. A violation
lowers \(\alpha_{t,h}\) and typically raises the next threshold. The
original one-step ACI recursion achieves distribution-free long-run
calibration when levels outside \([0,1]\) correspond to full or empty
prediction sets; applying that one-step recursion separately to the two
tails gives interval coverage of at least \(1-\alpha\). Following
\citet{wang2024}, \textbf{conformalForecast} instead applies the update
to each delayed horizon-specific stream. This operational multistep
extension does not automatically inherit the one-step result, and
excursions outside \([0,1]\) can produce unbounded or empty intervals.

Conformal PID control instead updates thresholds directly on the score
scale \citep{angelopoulos2023}. For each horizon \(h\), the proportional
tracking state and final threshold are \[
\begin{aligned}
q^P_{t,h}
&=q^P_{t-1,h}+\eta_{t,h}(v_{t,h}-\alpha_*),\\
q_{t,h}
&=q^P_{t,h}
+r_{t,h}\!\left(\sum_{u=b_h}^{t}(v_{u,h}-\alpha_*)\right)
+\widehat s_{t+h\mid t},
\end{aligned}
\] Here \(\eta_{t,h}\) is the tracking step size, \(r_{t,h}\) is a
saturating error integrator, \(b_h\) starts the controller feedback, and
\(\widehat s_{t+h\mid t}\) is an optional predictable score forecast.
These terms provide reactive tracking, cumulative correction, and
prospective adjustment, respectively. \textbf{conformalForecast}
maintains horizon-specific states; with asymmetric scores, the
signed-error forecast enters the two tail thresholds with opposite
signs. Under the score, predictable-threshold, and integrator-saturation
conditions of \citet{angelopoulos2023}, extended to delayed multistep
feedback by \citet{wang2024}, the violation frequency at each horizon
converges to \(\alpha_*\). Separate tail calibration therefore gives
long-run interval coverage of at least \(1-\alpha\). The finite-sample
bound contains a delay term that increases with \(h\) for a fixed
evaluation length.

\subsubsection{Dependence-aware multistep
calibration}\label{dependence-aware-multistep-calibration}

Autocorrelated multistep conformal prediction (AcMCP) replaces the
generic PID scorecaster with a structured forecast-error adjustment
\citep{wang2024}. A first-order approximation for a general
non-stationary autoregressive process gives optimal \(h\)-step errors an
approximate MA\((h-1)\) representation. AcMCP therefore combines an
MA\((h-1)\) model for the available \(h\)-step errors with a regression
linking errors from the same forecast origin; unavailable within-path
errors are supplied recursively. \textbf{conformalForecast} applies this
adjustment to signed scores with separate tail thresholds. Under the
same predictable-threshold and integrator conditions as the multistep
PID construction, AcMCP achieves exact long-run calibration at each
horizon. The adjustment targets interval efficiency rather than a
stronger coverage criterion, while longer horizons require more history
and incur a larger finite-sample delay term.
Section~\ref{sec-application} compares these methods using common
rolling-origin forecasts.

\section{The R package conformalForecast}\label{sec-software}

\subsection{Package architecture}\label{sec-software-design}

The \textbf{conformalForecast} package provides an R \citep{RCoreTeam}
implementation of the methods described in Section~\ref{sec-method}. It
is available on CRAN, with source code hosted on GitHub, and is
distributed under the GPL-3 license. The package uses the S3 class
system and a data structure compatible with \textbf{forecast}
\citep{Hyndman2008-forecast}. Its core object classes inherit from
\texttt{forecast}; \textbf{conformalForecast} defines class-specific
methods for \texttt{print()}, \texttt{summary()}, and
\texttt{accuracy()}, while stored forward forecasts can be visualized
through the inherited \texttt{autoplot()} method. Additional
dependencies include \textbf{ggdist} \citep{Kay2024-ggdist} for weighted
sample quantiles, \textbf{zoo} \citep{Zeileis2005-zoo} for rolling
summaries, and \textbf{rlang} \citep{Henry2026-rlang} together with
\textbf{stats} for supporting programming and statistical utilities.

Point forecasting and conformal calibration form separate stages. At
successive forecast origins, \texttt{cvforecast()} calls a user-supplied
forecasting function that returns a \texttt{forecast} object. The
function may fit a model in R or wrap forecasts generated externally, as
illustrated in Section~\ref{sec-application}. The conformal stage
operates on the resulting origin--horizon arrays of point forecasts and
realized errors rather than on the internal representation of the
forecasting model. Consequently, the same rolling-origin forecasts and
error history can be supplied to each conformal method, holding the
point forecast input fixed when methods are compared.

Two core S3 classes encode these stages. A \texttt{cvforecast} object
inherits from \texttt{forecast} and stores the rolling-origin forecasts
and realized errors by horizon, together with any intervals supplied by
the forecasting function if available. A \texttt{cpforecast} object is
also a subclass of \texttt{cvforecast}; it contains the calibrated
intervals and the method-specific information needed for sequential
updating, and carries an additional class identifying the selected
method. As shown in Figure~\ref{fig-workflow}, \texttt{conformal()}
dispatches on a \texttt{cvforecast} object and returns the corresponding
method-specific \texttt{cpforecast} object. Objects from either stage
can be evaluated using \texttt{coverage()}, \texttt{width()}, and
\texttt{accuracy()}, whereas \texttt{update()} operates on a
\texttt{cpforecast} object and returns an updated object that extends
the calibration sequence when new observations become available.

