\subsection{Rolling-origin forecasting}\label{sec-software-cv}

The conformal methods require rolling-origin forecast errors, which are
computed by \texttt{cvforecast()}:

\begin{Shaded}
\begin{Highlighting}[]
\FunctionTok{cvforecast}\NormalTok{(y, forecastfun, }\AttributeTok{h =} \DecValTok{1}\NormalTok{, }\AttributeTok{level =} \FunctionTok{c}\NormalTok{(}\DecValTok{80}\NormalTok{, }\DecValTok{95}\NormalTok{), }\AttributeTok{forward =} \ConstantTok{TRUE}\NormalTok{,}
           \AttributeTok{xreg =} \ConstantTok{NULL}\NormalTok{, }\AttributeTok{initial =} \DecValTok{1}\NormalTok{, }\AttributeTok{window =} \ConstantTok{NULL}\NormalTok{, ...)}
\end{Highlighting}
\end{Shaded}

It moves a forecast origin along \texttt{y} and, at each origin, applies
\texttt{forecastfun} and stores the point forecasts and the errors for
all horizons up to \texttt{h}. The forecasting function takes a
univariate time series as its first argument and accepts \texttt{h} and
\texttt{level}, together with \texttt{xreg} and \texttt{newxreg} when
exogenous predictors are used. It returns a \texttt{forecast} object
whose \texttt{mean}, \texttt{lower}, and \texttt{upper} components are
aligned with the requested horizons and coverage levels. When the
forecasting method does not provide prediction intervals, \texttt{lower}
and \texttt{upper} can instead contain missing values, as in
Section~\ref{sec-application}. Beyond this interface, the package places
no restriction on the forecasting method.

The location of the forecast origins is determined by \texttt{initial}
and \texttt{window}. When \texttt{window\ =\ NULL}, the first origin is
\texttt{initial}, and the estimation sample expands as new observations
become available. When a rolling window is specified, each origin uses
the most recent \texttt{window} observations, and the first origin is
\texttt{max(initial,\ window)}. If \texttt{forward\ =\ TRUE},
\texttt{cvforecast()} also generates forecasts from the final
observation in the series, storing these out-of-sample forecasts and any
associated intervals or fitted-model information in the \texttt{mean},
\texttt{lower}, \texttt{upper}, and \texttt{model} components. The
\texttt{level} argument supplies one or more nominal coverage levels,
which are stored in the resulting object and used in the subsequent
conformal calibration.

The result is a \texttt{cvforecast} object. Its \texttt{MEAN} and
\texttt{ERROR} components are multivariate time series indexed by target
time, with the \(h\)th column containing point forecasts and errors,
respectively, for horizon \(h\). The \texttt{LOWER} and \texttt{UPPER}
components preserve the interval bounds returned by
\texttt{forecastfun}; they are lists indexed by nominal coverage level,
each containing multivariate time series in the same layout. In the
\texttt{cpforecast} object described in Section~\ref{sec-software-cp},
the corresponding components instead contain the conformally calibrated
intervals. The forecast errors
\(e_{t+h\mid t}=y_{t+h}-\widehat y_{t+h\mid t}\) are transformed into
absolute or signed nonconformity scores during calibration, as defined
in Section~\ref{sec-framework}. The point forecasts in \texttt{MEAN}
locate the resulting intervals.

\subsection{Conformal calibration}\label{sec-software-cp}

The \texttt{conformal()} S3 generic provides a common interface to the
conformal methods in Section~\ref{sec-cpmethods}:

\begin{Shaded}
\begin{Highlighting}[]
\FunctionTok{conformal}\NormalTok{(object, }\AttributeTok{method =} \FunctionTok{c}\NormalTok{(}\StringTok{"scp"}\NormalTok{, }\StringTok{"acp"}\NormalTok{, }\StringTok{"pid"}\NormalTok{, }\StringTok{"acmcp"}\NormalTok{), ...)}
\end{Highlighting}
\end{Shaded}

For a \texttt{cvforecast} object, each value of \texttt{method} selects
the corresponding implementation function and passes \texttt{object} and
\texttt{...} unchanged, so
\texttt{conformal(fc,\ method\ =\ "scp",\ ...)} and
\texttt{scp(fc,\ ...)} are equivalent. The interface contains four such
functions: \texttt{scp()}, \texttt{acp()}, \texttt{pid()}, and
\texttt{acmcp()}. WCP is obtained by supplying a non-uniform weighting
rule through \texttt{weightfun} to \texttt{scp()}, whereas SCP uses
equal weights. Table~\ref{tbl-methods} summarizes the four
implementations and their additional arguments.

\begin{table}[H]

\caption{\label{tbl-methods}Implementations selected by the
\texttt{method} argument in \textbf{conformalForecast} and their
principal additional arguments. Arguments common to all implementations
are described in the text.}

\centering{

\begin{tabular}{llp{7.9cm}}
\toprule
\textbf{\texttt{method}} & \textbf{Function} & \textbf{Principal additional arguments}\\
\midrule
\texttt{"scp"} & \texttt{scp()} & \texttt{weightfun}, \texttt{kess}\\
\texttt{"acp"} & \texttt{acp()} & \texttt{gamma}\\
\texttt{"pid"} & \texttt{pid()} & \texttt{integrate}, \texttt{scorecast}, \texttt{scorecastfun}, \texttt{lr}, \texttt{Tg}, \texttt{delta}, \texttt{Csat}, \texttt{KI}\\
\texttt{"acmcp"} & \texttt{acmcp()} & \texttt{integrate}, \texttt{scorecast}, \texttt{lr}, \texttt{Tg}, \texttt{delta}, \texttt{Csat}, \texttt{KI}, \texttt{ma\_method}\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

}

\end{table}%

The target miscoverage, \texttt{alpha}, is taken by default from the
levels stored in the object. In \texttt{scp()} and \texttt{acp()},
\texttt{ncal} specifies the initial size of an expanding calibration
history or the length of a rolling history of past nonconformity scores.
In \texttt{pid()} and \texttt{acmcp()}, it defines the initial
calibration and scorecasting burn-in period. When
\texttt{rolling\ =\ TRUE}, it also determines the length of the trailing
histories used for scorecasting and learning-rate calculations.
Otherwise, these histories expand as observations become available.
Because \texttt{acmcp()} constructs scorecasts recursively across
horizons, longer horizons require additional history before all
scorecaster inputs are available. The \texttt{symmetric} argument
selects absolute error scores or separate signed scores in
\texttt{scp()}, \texttt{acp()}, and \texttt{pid()}, whereas
\texttt{acmcp()} supports only the latter. For \texttt{scp()} and
\texttt{acp()}, \texttt{quantiletype} selects one of the nine estimators
implemented by \texttt{ggdist::weighted\_quantile()}. The default, type
1, matches the generalized-inverse definition in
Section~\ref{sec-cpmethods}. The finite-sample result stated there for
SCP and WCP relies on this corrected quantile construction together with
the corresponding assumptions and weighting conditions. Other quantile
types are implementation options and do not automatically inherit that
result.

Function-specific arguments control weighting, online adaptation, and
nonconformity-score forecasting. When the calibration history contains
\(m\) nonconformity scores, \texttt{scp()} calls \texttt{weightfun} with
\(m+1\) and applies the returned vector of fixed, non-data-dependent
weights to the historical scores and the additional \(+\infty\) value.
This construction allows recent historical scores to receive greater
weight than older ones while retaining an explicit weight for the added
point mass. When weighted scores are used, setting
\texttt{kess\ =\ TRUE} bases the quantile calculation on Kish's
effective sample size rather than on the number of available values. As
noted in Section~\ref{sec-cpmethods}, this adjustment is an
implementation option and does not automatically inherit the exact
weighted conformal guarantee. The step size used by \texttt{acp()} to
update the miscoverage level over time is determined by \texttt{gamma}.

For \texttt{pid()}, \texttt{lr} supplies the initial scale for the
proportional quantile-tracking update. The learning rate used in the
recursion is subsequently scaled by the range of the available signed or
absolute errors. The arguments \texttt{integrate}, \texttt{KI},
\texttt{Csat}, \texttt{Tg}, and \texttt{delta} configure the integral
component. The derivative component is specified through
\texttt{scorecast} and \texttt{scorecastfun}. The latter accepts a
univariate time series and a forecast horizon and returns a
\texttt{forecast} object whose \texttt{mean} component supplies the
nonconformity-score forecasts. Most of these controller arguments are
also available in \texttt{acmcp()}, whose structured error-based
scorecaster is defined internally and therefore does not require
\texttt{scorecastfun}. The additional argument \texttt{ma\_method}
determines how the moving-average component is estimated.

All four implementation functions and calls through the
\texttt{conformal()} S3 generic return a \texttt{cpforecast} object with
the same \texttt{MEAN} and \texttt{ERROR} components as its
\texttt{cvforecast} input, while \texttt{LOWER} and \texttt{UPPER}
contain the calibrated intervals in the same by-horizon layout. By
preserving a common structure across conformal methods, the package
provides a uniform interface for inspecting, comparing, and processing
calibrated forecasts.

\subsection{Evaluating prediction intervals}\label{sec-software-eval}

The intervals are summarized by three functions, each of which accepts
either a \texttt{cvforecast} or a \texttt{cpforecast} object, so that a
conformal method can be compared with the intervals of the underlying
model on the same terms. The \texttt{accuracy()} call below shows
simplified usage and omits the optional \texttt{x}, \texttt{CV}, and
\texttt{period} arguments.

\begin{Shaded}
\begin{Highlighting}[]
\FunctionTok{coverage}\NormalTok{(object, ..., }\AttributeTok{level =} \DecValTok{95}\NormalTok{, }\AttributeTok{window =} \ConstantTok{NULL}\NormalTok{, }\AttributeTok{na.rm =} \ConstantTok{FALSE}\NormalTok{)}
\FunctionTok{width}\NormalTok{(object, ..., }\AttributeTok{level =} \DecValTok{95}\NormalTok{, }\AttributeTok{includemedian =} \ConstantTok{FALSE}\NormalTok{, }\AttributeTok{window =} \ConstantTok{NULL}\NormalTok{,}
      \AttributeTok{na.rm =} \ConstantTok{FALSE}\NormalTok{)}
\FunctionTok{accuracy}\NormalTok{(object, }\AttributeTok{measures =}\NormalTok{ interval\_measures, }\AttributeTok{byhorizon =} \ConstantTok{FALSE}\NormalTok{, ...)}
\end{Highlighting}
\end{Shaded}

The \texttt{coverage()} function returns empirical coverage for each
horizon as both a mean over the evaluation sample and a sequence of
observation-level indicators. The \texttt{width()} function returns the
interval widths together with their mean for each horizon and, when
\texttt{includemedian\ =\ TRUE}, their median. Supplying \texttt{window}
adds rolling mean coverage and rolling mean width, together with rolling
median width when requested. These rolling summaries are used in
Section~\ref{sec-application} to examine changes in local coverage and
interval width.

Accuracy measures come from \texttt{accuracy()}, which computes them
from a list supplied by the user. The package exports two such lists:
\texttt{point\_measures}, with ME, MAE, MSE, RMSE, MPE, MAPE, MASE and
RMSSE, and \texttt{interval\_measures}, with the Winkler score
\citep{Winkler1972-interval} and the mean scaled interval score
\citep{Makridakis2020-m4}. Users may instead provide a list of functions
that accept the relevant inputs supplied by \texttt{accuracy()}. With
\texttt{byhorizon\ =\ TRUE}, each measure is reported separately by
horizon, allowing changes in interval performance across forecast
horizons to be examined.

\subsection{Updating with new observations}\label{sec-software-update}

Rebuilding the pipeline every time an observation arrives gives the
right answer at an unnecessary cost: the model is refitted at origins
that were already fitted, and intervals that have already been reported
are computed again. Both are avoided by \texttt{update()}:

\begin{Shaded}
\begin{Highlighting}[]
\FunctionTok{update}\NormalTok{(object, new\_data, forecastfun, }\AttributeTok{new\_xreg =} \ConstantTok{NULL}\NormalTok{, ...)}
\end{Highlighting}
\end{Shaded}

It appends \texttt{new\_data}, computes forecasts only for the newly
available origins, and extends the calibration using the stored score
histories or recursive state. Because the forecasting function is not
stored in the object, each update call requires \texttt{forecastfun} to
be supplied again. It should be the same function used in the original
call to \texttt{cvforecast()}.

During an update, the conformal method and its calibration settings are
recovered from the fitted object and kept unchanged. Modifying
parameters such as \texttt{ncal}, \texttt{lr}, or \texttt{KI} therefore
requires a new calibration rather than an update. When exogenous
predictors were used in the original fit, \texttt{new\_xreg} is required
and contains one row per new observation. These rows extend the stored
predictor matrix over the periods immediately following it. When
\texttt{forward\ =\ TRUE}, that matrix can already contain future
predictor values, so \texttt{new\_xreg} continues from the end of the
stored matrix rather than necessarily aligning row by row with
\texttt{new\_data}. With the same forecasting function and calibration
settings, the updated object produces the same intervals as a complete
reconstruction of the pipeline on the extended series, while leaving
previously issued intervals unchanged. As a result, the computational
cost is driven mainly by the number of new observations rather than by
the length of the full series.

\section{Application}\label{sec-application}

\subsection{Data source and preprocessing}\label{sec-app-data}

We illustrate the package on wholesale electricity prices from the
Australian National Electricity Market (NEM), operated by the Australian
Energy Market Operator (AEMO) \citep{AEMO2026-mmsdm}. AEMO's P5MIN
process produces twelve five-minute regional price forecasts for the
next hour, with a new run every five minutes. At each selected
quarter-hour reference time, we aggregate forecasts from a single run
into four non-overlapping 15-minute forecasts and apply the same
aggregation to realized prices. The reference times identify the
forecast schedule rather than the exact publication times. The complete
data cover April 2026, with 2,880 selected reference times per region.
The main analysis uses 1--29 April, while 30 April is reserved for the
updating experiment in Section~\ref{sec-app-update}.

This application demonstrates how the package can calibrate forecasts
produced by an external system. The point forecasts are published by
AEMO rather than estimated within our analysis. The P5MIN data used here
contain no associated prediction intervals or predictive distribution.
The application therefore illustrates the model-independent interface
described in Section~\ref{sec-software-design}.

Figure~\ref{fig-nsw-prices} shows the New South Wales series for 1--29
April 2026. Prices range from \(-20\) to \(204\) AUD/MWh, with a median
of \(67.5\). Negative prices are genuine market outcomes rather than
data errors. Price variability changes considerably over time, with
relatively stable periods interrupted by sharp intraday movements. The
corresponding forecast errors exhibit time-varying dispersion and
horizon-dependent asymmetry. These features motivate interval methods
that can adapt their width over time and calibrate the two tails
separately.

