\abstract{The abstract serves both as a general introduction to the topic and as a brief, non-technical summary of the main results and their implications. Authors are advised to check the author instructions for the journal they are submitting to for word limits and if structural elements like subheadings, citations, or equations are permitted.}
\abstract{
\textbf{Purpose:} Relative changes in event rates are a common primary endpoint in confirmatory asthma trials, and reliable assumptions about baseline event rates and extra-Poisson variability are essential for valid sample size planning. Using an example application in asthma, we aim to synthesize placebo-group exacerbation data, evaluate calendar-time trends, and assess implications for trial design.

\textbf{Methods:} 
We conducted a systematic review of randomized controlled trials (RCTs) reporting recurrent asthma exacerbations and extracted outcomes exclusively from their control groups, which were classified according to placebo type or asthma severity. Control-group outcomes were synthesized using hierarchical negative binomial models that accommodated heterogeneous reporting formats and jointly modeled event rates and overdispersion. To account for differences among control groups defined by placebo type or asthma severity, we considered two alternative approaches: separate models by subgroups or adjustment via covariates. Meta-regression was used to adjust for calendar-time trends. Trial design implications are illustrated by considering the example case of the subsequent NAVIGATOR trial.


\textbf{Results:} Sixty-two trials met the inclusion criteria. The overall analysis indicated an estimated relative change in annualized exacerbation rates of $-1.8\%$ (95\% CrI: $-7.5\%, 4.3\%$) per year, corresponding to an estimated $16.5\%$ decrease per decade (95\% CrI: $-54.2\%, 52.2\%$). Substantial between-study heterogeneity in both event rates and overdispersion was observed. (Within-trial) overdispersion~($\varphi$) was estimated as $1.53$ (95\% CrI: $0.60, 2.83$). In an illustrative phase~III design scenario, sample size requirements were sensitive to both the target subgroup and the uncertainty in the predicted event rate and overdispersion parameter.

\textbf{Conclusion:} 
Event rates in clinical trials exhibit considerable heterogeneity and potential temporal variation, and overdispersion represents a critical design parameter. Comprehensive modelling of recurrent-event endpoints allows for evidence-based and robust sample size calculations and trial design.
}

\keywords{Recurrent events; Negative binomial distribution; Overdispersion; Bayesian hierarchical model; Meta-regression; Sample size planning; Asthma trials}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}\label{sec:intro}
Recurrent event endpoints are central to the evaluation of many chronic diseases. In conditions such as multiple sclerosis, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD), heart failure, and asthma, the frequencies of clinically relevant events---such as relapses, exacerbations or hospitalizations---serve as primary outcomes in confirmatory trials. Because such endpoints are typically analyzed using count-based regression models, reliable assumptions regarding baseline event rates and variability are essential for valid study design and interpretation. Asthma provides a particularly relevant example, as recurrent exacerbations constitute a defining feature of disease severity and long-term burden. Asthma continues to impose a substantial clinical and socio\-economic impact despite considerable advances in pharmacologic treatment. Severe exacerbations, defined by episodes of worsening respiratory symptoms requiring systemic corticosteroids, emergency department visits, or hospitalization, are associated with impaired lung function, reduced quality of life, increased health care utilization, and elevated mortality risk. Consequently, prevention of exacerbations represents a primary objective of long-term asthma management.

Reflecting their clinical relevance, exacerbation rates are frequently chosen as the primary endpoint in confirmatory phase~III trials in asthma. Because such endpoints involve recurrent events over a defined follow-up period, analyses are commonly performed using Poisson or negative binomial regression models \citep{Hilbe2011}. As emphasized by Keene et al.\ \cite{KeeneEtAl2007}, sample size requirements for recurrent-event trials depend not only on the assumed group-specific event rates but also on the extent of extra-Poisson variability (overdispersion). Failure to account for overdispersion may lead to underpowered studies or inflated type~I error rates when overdispersion is substantial.

Beyond statistical considerations at a single time point, accumulating evidence from other chronic diseases suggests that baseline event rates in RCTs may change over calendar time. Declining relapse rates have been documented in multiple sclerosis \cite{NicholasEtAl2011,SteinvorthEtAl2013,NicholasEtAl2019}, and similar temporal patterns have been reported in chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and heart failure \cite{AndreasEtAl2019,AndreasEtAl2022,KimmounEtAl2021}. Such trends may arise from improved background therapy, broader implementation of preventive measures, earlier diagnosis, changes in eligibility criteria, or evolving patient selection processes. If baseline exacerbation rates evolve over time but trial designs continue to rely on historical assumptions, sample size calculations may no longer be adequate. In asthma, however, systematic evidence regarding potential temporal trends in exacerbation rates remains limited. In particular, it remains unclear whether exacerbation rates have changed over the past decades across placebo-type or asthma-severity groups in RCTs, and how such differences might affect planning assumptions for future studies. Moreover, overdispersion in event counts has not been comprehensively synthesized across trials.

Temporal changes may hence affect both the comparability across trials, as well as the assumptions used in sample size calculation and power determination~\cite{FriedePohlmannSchmidli2019}. In the present work, we focus on asthma as a clinical application and conduct a systematic review of RCTs with recurrent exacerbation endpoints to investigate potential calendar-time trends in exacerbation rates across placebo-type and asthma-severity groups. We analyse the aggregated recurrent-event data using hierarchical models, considering both separate analyses by subgroups as well as covariate-based models that incorporate placebo type or asthma severity as study-level covariates in the event-rate model. We quantify between-study heterogeneity in event rates and overdispersion parameters and evaluate the implications of these findings for sample size determination in future confirmatory trials.

The remainder of this article is structured as follows.
Section~\ref{sec:MAandSR} introduces a motivating examples, hightlighting the challenges faced at the trial design stage, and describes the literature review conducted to form an empirical data base for informed design choices.
In Section~\ref{sec:methods}, we outline the background and methodological motivation for modeling recurrent event endpoints and addressing overdispersion in confirmatory trials. This section first introduces the proposed statistical framework, including the negative binomial model, likelihood construction accommodating heterogeneous reporting formats, modeling of calendar-time trends, and sample size planning based on event rates and dispersion parameters.
Section~\ref{sec:results} presents the application of the modeling framework to the collected dataset, including estimation of temporal trends and between-study heterogeneity, as well as implications for trial design. Finally, the paper concludes in Section~\ref{sec:discussion} with a discussion of methodological considerations, limitations, and directions for future methodological research.


\section{Motivating example and systematic review}\label{sec:MAandSR}
\subsection{The motivating example: design of an asthma trial}\label{sec:AsthmaExample}
To illustrate the implications of baseline event-rate and overdispersion
assumptions for trial design, we consider the phase~III \textsc{navigator}
trial, a randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled, two-arm study evaluating
\emph{tezepelumab} in patients with severe, uncontrolled asthma
\cite{menzies2021tezepelumab,Navigator}.
The trial enrolled adolescents and adults aged 12--80 years. Eligible patients
were required to have uncontrolled asthma, reduced baseline lung function, and
at least two asthma exacerbations during the preceding year despite treatment
with medium- or high-dose inhaled corticosteroids (ICS) plus at least one
additional controller medication, with or without oral corticosteroids.
Patients were randomized in a 1:1 ratio to receive \emph{tezepelumab} or placebo
every four weeks over a 52-week treatment period, while continuing their
prescribed background therapy. The primary endpoint was the annualized asthma
exacerbation rate. The total sample size was approximately 1060~patients
(530~patients per group), within a confirmatory phase~III framework
incorporating hierarchical testing, multiplicity control, and key secondary
endpoints.

The \textsc{navigator} design was based on an assumed annualized exacerbation
rate of $0.9$~events per year in the placebo group, a negative binomial shape
parameter of~2.4 (corresponding to $\varphi=1/2.4=0.417$ in the
parameterization introduced in Section~\ref{sec:NB} below), and a 10\% dropout
rate. Under these assumptions, the protocol reported at least 99\% power to
detect a 50\% rate reduction at a two-sided 1\% significance level. The
relatively stringent significance and power levels were intended to yield
\emph{statistically persuasive} evidence. In contrast, our calculations are not
meant to reproduce the original \textsc{navigator} setup; instead, we aim for a
conventional target power of~80\% and a two-sided significance level of~5\% to
illustrate sample size requirements under common planning assumptions. For the
treatment effect, we again assume a 50\% reduction in the event rate as
clinically relevant.

The \textsc{navigator} population is particular in that it included both adults and adolecents in the study population, and that it focused on cases of \emph{severe} asthma. To provide an empirical basis for alternative planning assumptions, we
therefore consider control-group data from a systematic review of historical asthma trials. The synthesis of control-group event rates and overdispersion, while accounting for placebo type, asthma severity, and calendar time, provides
a range of historically informed assumptions for illustrating their implications for sample size planning.

\subsection{The literature review}\label{sec:LitReview}
To provide an empirical basis for the study design setup,
we performed a systematic literature search to identify placebo-controlled asthma trials reporting exacerbation outcomes. We queried the \emph{PubMed} and \emph{Cochrane CENTRAL} data bases using the search terms \textquotedblleft{asthma* AND (double-blind OR triple-blind) AND (exacerba* OR hospitali*)}\textquotedblright, using the limits \textquotedblleft{Clinical Trial}\textquotedblright, \textquotedblleft{Randomized Controlled Trial}\textquotedblright and \textquotedblleft{adults}\textquotedblright. In order to also retrieve studies that have only recently been added to the database and may not be completely indexed, we repeated the search using only the limit \textquotedblleft{up to present}\textquotedblright.  The date of last search was January~2nd, 2019. The list of abstracts was independently reviewed by three authors (CR, SS, JH) using the following inclusion criteria: (i)~the study needs to deal with adult Asthma patients; (ii)~the study has a placebo control group; (iii)~the study design uses parallel group or a cross-over design; (iv)~the trial is double-blind and randomized; (v)~exacerbation rates are quoted or can be calculated from the data presented; (vi)~at least 100 patients (intention-to-treat population) are included into the study; (vii)~the study has a treatment duration of at least 12~weeks.

\subsection{Data extraction}\label{sec:DataExtraction}
We recorded information on patient demographics, trial eligibility criteria, exacerbation rates, and the corresponding uncertainties as well as overdispersion. Information on studies was assembled in a table and checked independently by at least two authors (CR; SS; SA; HW; JH). The reporting of rates was rather heterogeneous, partly because not all studies considered here treated exacerbation rates as a primary endpoint \cite{AaronEtAl2008}. The preferred source of evidence on rates are explicitly quoted rate estimates, and corresponding standard errors or confidence intervals. If a rate is given along with the number of exacerbation-free patients, this pair of figures provides some evidence on the overdispersion. If only one of the numbers is given, these may still be used to fit the joint model \cite{RoeverAndreasFriede2016}. Quoted rates or total exacerbation counts may be converted to one another, as can numbers and fractions of (non-)exacerbating patients. When exacerbation counts of differing severities are given, the numbers pertaining to ``moderate to severe'' exacerbation events were used. Our review focuses on data from the studies’ placebo groups. Only the concurrent use of inhaled corticosteroids (ICS),  long-acting beta agonist (LABA) or rescue medications was acceptable in order to be considered as placebo groups for purposes of our analysis. We distinguished between studies involving ``true placebo'', ``ICS-placebo'' and ``ICS-LABA-placebo'' groups where ``true'' placebo indicates that ICS and LABA were not allowed while in the ``ICS'' and ``ICS-LABA'' groups, the corresponding co-medications were allowed. In addition, we considered asthma severity levels. Classification was based on the mean baseline forced expiratory volume in 1~second (\fevone, in litres). {\fevonepp} is defined as the \fevone expressed as a percentage of its predicted value. As per the 2020 NAEPP guidelines \cite{cloutier20202020}, asthma severity can be categorized using the following criteria: Mild Asthma: $\mbox{\fevonepp} \geq 80 \%$; Moderate Asthma: $60\% \leq \mbox{\fevonepp} < 80\%$; Severe Asthma: $\mbox{\fevonepp} < 60\%$. Other extracted study characteristics were: number of participants, study duration, number of patient-years, fraction of smokers, mean pack-years,  mean Asthma Quality-of-Life Questionnaire score (AQLQ), mean Asthma Control Test score (ACT) and mean eosinophil count per microlitre. Study quality was assessed by two reviewers (SS; SA) using the Oxford Quality Scale score \cite{JadadEtAl1996}; discrepancies in this regard were resolved by a third reviewer (TF).

\section{Methods}\label{sec:methods}
We begin by formally introducing the negative binomial  model for recurrent event data (Section~\ref{sec:NB}), followed by the trial design considerations (the sample size formula and its dependence on event rates and overdispersion; Section~\ref{trialdesign}). Subsequently, we present the hierarchical synthesis framework for aggregated recurrent event outcomes, including the construction of a joint likelihood for heterogeneous reporting formats. Finally, we extend this framework to meta-regression models that incorporate calendar time and subgroup characteristics as study-level covariates (Section~\ref{aggregated}).


\subsection{The negative binomial model}\label{sec:NB}
Modeling of event count data here is based on the negative binomial distribution, which generalizes the Poisson model. The Poisson distribution is appropriate for modeling event counts that occur at a constant rate over time \cite{SpiegelhalterEtAl}. If a patient experiences events at an annualized rate~$\lambda$ and is observed for a duration of $d$~years, the total number of observed events follows a Poisson distribution with parameter $d\lambda$. Under this model,
\begin{equation}
  \expect[X] = d\lambda 
  \quad \text{and} \quad 
  \var(X) = d\lambda,
\end{equation}
so that the mean and variance coincide. This equidispersion property is a defining characteristic of the Poisson model. In many clinical applications, however, observed event counts exhibit greater variability than permitted by the Poisson assumption \cite{AaronEtAl2008,EMA2012,EMA2015b,AndreasEtAl2019}.
Such extra-Poisson variability may arise from heterogeneity between patients, for example due to differing baseline risks or disease activity levels. The negative binomial distribution extends the Poisson model by introducing an overdispersion parameter~$\varphi > 0$, allowing the variance to exceed the mean. A common motivation for the negative binomial model is as a Poisson mixture in which the individual rate parameter itself is varying. In this formulation, patients are assumed to have similar but not identical underlying event rates, and the resulting marginal distribution of event counts follows a negative binomial distribution.
Under the negative binomial model, the expected number of events 
remains unchanged, i.e.
\begin{equation}
  \expect[X] = d\lambda,
\end{equation}
while the variance becomes
\begin{equation}
  \var(X) = d\lambda \bigl(1 + \varphi d\lambda \bigr),
\end{equation}
which exceeds the mean whenever~$\varphi > 0$. Thus, the parameter $\varphi$ directly quantifies the degree of extra-Poisson variability. 
In the limiting case $\varphi \to 0$, the  negative binomial model again reduces to the Poisson model.

\subsection{Sample size calculation}\label{trialdesign}
Sample size determination for clinical trials involving overdispersed count outcomes requires specification of two key nuisance parameters: the control group event rate and the overdispersion parameter. Misspecification of these parameters may result in studies that are either under- or overpowered, both of which are undesirable for ethical and economical reasons \cite{kim2021handbook}.

Consider a two-arm trial with arms indexed by $i\in\{1,2\}$ and $n_i$ patients in arm~$i$. Let $\lambda_1$ denote the baseline (control-group) event rate, and let $\lambda_2$ denote the event rate under the experimental treatment. The treatment effect is expressed through the logarithmic rate ratio
\begin{equation}
  \delta_1 = \log(\lambda_1) - \log(\lambda_2) = \log(\lambda_1 / \lambda_2);
\end{equation}
at the trial design stage, a \emph{minimal clinically important difference (MCID)} is commonly considered here.

To derive the arm-level variance required for the subsequent sample size calculation, we adopt an individual-level negative binomial working model. Let $X_{ij}$ denote the number of recurrent events observed for patient $j=1,\dots,n_i$ in trial arm~$i$ over follow-up time $d_{ij}$. Conditional on $d_{ij}$, the event counts are assumed to follow a
negative binomial distribution with mean $\mu_{ij}=\lambda_i d_{ij}$ and overdispersion parameter~$\varphi$, implying
\begin{equation}
  \var(X_{ij})
  =
  \mu_{ij}\bigl(1+\varphi\mu_{ij}\bigr).
\end{equation}

We assume a treatment-to-control allocation ratio of $1:r$, with $n$ patients in the treatment group and $r n$~patients in the control group.
Assuming equal follow-up $d_{ij}=d$ for all patients, we test
\begin{equation}
  H_0:\;\delta_1=\log(\lambda_1)-\log(\lambda_2)=0,
\end{equation}
or equivalently,
\begin{equation}
  H_0:\;\lambda_1/\lambda_2=1.
\end{equation}
To achieve a two-sided type~I error rate~$\alpha$ and power~$1-\beta$
under the alternative $H_1:\;\delta_1\neq0$, the required sample size in the
treatment group is
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:NBSampleSize}
n \;=\;
\frac{(z_{1-\alpha/2}+z_{1-\beta})^2}{\delta_1^2}
\left\{
\frac{1}{r}
\left(
\frac{1}{d\lambda_1}+\varphi
\right)
+
\left(
\frac{1}{d\lambda_2}+\varphi
\right)
\right\},
\end{equation}
and the corresponding control-group sample size is $rn$ \cite{chow2017sample}.

\subsection{Synthesis of aggregated recurrent event outcomes}\label{aggregated}
\subsubsection{The hierarchical meta-regression model}
Count data commonly arise as endpoints in clinical trials, however, recurrent event outcomes are often reported using heterogeneous summary measures across studies, such as (i)~rates with standard errors, (ii)~total event counts, (iii)~proportions of event-free patients, or (iv)~combinations thereof. These reporting formats can be viewed as alternative summaries relating to a common underlying event-count process.
To accommodate such heterogeneous data, we employ a Bayesian hierarchical modeling framework based on a negative binomial (NB) data-generating mechanism.
A joint likelihood is constructed that links the different reporting formats to shared latent study-specific parameters, namely, the event rate~($\lambda$) and the overdispersion parameter~($\varphi$). This formulation allows all available information to contribute coherently to the estimation of rate and dispersion parameters.
Technical details of the likelihood construction for the different
reporting formats are provided in Appendix~\ref{AppendixA} and in
\cite{RoeverAndreasFriede2016,AndreasEtAl2019}.

For study~$i=1,\dots,I$ with follow-up duration~$d_i$, the placebo-group event counts are assumed to follow a negative binomial distribution with mean~$d_i\lambda_i$ and overdispersion parameter~$\varphi_i$.
Between-study variability in both the baseline event rates and the overdispersion parameters is modeled hierarchically within a Bayesian framework.
The study-specific rate parameters may depend on study-level covariates through a meta-regression structure while retaining residual heterogeneity across studies. Specifically, the general
event-rate model is written as
\begin{equation} \label{eqn:rateModel}
\log(\lambda_i) \sim
\normaldistn(\mathbf{x}_i^\top\boldsymbol{\beta}, \sigma_\lambda^2),
\end{equation}
where~$\mathbf{x}_i$ denotes a vector of study-level covariates and
$\boldsymbol{\beta}$ denotes the corresponding regression coefficients. For
example, $\mathbf{x}_i$ may include calendar time, baseline disease severity,
placebo type, treatment background, or other study-level design characteristics.
The study-specific overdispersion parameters were modeled using a random effects model as
\begin{equation} \label{eqn:OverdispModel}
\log(\varphi_i) \sim
\normaldistn\bigl(\mu_\varphi,\,\sigma_\varphi^2\bigr),
\end{equation}
and with that allowing for between-study heterogeneity in the extra-Poisson variability.


\subsubsection{Prior specification}
For the analysis of the assembled asthma RCT data, placebo type and asthma severity were considered as study-level grouping variables, while calendar time was included as a continuous covariate. Priors for intercept and slope were specified as
\begin{equation}
  \beta_0 \sim \unifdistn\bigl(\log(10^{-3}), \, \log(10^3)\bigr),
  \qquad
  \beta_1 \sim \normaldistn\bigl(0, \, 10^2\bigr),
\end{equation}
essentially mimicking an uninformative (uniform) prior.
Priors for the random effects' parameters were set to 
\begin{equation}
  \sigma_\lambda \sim \halfnormaldistn(1.0),
  \quad
  \mu_\varphi \sim \unifdistn\bigl(\log(10^{-4}), \log(10^4)\bigr),
  \quad
  \sigma_\varphi \sim \halfnormaldistn(1.0).
\end{equation}
For covariate-based models with group-specific intercepts and covariate effects,
the same priors were assigned independently to each~$\beta_{0,g}$ and
$\beta_{1,g}$.
The choice of prior distributions was motivated by previous work in
hierarchical modeling of recurrent event data~\cite{RoeverAndreasFriede2016}.
The models were fitted using Markov chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) methods implemented
in JAGS via the~\texttt{rjags} package in R~\cite{R:rjags}.
Posterior inference is summarized using posterior medians and central 95\% credible intervals (CrIs).


\subsubsection{Additional analyses}
As an additional exploratory analysis, we examined associations among
study-level characteristics, including disease severity and other clinical or
design covariates, using mixed-effects meta-regression models. This analysis was
conducted to assess potential correlations between study characteristics and to
identify possible confounding structures that could inform model specification.
Stratified meta-regression analyses were also performed to explore
subgroup-specific patterns across different study-level classifications. These
auxiliary analyses were conducted using the~\texttt{metafor} R
package~\cite{Viechtbauer2010}.

\subsubsection{Posterior predictive assurance}
The sample size formula~(\ref{eqn:NBSampleSize}) is based on \emph{fixed}
values of the event rates and overdispersion parameter. To account for
uncertainty in these planning parameters, we consider the Bayesian
\emph{assurance} framework~
\cite{stallard1998sample,mutze2018sample,grieve2022hybrid}.
Let $\pi(n;\delta_1,\boldsymbol{\theta},\mathbf{x})$ denote the frequentist
power of a planned trial with $n$ patients in the treatment group and $rn$
patients in the control group, corresponding to a treatment-to-control
allocation ratio of $1:r$. The power is conditional on the prespecified target
log rate ratio~$\delta_1$, the uncertain planning
parameters~$\boldsymbol{\theta}$, and the prespecified trial
characteristics~$\mathbf{x}$. Given a planning distribution
$p(\boldsymbol{\theta}\mid\mathbf{x})$, assurance is defined as

\begin{equation}
\assurance(n,\delta_1,\mathbf{x})
=
\expect_{\boldsymbol{\theta}\mid\mathbf{x}}
\bigl[
\pi(n;\delta_1,\boldsymbol{\theta},\mathbf{x})
\bigr]
=
\int
\pi(n;\delta_1,\boldsymbol{\theta},\mathbf{x})
\, p(\boldsymbol{\theta}\mid\mathbf{x})
\, \differential\boldsymbol{\theta}\mbox{,}
\end{equation}
that is, the expected power, marginalized over uncertainty in the planning
parameters.

In our application, the uncertain planning parameters are
$\boldsymbol{\theta}=(\lambda_1,\varphi)$, comprising the control-group event
rate~$\lambda_1$ and overdispersion parameter~$\varphi$ in a future trial,
while~$\mathbf{x}$ contains the relevant study-level characteristics. Their
posterior predictive distributions are induced by the hierarchical models in
Equations~(\ref{eqn:rateModel}) and~(\ref{eqn:OverdispModel}). Prediction of
the event rate accounts for the regression parameters and residual
between-study heterogeneity~$\sigma_\lambda$, whereas prediction of the
overdispersion parameter accounts for both the central log-overdispersion
level~$\mu_\varphi$ and between-study heterogeneity~$\sigma_\varphi$.
Consequently, the planning distribution incorporates both posterior
uncertainty in the model parameters and predictive study-level variation in
the event rate and overdispersion parameter.

For a prespecified target log rate ratio
$\delta_1=\log(\lambda_1/\lambda_2)$, the treatment-group event rate is
\[
\lambda_2=\lambda_1\exp(-\delta_1).
\]
Using the notation introduced in Section~\ref{trialdesign}, the large-sample
variance of the estimated log rate ratio~$\hat{\delta}_1$ can be written as
\begin{equation}
\textstyle
\var\bigl(
\hat{\delta}_1
\mid\delta_1,\boldsymbol{\theta},\mathbf{x}
\bigr)
=
\frac{V(\delta_1,\boldsymbol{\theta},\mathbf{x})}{n},
\quad
\mbox{where}
\quad
V(\delta_1,\boldsymbol{\theta},\mathbf{x})
=
\frac{1}{r}
\left(
\frac{1}{d\lambda_1}+\varphi
\right)
+
\left(
\frac{1}{d\lambda_2}+\varphi
\right)
\end{equation}
\cite{friede2010ablinded,friede2010bblinded}.
Defining
\begin{equation}
\textstyle
\eta(n;\delta_1,\boldsymbol{\theta},\mathbf{x})
=
\sqrt{
\frac{n\delta_1^2}
     {V(\delta_1,\boldsymbol{\theta},\mathbf{x})}
},
\end{equation}
the conditional power of the two-sided Wald test is
\begin{equation}
\pi(n;\delta_1,\boldsymbol{\theta},\mathbf{x})
=
\Phi\left(
\eta(n;\delta_1,\boldsymbol{\theta},\mathbf{x})
-z_{1-\alpha/2}
\right)
+
\Phi\left(
-\eta(n;\delta_1,\boldsymbol{\theta},\mathbf{x})
-z_{1-\alpha/2}
\right),
\end{equation}
where $\Phi(\cdot)$ denotes the standard normal cumulative distribution
function. For given historical data~$\histdata$, the posterior predictive
assurance is therefore
\begin{equation}
\assurance(n,\delta_1,\mathbf{x};\histdata)
=
\int
\pi(n;\delta_1,\boldsymbol{\theta},\mathbf{x})
\,p(\boldsymbol{\theta}\mid\mathbf{x},\histdata)
\,\differential\boldsymbol{\theta}.
\end{equation}
For a target assurance level~$\gamma$, the required treatment-group sample
size then is
\begin{equation}
n_\gamma(\delta_1,\mathbf{x};\histdata)
=
\min\left\{
n\in\mathbb{N}:\,
\assurance(n,\delta_1,\mathbf{x};\histdata)\geq\gamma
\right\}\mbox{,}
\end{equation}
and the corresponding total sample size is
\[
N_\gamma=(1+r)n_\gamma\mbox{.}
\]
The posterior predictive expectations were approximated using draws generated
from the MCMC output of the hierarchical model.
\section{Results}\label{sec:results}
\subsection{Studies identified}\label{sec:Studies identified}
According to the predefined eligibility criteria, a total of 2,725~records were identified, including 514 from PubMed, 489 from Embase, and 1,722 from Cochrane CENTRAL. After removal of 1,020 duplicate records, 1,705~records remained for title and abstract screening. Eventually, 538~reports underwent full-text assessment. Reports were excluded at the full-text stage according to the inclusion criteria, including insufficient sample size or study duration, ineligible populations or study designs, absence of relevant exacerbation outcomes or placebo groups, and inappropriate background therapy
(see Sections~\ref{sec:LitReview} and~\ref{sec:DataExtraction}).

Ultimately, 62 studies were included in the meta-analysis; some publications contributed more than one eligible study. The complete search and screening process, including detailed reasons for exclusion, is summarized in the PRISMA flow diagram in Appendix~\ref{sec:AppendixB} (Figure~\ref{fig:PrismaFlowChart}).


