A total of 62~studies were included in the analysis; their study characteristics are summarized in Table~\ref{table:baseline}. The median sample size was 176~participants (range 27--3568), and the median study duration was 0.54~years (range 0.07--3~years). Across all studies, the median proportion of male participants was 38.2\% (range 25.9\%--61.7\%), and the median mean age was 45.7~years (range 24--55~years).
The median baseline forced expiratory volume in 1 second (\fevone), expressed as percentage of the predicted value (\fevonepp), was 68.0\% (range 52.7\%--96.4\%). The median {\fevone} was 2.13~L (range 1.56--3.39~L). The median proportion of smokers was 4.0\% (range 0--38\%). The median Asthma Quality of Life Questionnaire (AQLQ) score was 4.22 (range 3.6--5.5). The median eosinophil count was 337.5~$\mu$L$^{-1}$ (range 0.25--688).
Among the included studies, 27 used true placebos, 16 used ICS-placebo, and 19 used ICS-LABA-placebo. Sample size and study duration varied across subgroups, with ICS-LABA-placebo studies generally having larger sample sizes (median 234~participants) and longer duration (median 0.92~years). Baseline demographic and lung function characteristics were broadly comparable across subgroups. Detailed study characteristics according to placebo type and
asthma severity are provided in Table~\ref{table:baseline_subgroups} in Appendix~\ref{AppendixE}. 

\subsection{Trends in exacerbation rates}\label{sec:exacerbation-trends}
To assess calendar-time trends, publication year (centered at the year~2000) was included as a study-level covariate in the event-rate model. The overall model included all 62~studies and estimated a common intercept and time trend. Potential differences according to placebo type and asthma severity were examined using two complementary strategies. In the subgroup-specific analyses, the hierarchical model was fitted independently to each data subset, whereas the covariate-based analyses included all studies jointly using group indicators and their interactions with publication year. The latter approach allowed group-specific intercepts and time trends while sharing the heterogeneity and overdispersion parameters across groups. 

Data on exacerbations were reported in heterogeneous formats.
Nine studies provided rate estimates with corresponding standard errors, 17 studies reported total exacerbation counts together with the number of exacerbation-free patients, 13 studies reported only total counts, and 23 studies provided only the proportion of non-exacerbating patients.

In the overall analysis of all 62 studies, the estimated association between publication year and exacerbation rate corresponded to a relative change of $-1.8$\% per year (95\% CrI: $-7.5$\%, 4.3\%), equivalent to an estimated 16.5\% decrease per decade (95\% CrI: $-54.2$\%, 52.2\%). The overall trend is shown in Figure~\ref{fig:AsthmaTrendPlot}(a). The placebo-type analyses are shown in Figure~\ref{fig:AsthmaTrendPlot}(b) and~\ref{fig:AsthmaTrendPlot}(c) for the subgroup-specific and covariate-based models, respectively, and the corresponding analyses by baseline severity are shown in Figure~\ref{fig:AsthmaTrendPlot}(d) and~\ref{fig:AsthmaTrendPlot}(e).
Detailed parameter estimates are provided in Table~\ref{tab:regression-ana-all}.

When placebo type was considered, temporal trends varied in direction but remained imprecisely estimated. In the subgroup-specific analyses, studies using true placebos showed an estimated annual relative change of 3.2\% (95\% CrI: $-7.9$\%, 15.3\%), whereas ICS-placebo studies showed an annual relative change of $-8.1$\% (95\% CrI: $-17.5$\%, 3.3\%) and ICS--LABA-placebo studies an annual relative change of $-2.6$\% (95\% CrI: $-10.2$\%, 5.4\%). The corresponding covariate-based analyses gave broadly similar conclusions: the annual relative changes were estimated as 
3.3\% (95\% CrI: $-5.9$\%, 13.3\%) for true-placebo studies, $-7.2$\% (95\% CrI: $-16.8$\%, 3.6\%) for ICS-placebo studies, and $-3.0$\% (95\% CrI: $-13.2$\%, 8.2\%) for ICS--LABA-placebo studies.

Analyses according to asthma severity also showed imprecise and heterogeneous temporal patterns. In the subgroup-specific analyses, the annual relative changes were estimated as $-2.7$\% (95\% CrI: $-15.3$\%, 12.1\%) for mild asthma, $-4.1$\% (95\% CrI: $-11.6$\%, 3.8\%) for moderate asthma, and $-1.6$\% (95\% CrI: $-13.3$\%, 7.8\%) for severe asthma. In the covariate-based analyses, the corresponding estimates were $-2.7$\% (95\% CrI: $-12.8$\%, 8.4\%), $-4.2$\% (95\% CrI: $-11.7$\%, 4.0\%), and $-8.3$\% (95\% CrI: $-25.9$\%, 9.5\%), respectively.

Neither the overall analysis nor the subgroup analyses provided convincing evidence of a calendar-time trend in exacerbation rates, as the credible intervals included a zero slope. The subgroup estimates were generally more uncertain because they were based on fewer studies or involved additional parameters. Apparent differences across placebo-type or severity groups and between modelling strategies should therefore not be over-interpreted.

Although neither the overall nor the subgroup analyses provided clear evidence
of a calendar-time trend, the estimated overdispersion parameters suggested
extra-Poisson variability in patient-level event counts within studies. In the
overall analysis, the estimated central log-overdispersion parameter was
$\mu_\varphi=0.422$ (95\% CrI: $-0.503$, 1.041). In the
subgroup-specific analyses, the corresponding estimates ranged from 0.232 to
0.854 across placebo-type subgroups and from $-3.650$ to 0.507 across
asthma-severity subgroups. The particularly low estimate for the severe-asthma
subgroup, $\mu_\varphi=-3.650$ (95\% CrI: $-8.933$, 0.999), was accompanied
by substantial uncertainty and should therefore be interpreted cautiously. In
the covariate-based analyses, the estimated common central
log-overdispersion parameters were $\mu_\varphi=0.498$ for the placebo-type
model and $\mu_\varphi=0.465$ for the asthma-severity model.

Beyond the within-study extra-Poisson variability captured by
$\varphi_i$, substantial between-study heterogeneity was observed in both the
underlying negative binomial event rates and the extent of overdispersion. In
the overall analysis, the estimated between-study standard deviations on the
log scale were $\sigma_{\lambda}=1.419$ (95\% CrI: 1.136, 1.784) for the
event rates and $\sigma_{\varphi}=1.226$ (95\% CrI: 0.722, 2.058) for the
overdispersion parameters. In the subgroup-specific analyses,
$\sigma_{\lambda}$ ranged from 0.979 to 1.628 across placebo-type subgroups
and from 0.726 to 1.752 across asthma-severity subgroups, while
$\sigma_{\varphi}$ ranged from 0.804 to 1.281 and from 0.686 to 1.289,
respectively. In the covariate-based analyses, the corresponding common
heterogeneity estimates were $\sigma_{\lambda}=1.391$ (95\% CrI: 1.089,
1.777) and $\sigma_{\varphi}=1.214$ (95\% CrI: 0.700, 2.048) for the
placebo-type model, and $\sigma_{\lambda}=1.385$ (95\% CrI: 1.112, 1.749)
and $\sigma_{\varphi}=1.197$ (95\% CrI: 0.727, 1.969) for the
asthma-severity model.

These parameters describe conceptually distinct sources of variability. The
negative binomial overdispersion parameter~$\varphi_i$ captures extra-Poisson
variability in event counts within study~$i$, whereas
$\sigma_{\lambda}$ captures between-study heterogeneity in exacerbation rates
and $\sigma_{\varphi}$ captures between-study heterogeneity in the extent of
overdispersion. The hierarchical negative binomial framework accommodates
these sources of variability simultaneously when synthesizing exacerbation
outcomes reported in heterogeneous formats.

