\subsection{Trends in patient characteristics}\label{sec:patient-characteristic-trends}
 \begin{figure*}[htbp]
	\centering
	\subfloat[age]{\includegraphics[width=.45\linewidth]{figures/Meta_Regression/Meta-regression_age_.pdf}}\hspace{5pt}
	\subfloat[AQLQ score]{\includegraphics[width=.45\linewidth]{figures/Meta_Regression/Meta-regression_AQLQ_.pdf}}\\
	\subfloat[{\fevone} (L)]{\includegraphics[width=.45\linewidth]{figures/Meta_Regression/Meta-regression_FEV1L_.pdf}}\hspace{5pt}
	\subfloat[{\fevonepp} (\%)]{\includegraphics[width=.45\linewidth]{figures/Meta_Regression/Meta-regression_FEV1PP_.pdf}}
	\caption{Time trends in (a) mean age (b) mean AQLQ score (c) mean {\fevone} (L) (d) mean {\fevonepp} (\%) in a mixed-effects meta-regression model with pulication year. Point sizes are proportional to the respective study sizes. Straight lines show predictions 
    as a function of publication year,
    dotted lines the corresponding 95\% CIs.}
    \label{fig:mixed-effect}
\end{figure*}

In order to assess potential confounding by baseline characteristics, we fitted several univariate mixed-effects meta-regression models as illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:mixed-effect}; the corresponding coefficient estimates are provided in Table~\ref{table:mixed-effect} in Appendix~\ref{AppendixC}. In these models, (a)~mean age, (b)~mean AQLQ~score, (c)~mean {\fevone}, and (d)~mean {\fevonepp} were regressed on publication year (centered at the year~2000). The analyses included (a)~55~studies, (b)~26~studies, (c)~38~studies, and (d)~54~studies, respectively. Estimated effects of publication year varied across outcomes. A positive time trend was observed for mean patient age at baseline ($\hat{\beta}_1=0.610$, 95\% CI: 0.108, 1.113), corresponding to an increase of approximately 6.1 years per decade. By contrast, negative trends were estimated for mean~{\fevone} ($\hat{\beta}_1=-0.048$, 95\% CI: $-0.081$, $-0.015$) and mean~{\fevonepp} ($\hat{\beta}_1=-0.611$, 95\% CI: $-1.173$, $-0.049$).

For lung function measures, the baseline average {\fevone} decreased by approximately 0.48 litres per decade, while {\fevonepp} decreased by approximately 6.1 percentage points per decade. These findings indicate that, over time, studies have tended to include participants with lower baseline lung function. Importantly, these trends may reflect evolving study populations rather than true population-level deterioration in lung function. The observed
